      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Bloodless for Five Years.</head>
        <p>Bloodless for Five Years.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Elizabeth Adcock, of 26, Cemetery Avenue, Ecclesall Road, Sheffield, states:— "When my daughter Mary was about fifteen she became weak and depressed, seldom knew what it was to get refreshing sleep at nights, and. ate so little that I became alarmed.</p>
        <p>"I gave her tonics and blood-mixtures, but these did not help her. A doctor said the trouble was Ancemia. The girl's face was as white as a sheet, and she did not appear to have an ounce of blood in her body. "On the advice of a friend I got a supply of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Mary. After she had taken a few boxes, the colour came back to her cheeks. Then her appetite became keen, and she had no discomfort after meals. Before long Dr. Williams' Pink Pills supplied the New Blood she needed, and now she is in splendid health."</p>
        <p>For further information write to the address below for booklet, "Diseases of the Blood." Because Dr. Williams' Pink Pills create New Strong Blood they have achieved thousands of cures in cases of Anwinia, Indigestion, General Weakness, Rheumatism, St. Vitus' Dance, Neurasthenia, and other Disorders of the Blood and Nervous System. Obtainable of dealers, or direct price 2s. 9d. one box, or 13s. 9d. for six boxes, post free, from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London. When dealing at shops, for your health's sake spurn all shopmen's substitutes and always insist upon being supplied with</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills</head>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills</p>
        <p>THE PILLS THAT STRENGTHEN.</p>
        <p>unshakable conviction, " Sueey, Awm sewer tha limps." "An' wat bi that ? " demanded Susey defiantly. "Is a woman onny war for limping ? "</p>
        <p>BRIDE IN PAWN.</p>
        <p>Dean Pigou, who was formerly vicar of Halifax, tells some amusing anecdotes respecting one Sager, who was verger there at the time ; but one of these, lately reproduced in the " Daily News," is not correctly narrated. I knew old Sager personally, and once sat by his side as he tolled the bell for a service which several f the local clergy went to participate. We commanded a view of the principal door, and as the parsons entered the rid man kept up a running com- ment on individuals, showing how thoroughly he had " sized 'em all up "--for he was a keen judge of character and an unsp :ring critic. But to my tale. An elderly pair had just been married at the Parish Church, and the brid, groom, a notoriously mean and stingy fellow, sought to make •a special bargain as to the fee. On heing informed of the total due, he tendered ex .ctly half the amount, and declared he would not pay a penny more. Wasting no words on the miserly bridegroom, the verger invited the lady to accompany him to the adjoining vestry, there to it snect a curious old register—a privilege which was denied the man. Politely stepping aside, he allowed the unsus: feting lady to pass within, and then, turning the key on her and pocketing it, he went back in triumph to the man and said, " There sl:e is, and there she bides till I get the proper fee !'' The bride was " popped," and the discomfited groom reluctantly handed over the difference, which, of course, meant immediate redemption. A RE-NVIVAL.</p>
        <p>Under last week's " Notes and Notions " we had " Woman in Epigram," and the first of the half-dozen examples (from Rabelais) ran : "My wife is dead. God help those who remain. I must think of finding another wife" The late Mr. Robert Hogan, a very much-married lilsex man, seems often to have been in that case, fr, recording to quotation in the latest of " Antiquarian Chapters already cited, he " was the husband of sezen wives successively." I know one man who was married for the fourth time, and we all have acquaintances who have had three wives. Years ago, I heard of one who on the death of his fourth was evidently ! inconsolable; in fact, as the body was being com- mitted to the ground he went ell in a dead faint. Anxious mourners hastened to his aid, upon which a cynic =I bystander exclaimed "Let him alone, he'll soon re-lure!" It is t Ad of another bereaved husband of the extremely emritiotal and sentimental kind, that he had inscribed on the tombstone of his first vvive's grave "My light has gone cut." When he re married an intimate friend called his attention to this, and asked how he proposed to escape from "the dilemma." It was no dilemma to him, however.; he simply caused to be added the words, "But I've struck another match I " CO-OPERATION OF SORTS.</p>
        <p>From her Warrington lecture on "The History of Co-operation," Mrs. Philip SaOwden went almost direct to a district where the movement is so strong that the three local societies boast of a membership .practically equalling the total number of dwelling houses in the entire 'coati ty.They have all been in es isttnce stme th:ee score years. One of them, managed by the present Mayor of the Borough (who began as a little boy at the bottom of the ladder, and has fr come year.s been at the top) celebrated its jubilee by a mill project calculated to provide employment for the ircreasing population ; while another, whose ex-president is also an ex-Nfayor, presented to the town its library, and built hand- some central premises in which to house it. It would be p s-ible to make an imposing picture of it all, for these societies had done much more that is cdmirable and praiseworthy. While Mrs. Snowden could not but commend their educational enterprise and public spirit, she would strongly thprecwe the policy of cut throat competition which has led them to set up shops close by and in direct opposition to each other. A number of them have just arranged,privately but none the less certainly, to boycott Port Sunlight and all its works; but one would not expect them to serve each other after that fashion. The fact is that, while the movement has its idealists and its self-sacrificing champions, too many of those as3cciated with it are mere selfish divi-hunters. STRANGE DRINKS."</p>
        <p>The Widnes t` ether drops " case led to a conver- sation cn my part with an experienced chemist, and he told me of people, mostly women, who do not hesitate to drink methylated spirits ; while not a few of a pre- sumably better class are unfortunately slaves to the drug habit. I cannot now repeat all be revealed to me, but I found it very bad and discouraging.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>ON MARRIAGE.</p>
        <p>By a remarkable coir cidence, along with the account of the Mayoress's wedding, last Saturday's "Examiner" contained an interesting article en "Marriage curiosi- ties" and a couple of paragraphs i "Women's Chat' were devoted to a kindred topc ; indeed, 1 think that ci the half-dozen printed, three may be said to hive arisen cut of consideration of the subject generally. I found " A M.'s" contribution peculiarly ruggestive, sad I may add one or two notes thereon. A VERY SOLOMON.</p>
        <p>The writer took occasion to refer to the ancient and persistent error that "A man was not liable for a woman's debts provided he married her in no clothing save a smock or shift." I was somehow reminded of a County Court incident which occuri ed in my presence more than a quarter of a century ago. An unscrupu- lous couple who had just " perpetrated matrimony" had conceived a plan whereby they bad thought to make a too confiding creditor bear the expense of a substantial part of the wedding festivities. The woman ordered a big barrel of beer for the jollifica- tion, saying nothing of the approaching nuptials, and tha debt was booked against her. The brewer's agent failed to obtain payment, and in due comae proceedings were taken for recovery of the amount, but, the original debtor being by that time martied, the husband was summoned. Then the play, as apparently pm-arranged, began. The defendant pleaded with an injured air that the debt was one contracted by his vile during her maidenhood, and confidently submitted that there could be no liability on his part. The Judge at once seemed to recognise the force of the argument, and told the man he must stand down. With an expansive smile of satisfaction, he left the bsx, and was about to hasten from the Court when His Honour called upon the wife to stand forward. In reply to the Judge's que-tions, the woman admitted that they and their guess had con- sumed the beer. She further conceded that the creditor ought not to be deprived of his money, but urged that her husband obdurately refuted to be party to it, so that she was helples4. Why, yes, it was true that she did occasionally do a little washing and cleaning for neighbours, and so earned a trifle on her own account How much? Well, really it was a mere nothing. " Will it average eislteenpelee a week?" asked the Judge. She supposed it might be about that not more. "Just so," pursued his Honour; "then I will allow the wife to be added as defendant, and I will make an order for Ca. a month And it was co—to the ill-concealed chagrin and disgust of the two conspirators, and the manifest pleasure of the Plaintiff. BEFORE—AND AFTER. The "social side-lights from parish registers "to which I now allude included a story of an invalid bride who had to be carried to church, being quite unable to walk. This brought to my mind one by John Hartley (Yorkshire dialect writer) whose personal friendship I enjoyed as a young man, and who was living in Cheshire when last I heard of him. He tells of a rather simple fellow whom a determined woman ' almost past hope suddenly succeeded in " hooking." The acquaintance was comparatively slight; the couple had never before walked out together ; and on the -way to church for the wedding, the otherwise happy enough bridegroom became somewhat uneasy owing to a distinct halt in the lady's gait. " Why, Susey," he whispered dubiously, "(biz ta—nay, higow, tha duz ; tha limps ! " " Not An'," was the confident assur- ances." Aw limp nootin." Retiring after the ceremony, the husband said to the wife with the emphasis of</p>
        <p>Colds are not a __ Luxury</p>
        <p>OR A NECEgSITV. ColdS or Catarrh make a man rldictdons—they make him an offensive nuisance and they make him dangerously sick. They are pretty sure to bring on consumption, pneumonia or at least a throat affection. You can not afford either, but you can afford the cure. It is Dr. Agnew's Catarrhal Powder. It relieves a cold or catarrh, or, cures a headache in 10 minutes. Don't hawk and spit and disgust your friends, but cure yourself by the use of this remedy. Dr. Agnewto lifsert Cure relieves instantiy heart end stomach. Sold by H. M. Ashton 8t Harold Smith, Chemists Warrington, &amp; Boots Cash Chemiets.</p>
