      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>"Such a weary time."</head>
        <p>"Such a weary time."</p>
        <p>Miss Agnes Longhorn, of 3, Swarldrick Street, Preston, states "About two years ago I had such weary time' from Anemia. I was languid and lost all inclination for food ; fearful head- aches attacked me, also gnawing aches across the shoulders. My temples throbbed and I rarely got a good night's rest. " I took much medicine and so-called tonics, but my body was cold, my face went white, and my lips became parched. For months and months the weakness and want of energy nearly drove me to despair. " When a friend advised me to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, I started the Pills, and the despondency passed off. I slept better at nights, and was able to take sub- stantial meals. As time went on Dr.Williams' Pink Pills made me strong, gave me a good colour and soon cured me of Anxmia."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>MAGISTRATES' CLERK.</head>
        <p>MAGISTRATES' CLERK.</p>
        <p>PROPOSAL TO INCREASE SALARY BY £lOO DEFEATED.</p>
        <p>Lively Debate.</p>
        <p>There iVnS a iive!yt aelatie aLtb ineeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening on a proposal by the General Purposes Committee to increase the salary of Mr. G. F. F. White, magistrates' clerk, from £5OO to £6OO per annum. In his application to the Committee, Mr. White stated :—The salary has not been increased since 1885, since which date the popula- tion of Warrington has very largely incieased and the boundaries of the borough, which has since become a county borcugh, have been extended. With these increases the work of my department has necessarily increased, and legisla- tion has conferred many new duties on the magistrates which have considerably increased Us work cf their clerk. The Court now sits daily and summonses are issued returnable daily, whereas formerly summonses were only heard twice a week. The following statement of the fees earned during the last three years gives some indication of the wotk entailed on my depirt- ment :-13.08-1909, £563 9. lid. ; 3909-1910, £636 Os. 3d. ; 1910-1911, £6lB Is. sd. I beg to point out that out of my salary I have to provide my own of6ce and clerks." It was resolved tbat the Home Secretary be recommended to increase the salary from £5OO to £6OO per annum. The MAYOR moved, end Alderman SMETHURST seconded, the confirmation of the minutes. Councillor WILKINSON moved that the minute be referred Lack, lecause he felt that the payment of £5(O a year for a borough like Warrington was ample —if not too much—considering that Al r.White had also private practice. From a published list of salaries of Manchester Corporation officials he found that it was recommended that the Clerk of the Peace for Man- chester should have an advance of salary from £lOO to £5OO a year. 'use MAYOR : It is an entirely different appointment. Councillor JEZ:. KINS : He has not a tenth part of the duties.</p>
        <p>The Tows CLERK : There is a magistrates' clerk as well as a Clerk cf the Peace in Manchester.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINFON said that even if the magis- trates' clerk in Manchester received £2,c00 a year, according to rateable value and population a salary of £5OO, with private practice, nets sufficient for the same office in IVarrington. No doubt the argument would be used that some years ago the magistrates' clerk received the fees and had no definite salary end that the fees were commuted for a fixed Hilary of £5OO. That was in 18E35. It was ev.ite evident that when a man commuted his fees for the handsome sum of £5OO the fees must have borne a good comparison with those of the present day. The MIN on : I Elmuld think they were less then than now. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON said that twenty-five years made a vast difference in the character of a town. There was less rowd3 ism and summoning to-day, in compariscn with the increased population, than there was in the old dais. (Hear, hear.) Education had brought that about, he was glad to say. Councillor LASIIrORD seconded, and said that Wigan, which lad a population almost twice as large as Warrington, paid its magistrates' clerk £450 a year. He, therefore, did not see why Mr. White should have a rise of £ICO.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON : Wigan is a good comparicon. Councillor ROBERTS characterised as "utterly mis- leading " the statement of Councillor Wilkinson that Mr. White had a salary of £5OO and private practice. Nominally the salary was £5OO, but the staff which Mr. , White had to keep had to he paid out of that sum. He had one clerk of considerable ability and experience whose salary must be considerable. Then there were the office expenses. If Mr. White netted £3OO be did very well, but be (Councillor Roberts) did not think for a moment that be got quite that amount. As to his private practice, those who knct, Mr. White nere aware that his official duties left little time for it; he did not think that ass matter of fact he did any private practice. He depended, he believed, practically entirely on the salary. If the principle of the payment by fees had continued Mr. White would have received £OlB last year or £l4B more than he was receiving now by way of salary, There was no question whatever that the present magistrate,' clerk had a great deal more work and responsibility than his predecessor had at the time when the fees were commuted. The Corporation had no right to make a profit out of the magistrates' clerk's department. It was only the correct thing to say that nobody had aright to make any money out of a lawyer's work except the lawyer himself. Speaking from a long and intimate acquaintance with Mr. White's work, Conncillor Roberts said he was a man of considerable ability and experience and he did not think there was a single case, since Mr. White took up the office, in which an appeal bad been lodged with success against any decision the raggistrates had given on his advice.tle was courteous, obliging, capable and indefatigable in his attention to the duties. The increase would not become a charge on the rates because they would only be paying Mr. White the amount he would be entitled to if fees were substituted for salary. Councillor JENKINS pointed out that in 1883 the income from fees was £566 18s. 3d. From that time down to the present,there had always been an increment carried to the credit of the Corporation and a profit had thus regularly accrued to the Corporation which they wese not entitled to receive. Councillor WILKINSON : If one man commutes any- thing for a given sum, can the other party call the balance profit? Councillor JENKINS expressed the opinion that the fees would continue to increase and thus carry to the borough funds more profit. The MAYOR said Councillor Wilkinson did not think the balance in favour of the party who commuted the fees could be considered profit. He (the Mayor) did not see how it could be anything else. If a man worked for £OOO and he (the Mayor) gave him £5OO a year in commutation, he (the Mayor) would consider he was £lOO to the good. That was common sense. What would Councillor Wilkinson call the balance—not loss? Councillor WILKINSON : If I sell a business for £5OO and it progresses I have no right to come to you and say "I want that £:500 altering into £600." The MAvott : You are fencing with the point. Councillor WILKINSON : lam not trying to fence. The MAYOR : Don't ask a question again if you know' all about it. Councillor ROBERTS : He does not know all about it or ha would not have said the silly things he did. Councillor PEMBERTON : Is Councillor Roberts iu order ?</p>
        <p>Councillnr ROBERTS: He says a man buys a business and himself carries it on— The MAYOR : You are not in order. Councillor licitEltrs : I admit lam not. Councillor PEMBERTON : Well, sit down. The MAYon : lam willing to tackle the point but he (Councillor Wilkinson) is determined not to take a reasonable answer. I think it is very unfair to the chair and I resent it. Councillor TOOLE asked for information as to the salaries paid in other towes. The MAYOR said there could be no comparison with other towns, because the salaries generally ranged in ratio with the fees. Councillor TOOLE : Councillor Lashford said the salary at Wigan was only £450. Does that mean that I crimes and offences are more numerous in Warrington than in Wigan ? The MAYOR : It would probably mean that. I don't see what it could mean except that a larger number of summonses is issued. The Towx CLERK : The Home Secretary fixes the, salary in proportion to the amount of fees in each town. Councillor TOOLE : Is it necessary that the matter should be brought before the Council? Would not a recommendation from the magistrates be sufficient? The TOWN CLERK: It has to come from the Council'.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Even then the Home Secretary takes bis own course. He may not confirm your recommenda- tion or he may alter the amount.</p>
        <p>Councillor HENSHALL said the magistrates' clerk was placed in a cleft stick. He could not do anything unless he got permission from the Council. When twenty-five years had elapsed sirce the commutation, Mr. White should have at leastla "lighting chance" and what he (the speaker) suggested at the meeting of the General Purposes Committee was that the salary should be raised to £sso.That would bring the question before the Home Office where it could be settled in a judicial atmosphere and probably more on its merits than by a Council which included many who had not been accounted worthy to be J.P.'s. (Laughter.) However, he supposed it was BD honour deferred. (Laughter). Councillor PEMBERTON : Well, Mr. Mayor, it is a questionable honour. (Laughter). Councillor lIENSH ALL said the difficulty was that the members to whom he referred did not possess special knowledge of the ability and zeal of the magistrates clerk. Alderman BURTON thought that considering Mr. White devoted only part of his time to the service of the town, the Council would be doing him justice by recommending nn advance of £5O. Aldermen SMETHURST and TINNION supported the recommendation of the committee as fair and remonable.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON said the very special pleading of Councillor Roberts was difficult to follow in all its phases, because it seemed to him to be contradictory, One of his remarks was to the effect that no one had a right to make a profit ont of lawyers. His experience was that no one had ever been able to do that. (Laughter.) He would like to see anyone doing so. (More laughter). Alderman Tmcrox : Here is a case in point. Councillor PEMBERTON : I don't think so. Continu- ing, be said that Mr. White was a puqlic servant and the Town Clerk was also a public servant, and they might as well say that because Mr. Whittle was fortunate enough some day to buy some property and make £l,OOO Drcfit for the Corporation,that he was entitled to it all. Councillor Pemberton quoted some of the fess earned. No-one bad ever advanced the argument in regard to the Town Clerk or the Deputy Town Clerk, who were both</p>
        <p>lass; that they should have a quid pro quo for any fees they j saved the Corporation. A saving of thousan c: of pounds wis effented on the Lawson contract but the C3rporation did not hand it over to the ofilcißl .In 1t352 the fees in the magistrates' clerk's department were MO ad, but in 1008-9 they lrd dropped to £3, There miel, huArever, pi; ,411.7e8- lion that the salary should be reduced. With advance(' education crime would surely be lessened, but it would ba hard on Mr. White if they rocomraended that when the fees fell eff he should be asked to accept £2.00. £h LIAVTh Tte ;:hildren's Act has imposed addi- tional work u_pon him. Councillor PEMBERTuN : And other Acts have done away with a great deal of crime. There is the Educa- tion Act, for instance. From 1982 to 1808-9 there was only a difference of £3 in the fees, showing how stationary crime had been. They might as well do away a ith a regular salary and go back to the fees. If they wanted to make an alteration, let them do so in a straightforward manner. Let them have a resolution that they no longer pay a salary. The MAvon : You can't do that.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : I suppose that is the law. A celebrated barrister once said the law was a " bass." Councillor HEVER ALL : No, it was not a barrister. I think it was Mr. Weller, senior. (Loud laughter.) The &amp;lAYOR pointed out that the fees were much lower in Wigan than in Warrington and the salary was apportioned according to the fees. Councillor PEMBERTON : It shows we have too much crime in Warrington yet. I am sorry we have more crime than Wigan. The MAYOR: Summonses do not all relate to crime. He added that the reason Parliament decided ripen a fixed salary instead of fees was to prevent abuse. Councillor PEMBERTON : I always thought officials in England were above that sort cf thing. The Tows CLERK said it would be quite competent for the Coutcil ,to apply to the Home Secretary to reduce the salary, if at any time they thought it advisable. Councillor PEMBERTON : There would be a fine chance in Warrington of doing such a thing. The MAYOR : You might do it yourself. Councillor PEMBERTON : I would have Ter) little support, I am afraid. Councillor JANE INS said the reason the cemmutation of fees was decided upon was to give the magistrates greater freedom in remitting fees to persons brought before them. They would not feel they were robbing their clerk.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON It was to prevent them robbing the poor, unfortunate people who came before them, to pay their clerk. Councillor DICKENSON, in supporting the amendment said Councillor Roberts bad informed them that there would not be above .£3OO left for Mr. White when he had paid his assistants' salaries, but he thought they had no proof of that. He should like to know how many cases on an average were heard at the police court. He was sure they must have "a power of crime" in Warrington. Alderman TINNION: Not necessarily crime. If you could distinguish and use light words, it would be better.</p>
        <p>Councillor DTCKENSON (to the Mayor) : I appeal to you to keep Alderman Tinnion in his place. Alderman FINNION : I object to these gross misstate- ments—villing everything which comes wader the magistrates' clerk's notice crime. If members were not so profoundly ignorant they would not use these phrases. The MAYOR : You rose to a point of order and I have no right to listen to you further. Alderman TINNION : There have been a good many out of order. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor DICK ENSON : There is co one out of order oftener than Alderman Tinnion. If he can throw any- body off, he is all right. Alderman TINNION : It does not take much...</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : iVe-cuiht-to kcep off personalities, as far as posslble. Couccillor DICKENSON : I have kept to the point. If the crime—</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : It is not crime. I objected to Coun- cillor Pemberten's using the word crime. The cases do not altogether relate to crime. Councillor DICKENSON : Then I will call them offences. I don't know any difference between the two, but it suits some people to throw you off. if it will suit them better I will call them offences. The MAYOR : If you look at the Chief Constable's report you will see the character of the offences. They are not all crime. In justice to the population of Warrington we should be careful in regard to the terms we use. (Hear, hear.) Councillor DICKENSON : I want to be fair. I am quite prepared to withdraw that expression and call them cffences against the law. (Hear, hear). We must have "a power" of offences in Warrington if we require an office with two or three clerks and on the top of that Mr. White all his abilities. It is a presumption for any gentleman on the Council to trot out such nonsense Councillor CunriNazoN : It is tommy rot . . . .</p>
        <p>Councillor DICKENSON asked the Council not to be gulled by the nice little stories they beard. Mr. White's brother was in the came office, and he questioned whether the office was not under the same name. Councillor Cc:vgiNuToN : He lives rent free. The MAYOR : What do you make out of his brother being in the same office ? Councillor Dicitmos : That he has not to pay the same rent.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : He has to pay his proportion of the rent._</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION He is not in the same office, although he is in the same building. Councillor DICKENSON (satirically) : I leave it to Alderman Tinnion. lie knows better than I. (Laughter). Alderman TINNION : That is the only true thing you have said to-night. (Renewed laughter). Councillor DICKENSON drew attention to the contrast between the treatment of workingmen and officials by the Council. If a workingman asked for an extra shilling— Councillor CUNNINGTeN: He will never get it. The MAYOR : Councillor Cunnington, I must ask you not to interrupt. If you want to make a speech you should get up. Councillor DICKINSON : If there is a shilling asked for by a workingman everybody is up in arms about it. There must bif an inquiry and a committee to investis gate as to whether he is able-bodied and what he doe-. Alderman TINNtos : There is to be an inquiry about this.</p>
        <p>Councillor DICKENSON : Mr. Mayor, will you keep this gentleman quiet—(iaughter)—or try to do ? lam afraid you will have a job. (Renewed laughter.) I know, my friend, you don't believe in the working man— Alderman TitoiroN : I have done a great deal more for the working man' and I have got a good cieal less out of him than you have in Warrington. The MAYOR : This is quite disoraerly. Comparisons are very odious. Councillor DICKENSON : I grant you there is no comparison between the salary of the workingman and of this individual. He may be qualified— The MAYOR: It was agreed to give the workingmen an increase of _3s. a week. . .</p>
        <p>Conncillor ROBERTS : decision of the Council!</p>
        <p>And it was the unanimous</p>
        <p>Councillor JACKSON HoLmEs testified to the assiduous way in which Mr. White always performed his duties. Councillor Pemberton had alluded to the enlightened times in which we lived, but the passing of the Education Act had caused a very great increase of work at the Police Court. He supported the minutes with the greatest pleasure. Councillor HAWTHORN raid they were all agreed that the work of the department was increasing but £5O would be a reasonable increase and he was prepared to vote for that sum, but not for £lOO. Councillor LOCKER also thought the minute should be referred back with a view to meeting Mr. White half way. The amendment was carried by 12 votes to 11, the voting being as follows: For the Amendment: Councillors Locker, Henshall, Ashcroft, Skelland, Halliwell, Wilkinson, Dickenson, Hawthorn, Cunnington, Toole, Pemberton and Lashford —l2. Against :—The Mayor, Aldermen Smethurst, John Evans and Tinnion, Councillors Wilson, Sankey, Steel, Roberts, Bellian, Jenkins and Jackson Holmes-11.</p>
        <p>Lord Delamere has given notice to 70 tenants on his Over, Winsford and Marton estates terminating their tenancies, the reason given for his action being that he intends to sell these portions of his land.</p>
        <p>• Chapter I. The Slavery of the Wash-tub Wash-day at its hest is a day of toil and worry and discomfort. Soap, steam and smell all through the house, smearing everything, blinding every- body. Rubbing, scrubbing, tubbing. Maybe it used to be necessary. It isn't now, at any rate—not since Fels-Naptha came. Those who have tried "the Fels- Naptha way" with other soaps may doubt it. If, by our advertisements, we could induce women to try Fels-Naptha soap in "the Fels-Naptha way" what a lot of friends we should make.</p>
        <p>Fcls-Naptha 39 Wilson street London E C</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>TIME TO FURNISH.</head>
        <p>THE TIME TO FURNISH.</p>
        <p>DURING RAY &amp; MILES'</p>
        <p>eLEPiRANeE STILE OF £lOO,OOO WORTH OF FURNISHING GOODS. ALL AT REDUCED PRICES. CARPETS AND LINOLEUMS LAID FREE ONCE A YEAR ONLY, FEB. STH TO MARCH 16TH.</p>
        <p>TABLE BIRDS-. _ _</p>
        <p>resp ct to other tastes and fancies. S) much then for the "eggs for hatching" side of the small- holder's business. Now about the</p>
        <p>LAYERS. At least you should mate up two pens for breeding from for egg-production. These should be a pea each of two first crosses, one for winter laying and one for spring and summer laying, and as I am tied down. to name only two out of the very long HA I do so after careful thought, and at the same time place their recommendation before you as a result of practical experience, Houdan —White Leghorn aid Hoadan— Buff Orpington. From these two pens you should not sell any eggs, but breed from as many as possible, commencing with the first pen in the month of March continuing tight on until June. From the second pen you should have already begun and continue until the end of May. We now leave the layers and come to the raising of bins for culinary purposes.</p>
        <p>Of these you should mate up at least three pens, because you would find plenty of ready customers for eggs for hatching. There is such a scarcity of small- holders who really mate up scientifically for the pro- duction of first-class table specimens. And in this direction they lose a very profitable means of adding to their income. These also should be a first cross between two pure brecds, and to simplify the is,ue I would recommend the following matings Game —Dorking, Indian Game—Sussex, and Faverolles —Durking.</p>
        <p>GENERIL REIEIRKS. So much then for having mated up your eight pens in all. The next best thing you should do would be to arrange an advertisement on the following lines. Mr. -- having mated up his pens for the present season, has eggs for sale from the following pens : Pen no. 1, Barred Rocks ss. a sitting of 13 ; pen no. White Orpingtons ; pen no. 3, Black Leghorns, same prices • unfertiles replaced or 15 eggs per sitting, unfertiles not replaced. Also sittings of eggg for sale from any of the following first crosses for table purposes: Indian Game—Dorking, Indian Game—Sussex, and Faverolles - D.,rking 3s. per sitting. Address so-and so. I will leave the figures subject to alteration, but at the same time consider them a fair average rate and certainly much more remunerative than ordinary market prices. You can always obtain the latter for surplus produce. Let me here say before passing that there is nothing pays like advertising,always providing your advei tis- ment is backed up with fair dealing and reliable stock. It may cost you in advance of receipts for the first season or two, but depend upon it, it will pay you in the end. Remember you are building up a business. People don't advertise for the fun of the thing. Per- sistent advertising pays. In conclusion let me say that not all by any means has been said on this all- important subject to the small-holders. There are many other points to observe, but these can be picked ,up as we prcceed with cur weekly observations.</p>
      </div>
