      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>[BY REDCAP].</head>
        <p>[BY REDCAP].</p>
        <p>DO HENS PAY?</p>
        <p>Last season I asked my readers to keep an account cf their poultry pursuits and forward me a balance sheet for publication. During the week one of them has sent along a very interesting account of last year's workings, and if we can all show the same results, then the above problem is a problem no lorger. The statement and balance sheet is as follows :</p>
        <p>"I am sending you kalance sheet,which I think will prove interesting to those who pet use your poultry column, and may help to decide the much debated question as to whether hens pay or not. I can vouche for the correctness of the balance sheet." Mr. had in January, 1911, 31 hens and one cockerel to commence with and at the end of the year had increased his stock with one hen, thus 35 hens and one ccckerel. He attributes his success to an occasional change of ground, keeping his fowls supplied with green food when same was scarce and providing a dry scratching shed for wet and cold weather. The profit may seem large from so few fowls but each item has been rigorously kept of both outgoing md incoming. Feeding Stuffs, Giit,&amp;c., Purchased during the year 1911. X s. d. January February 6 11 March April May 2 6 1 June July 2 13 11 August September October November December</p>
        <p>Total</p>
