        <p>I should be glad if cther readers would send along their results.</p>
        <p>From the " Feathered TVorld."</p>
        <p>LIGHT BRAH IIAS.</p>
        <p>Light Brahmas are the subject of this week's illustration. These are large handsome fowls, holding their own with most other breeds as wit,ter layers ( f rich brown shelled eggs. There is still another colour called the Datk Brahma because cf their dark pencilled plumage. Of late years the Brahma seems to have lost favour. There is perhaps a just cause why this should be. As a table bird it ranks high, but owing to so much heavy feathering they are not so easy to prepare as the clean-legged breeds.flowever they cross well with other varieties, and Brahmas of a good laying strain—when mated with either Indian game or Houdan make excellent winter layers and good table fowl. Let it be said to the credit of the Brahma that many a new breed has been originated with the aid of plenty of Brahma blood in their veins. And this has meant stamina, as readers will observe from the picture. As an exhibition fowl the Brahma ranks high and good specimens are frequently exported at high prices.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>SHALL-HOLDER.</head>
        <p>THE SHALL-HOLDER.</p>
        <p>Once the English small-holder has grasped all the essentials of practical poultry farming, then we can look forward with a degree of certainty for some real advancement in the poultry industry. In the hands of the small-holder poultry farming should be made a success. For this reason,—on a small-holding it is easier to pay closer attention to details, than when farming on a larger scale. It is also easier to specialise on the poultry section of the holding because you get more time to devote to the interests of your stock, aid to make utility pcultry farming pay one has to devote close attention to every detail. The reason why so many people fail to make a profit from their fowls is not far to seek. As a matter of fact it is here necessary to repeat °resell from a pre- ceding article. two weeks ago. I there stated some reasons for failure on cur large farms. The same reason holds good when applied to the small-holder. Very well then, let me give you a guide for future operations. There are three departments in which you must specrahr. As follows: eggs for hatching ; breeding and rearing for egg-production ; and breed- ing and rearing for table purposes. Let us deal with them in the order named.</p>
        <p>EGGS ror lIATCHING. Almost in every district there is a demand for sittings of eggs from pure breeds, sa it is necessary that you shc,u'd mate up a few pens of pure breeds. What breeds to take up largely depends upon local circumstances, such as the nature of the soil, etc. For instance supposing your holding is heavy clay land, then you should only take up those breeds best adapted to these natural conditions. Let us assume this to be so. The breeds best suited then would be as follows : —Barred Plymouth Rocks, White Wyandottes, White Orpingtmas and FaveroPes. And for a hardy non-sitting variety, the Leghorn (black or white) would suit best. Now on a small holding to be run on the lines suggested, you would not have convenience for a pen each of the aforementioned breeds. Therefore a good selection would be three pens oi the following : Barred Rocks. White Orpingtrns and black Leghorns. /cow again let us assume that the nature of the soil is light and dry. Then you could select three pens from any of the following, Buff OrpMgtons, Dorkings, Wyanclottes (any colour according to fancy) Langshans and Rhode Island Reds. Of the non-sittets you could choose from the Ancona, Andalusian, Minorca, Buff and Brown Leghorns. If I make a selection for you I should name Buff Orpington, Dorking and Black Minorca. At the same time I do so with every</p>
      </div>
