      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>„,•• :• 00 IILLA ./..,0,L,4:8,1,, it I 4sr% •I I • • ' ' 11 7A 111.1111 C ~ .... 4.,</head>
        <p>„,•• :• 00 IILLA ./..,0,L,4:8,1,, it I 4sr% •I I • • ' ' 11 7A 111.1111 C ~ .... 4.,</p>
        <p>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</p>
        <p>Many people were surprised on Saturday evening or Sunday morning to find from their papers that War- rington had not only played on Saturday, but had very nearly won at Wigan. The frost caused every other Northern Union match but this ale, to bs, cancellei. Grounds were as hard as cement, and the Wigan pitch was a long way from being soft when the cover- ing was removed, while the frost made it harder as the match proceeded.</p>
        <p>There was a rare good struggle between the teams, an Warrington gave about their best display this season away from home. They have not done so welt on the Wigan ground for some years, and the general opinion was that they would get a severe drubbing. But for once in a way they gave of their bast and Wigan were generally voted to be a lucky team to win 10-5. They got their points early, but after wards there was only one team in it. Warrington ought to have won, but the attack was lacking in finish at critical moments and the home team's defence prevailed after such a bombardment of the line as is rarely seen at Wigan.</p>
        <p>The gallant few who went with the Wirepullers were hugely delighted with the form they displayed, and only disappointed that they did not get their full reward. It certainly seemed as if the approach of the Cup-ties had given Warrineton the stimulus so long required. They were a different side on Saturday, from forivatil to lymk, and gave the Wigan folk something to remember. What the latter were con- tinually asking was: How did Warrington come to be in the second half of the League table ? Well, one rose does not make a summer and Warrington have played many poor games this season.</p>
        <p>The forwards covered themselves with glory, and reckoned nothing of the hard patches on the turf in their tierce following up and tackling. Wigan's six had to play second fiddle, and it was the hardest of luck, when Chesters and Tranter crowned the good work by dribbling over, to have the try disallowed by the referee. Every man distinguished himself, Skelhorn proving that his return to form was no "flash in the pan."</p>
        <p>Daintith and Brooks made a better show at half than has been seen on the Warrington side for some time. 'lle "A " team lad showed bettvr knowledge of beating a man before parting, while Brooks broke through in quite his best style. Some of his efforts were wasted by faulty passing, but he was the Brooks of old and made a great difference to the attack. Both centres were good, and b &amp;led up Thomas, Jenkins and Co., in fine style. Renwick was the best back on the field and fairly opened the eyes of his old comrades at Wigan. He made some fine openings for Bradshaw, but the latter only turned one to account and quite failed to re,dise the value of the inside pass when a tackle was certain. Five minutes of Jae,k Fish's judgment would have gained Warrington a memorable triumph.</p>
        <p>On the other wing Woodburn ran well, and finished badly. He tried to go through the full back instead of round him, and needs some lessans in side step. Once again : Apply, Fish's dancing academy ! In the rear line Mclntyre made one blunder which gave a try away, but he played such a greatly improved game that he deserves his place. His punting had some of the length and direction that used to 13 notable features of his work.</p>
        <p>Wigan seem to be in need of a tonic, and such stars as Jenkins, Todd, and Johnny Thomas were tallow dips in conmarison with former brilliance. Sharreck was on the suspension list, and Seddon made only a moderate substitute.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows: For. Against. Per- P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. c'nt'ge Huddersfrld23 20 2 1...86 143 601... 27 32 150 8913 Wigan 2l 20 4 0... 64 81 371... 27 27 135 83-33 Hunslet 23 16 6 1... 38 84 340... 38 36 184 7P73 Oldham 23 16 6 1... 67 94 416... 38 47 217 71-73 Hull K. R2l 15 6 0... 68 64 328... 27 37 165 7P12 St.llelens 22 15 7 0... 47 72 320... 34 37 179 68-18 Halifax 22 13 7 2... 41 66 280... 22 40 164 63.63 Wakefield T 22 13 8 1... 43 GO 266... 45 57 261 61.36 Broughton-23 13 9 1... 32 43 193... 29 34 160 5869 Batley 91 12 9 0... 36 41 195... 21 28 126 57-14 Dewsbury 22 12 9 1... 50 61 292... 36 49 223 56-81 Widnes 2l 10 8 3... 31 38 176... 19 32 134 5476 ........26Hu1l l3 11 2... 47 74 316... 34 44 230 53 84 Leigh 94 12 11 1... 10 51 233... 39 45 213 5208 Rochdale H. 22 11 11 0... 39 46 216... 35 59 247 56 30 Leeds 93 11 12 0... 40 68 281... 37 60 251 47 82 Warrington.2l 10 12 2... 27 48 198... 41 49 229 4583 Keigbley ...19 810 1... 34 41 200... 32 62 250 44.73 Swinton 23 8 12 3... 32 37 175... 39 52 234 4130 York 90 8 12 0... 29 35 163... 48 59 273 40 00 Salford 22 7 12 3... 28 37 167... 37 42 200 39.63 Barrow 90 6 13 1... 20 34 142... 52 67 305 3250 Runcorn 2l 5 15 1... 21 30 132... 40 50 230 26-19 Coventry 23 417 2... 27 26 132... 55 99 407 2P73 Ebbw Vale 20 2 15 3... 16 22 98... 55 82 356 17.50 Bradford 22 220 0... 23 26 124... 63 97 417 9-09 Bramley 2O 018 2... 16 18 86... 72 119 501 5.00</p>
        <p>For to-day's match with Runcorn, Warrington are relying on the same side with the exception of Clare for Woodburn and should easily avenge the New Year's Day defeat at Irwell-lane. The selected thirteen are: - Mclntyre; Clare, Dickenson, Renwick and Bradshaw, three-quarters; Daintith and Brooks, half-backs; Thomas, Shugars, Chesters, Fearnly, Tranter and Skelhorn, forwards.</p>
        <p>The " A "team to go away is : Brown, full-back ; Povey, Smith, Vernon, and Grainger, threequarters ; Lloya. and Arnold, half-backs; Harmer, H McCabe, P Boardman, Gibbs, Bradshaw, and Critchley, forwards. -- Waywell and Jordan are both suspended, as the result of a lively time at Widnes a fortnight ago. Waywell's time is up to-day, and Jordan has another week.</p>
        <p>The Australasians have finished their tour, and return with the best record of any touring side under the Northern Union. Only two Lancashire clubs in Wigan and Oldham and only two Yorkshire teams in Huddersfield and Batley can claim the satisfaction of having finished in front of the Colonials. And with the narrow victory by England at Nottingham, the full record of the tour gives 35 matches played, 28 won, 5 lost, and 2 drawn. Altogether the Colonials in these 35 games have scored 86 goals and 119 tries, against 16 goals and 63 tries by opponents. Gilbert heads the list of try-getters with 30 in 29 matches, V. Farnsworth has 18, and Frawley 13 in 11 matches.</p>
        <p>To-day's matches are as follow : Bramley v Batley. Bradford v Hull Kingston lovers Broughton Rangers v Wigan. Coventry v Huddersfield. Dewsbury v Rochdale Hornets. Ebbw Vale v Widnes Halifax v Wakefield Trinity. Hull v Hunslet. Keighley v Barrow. Leeds v Oldham. Leigh v Swinton. Warrington v Runcorn.</p>
        <p>While on the roof of a house at Bolton on Sunday afternoon, endeavouring to capture a neighbour's canary, James Simpson slipped and fell a distance of 60 feet, and was seriously injured.</p>
        <p>GOLF.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. The eleventh qualifying round for the Captain'a medal was played on Saturday. Scores :</p>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION, G roes. ll'cap. Net. A Knowles L A Voisey 9B ... 9 ... 89 •G E Guest Wli icliardson, jun. SECOND DIVISION. W Richardson lOO ... 15 ~. 85 B S Newcombe .. 108 ... 17 ... 91 A Bottomley 0 Lofthouse R T Fairclough</p>
