      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Frozen Out. Wigan it. Warrington the Only Match.</head>
        <p>Frozen Out. Wigan it. Warrington the Only Match.</p>
        <p>So severe was the frost last week-end that in the whole of the Northern Union area only one match was played—that between Warrington and Wigan at Central Park, Wig in. The " Colliers" had had their ground well protected, and it rolled out in fair condition, though there were places as hard as a board. The general opinion was that no football was possible and in consequence the crowd was much below the usual dimensions at Wigan, some 5,000 assembling when it became known that the match was certain to be played. Both teams were well represented, Warrington sub- stituting Daintith for Crcevey and Wigan playing Seddon at back in place of Sharrocks, suspended. The teams were : Warrington—Mclntyre, back ; Bradshaw. Dickenson, Renwick and Woodbdrn, threequarter backs ; Brookes and Dainteth, half-backs ; Thomas, Shugars, Chesters, Fearnley, Tranter and Skelhorn, forwards. Wigan—Seddon, back ' • Bradley, Jenkins, Todd and threequarter backs ; Prescott and Thomas, half- backs ; Seeling, Winstanley, Williams, Myers, Holding and Whittaker, forwards. Referee, Mr A Hesford Broughton ; touch judges, Messrs A Cross (St Helens) and J A Barrow (Preston). The weather was still very cold but bright, when Wigan started operations against the sun. Early punt- ing gave the homesters a footing in the Warrington half, and within five minutes Wigan were ahead. The ball was passed smartly out to Bradiey who doubled inside, and some clever handling resulted in Seeling scoring near the posts for Thomas to kick a goal. This early cleverness by Wigan was not maintained and Mclntyre changed the scene with a lofty punt and tackle of 'Todd in the home quarter. Brooks broke away well hereabouts and made a nice opening for Woodburn, who had only to side-step to score, but he ran (straight into the full back and was held. Wigan responded with some speedy work and Bradley made for the line, but was thrown in touch by Mclntyre. The Warrington forwards now found their feet, and some fine play by the front rank men was well supported by Dickenson, who dashed away and gave Bradshaw a flee opportunity, but the wing man was not mite speedy enough, and Seddon grassed him in the corner. J. Thomas temporarily cleared, but Warrington were soon back, and Woodburn made a dash which resulted in himself, Seeling, and a " bobby " rolling in a heap among the straw. Bradley aga7,n ran well into the Warrington half, but Dickerson brought him down in grand style, while Daintith did the came for Sealing. Wigan were persistent at this stase, and Mclntyre failing to clear properly sent the ball into the hands of Myers, who gave Winstanley a walk-over, Thomas gosling again. Wigan now held a lead of ten points, which was not deserved. Brooks made a sensational run half the length of the field, but his pass went astray and Wigan escaped. Thomas, Fearoley and Tranter harassed the home defence with fine following-up, and from a subsequent penalty Renwick kicked a goal. Brooks had another chance, but passed forward, and just on the interval Whittaker charged a kick and was only beaten by the dead ball line. Half-time: Wigan, 19 points ; Warrington, 2 points. On resumption, Wigan made an early attack and were then forced back by fine play from Warrington. The VVirepullers' forwards were an grand form and following their smart work, Daintith, 13rotIrs and Renwick passed brilliantly, the Colonial making a fine opening for Bradshaw, who raced over smartly. Thomas failed at goal, but his men were all over Wigan for the next twenty minutes. Chesters and Tranter dribbled over finely, and the surprise was general when the referee disallowed the try near the posts. Seddon kicked weakly and Warrington again went for the line, but the ball went dead. Wigan failed to clear from the drop-out, which was well met by Dickenson. and had the visitors been a little more exact in their handling they must have recorded points. J. Thomas eased the pressure slightly and tried to set Miller going, but the tackling did not allow of much progress. The Warrington forwards, led by Tranter (who was exhibiting One form) once more raised the hopes of the small band of supporters who had accom- panied them, and it required all the efforts of Seeling Prescott and Todd to hold the opposition in check. A line run by Skelhorn was checked by Jenkins. Renwick did grand wot kat centre, and got Bradshaw going more than once, but the wing man missed chances through failing to pass inside when the defence was drawn.Wigan held their lead to the end, but the best team lost. Final : Wigan 2 goals, 2 tries (10 points) ; Warrington 1 goal, 1 try (5 points). LANCASHIRE JUNIOR CUP. VENUE FOR THE FINAL TIE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Lancashire Union in Manchester on Monday evening, the Lancashire Junior Cup final between Rylands' Recreation and Runcorn White Star was fixed to take place at St. Helens on February 17th. Mr. R. H. Jackson, of St. Helens, is the appointed referee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCIIAM DISTRICT LEAOCE. FIRST DIVISION. RESULTS TO DATE.</p>
        <p>Goals.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. F. A. PTS. Mobberley lB ...12 ... 4 ... 2 ... 42 ... 23 ...26 Latchford l9 ...12 5 ... 2 ... 47 ... 26 ...26 Lostock Gralam l4 ...10 ... 2 ... 2 ... SO ... 18 ...22 Lymm l4 ..10 .. 2 . 2 .40 .. 21 ...22 Bowdon Vale 13 ... 9 ... 3 ... 1 ... 27 ... 25 .. 19 Broadheath 15 ... 7 ... 5 ... 3 ... 40 ... 26 ...17 Oughtrington Am't'sl7 ... 8 ... 3 ... 31 ... 27 —.15 Oughtrington Parkl2 ... 5 ... 7 ... 0 ... 26 ... 22 ...10 Altrincham Lads l2 ... 5 ... 7 ... 0 ... 22 ... 32 ...10 St. Vincent's l5 ... 3 ... 8 ... 4 ... 15 ... 32 ...10 Sale Albion 16 .. 2 ... 3 ... 19 ... 42 ... 7 Penketh l5 .. 2 ...12 ... 1 ... 13 ... 52 5 Kuutsford St Crossl2 0 ... 9 ... 3 ... 23 ... 76 ... 3 ST. VINCENT'S V. LATCHFORD. At Stamford Park. At the commencement play ruled even. After 20 minutes Ross (centre-half) obtained possession and with a capital effort put Meunier' in motion for the latter to beat Powell with a grand shot. half-time: St. Vincent's one, Latchford, nil. Upon the re-start Latchford got down early. Connor from outside left beat Carr and equalized the scores. Encouraged by this success the visitors put more life into the game and about 10 minutes suceeded in taking the lead through a good goal by Trafford. Aroused by this reverse the Saints applied pressure but weak play in front of goal nullified all efforts. Taking the game all through St. Vincent's had most of the play, but their forwards were weak in front of goal, a hard contested game ending—Latchford, two; St. Vincent's. one,</p>
        <p>L-YMM V. 0130I1TRINGTON PAnir. At Lymm. After 15 minutes' play a bad miss by Potts provided" Sparker " Shaw with an opportunity he was quick to take advantage of, running through and opening the Park's account. Still keeping up the pressure the Park had the best of matters and follow- ing a good centre by 3 Shaw, Heywood increased his side's lead to two goals. Half-time Oughtrington Park, two ; Lymm, nil. Re-starting, Lymm began to bestir themselves and gradually assumed the upper hand. After 20 minutes play Potter (who was making his first appearance this season) opened their account, following with a second a few minutes later, thus equalizing matters. Lymm were now overplaying the Park and amidst excitement in practically the last minute of the game Lymm gained a most fortunate victory,Simpson dropping n long shot and allowing it to roll into the net. Final: Lymm, three goals ; Oughtrington Park, two goals: ALTRINCHAM AMATEUR CUPS OVCITITRINGTON AMATEURS V. LYMNI LADS.</p>
        <p>At Heatley this postponed tie proved a more even encounter than NC as generally anticipated. Under the circumstances both sides showed some good football. In the end the senior club just managed to gain the verdict. Score Amateurs 3 ; Lymm Lads 1.</p>
        <p>JUNIOR COMPETITION. Robertson's, 3 ; Stockton Heath St. Thomas', 0. Altrmcham Lade, 4 ; Altrincham St. Margarets, 0.</p>
        <p>A GOOD CASE, The appreciation of the public; the com- mendation of doctors, and the support of the Press make together a very good case in favour of margarine as a food. Those who have never used margarine before would do well to cast all prejudice aside and just try 'Stork,' by far the finest Margarine obtainable. They will discover, no doubt to their surprise, that they can detect no difference in flavour or appearance between ' Stork ' Margarine and choice country butter. In the certificate of merit awarded for 'Stork' Margarine by the Incorporated Institute of Hygiene, there is sufficient evidence of quality; medical' testi- mony proves a stamlard, of: food value as high as that of butter; and, the economy is obvious --indeed,' i nteanl- a, saving of half.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>OPEN SPACES AND TOWN PLANNING.</p>
        <p>V/ARRINGTON'S "DARK SPOTS."</p>
        <p>I The 7Alayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph) presided at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council. which was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening: There were also present :—no Deputy Mayor (Alder- man Forshaw)' Aldermen T Button, John Evans, James Evans, J Smethurst and D Tinnion ; Councillo'rs G Archer, T H Ashcroft, W H Bellian, T Hirtles, T Chantler, E Cunnington, J Dickenson, .1 llalliwell, J Hawthorn, R Henshall, C Jackson Holmes, H J Jenkins, Li' T Lashford, J T Locker, J Pemberton, H Roberts, ..1" Sankey, J Skelland, T r.. 5 Steel, 0 Toole, J Wilkinson and J W ; Messrs J L Whittle (Town Clerk), A T Hallaway (Deputy Town Clerk), I" lf Spinks (Assistant rown Clerk), J Fairhurst (Borough Treasurer), W H Woodcock (Assistant Treasurer), W S Haddock (Gas Engineer), F V L Mathias (Electrical Engineer). J M Murray (Director of Education) and J Gray (Water Engineer). THE LATE DUKE OF FIFE.</p>
        <p>Before the Council entered upon the ordinary busi- ness the MAYOR made a symrathetic reference to the King's recent bereavement. It was particularly unfor- tunate that the rejoicings on his Majesty's home- coming had been damped by the catastrophe to the submarine A 3 and the death of the Duke of Fife. It was characteristic of his Majesty's consideration for his subjects that he had, as it %ere, suspended his private feelings of grief,so that he might not encroach upon the pleasure they would have in meeting him again and giving him a loyal welcome. (Hear, hear.) He (his Worship) wro sure he was echoing the sentiments of the Council when he said they looked upon the death of the Duke of Fife with the utmost regret. (Hear, hear).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>AN ALLEGED NUISANCE.</head>
        <p>AN ALLEGED NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Councillor Bellian attended the Health Com- mittee and lodged a complaint as to pigeeries in the neighbourhood of Wellfleld-street, and the Inspector of Nuisances reported that he had investigated the com- plaint and was making observations thereon, with a view to preventing any nuisance. Councillor BELLTAN asked for an assurance that the result of the inquiries would be reported to the Council. Alderman JAMES EVANS replied that Councillor Bellian bad had the assurance that the whole matter would be gone into and the bylaws enfotced as far as they applied to the complaint. They were vigorously carrying out what they promised. Councillor BELLIAN said he did not know what the powers of the Elealth Cunmittee were but he agreed with the inhabitants of the district that the nuisance ought to be abated. Many things were being prevented by the Health Committee that were not half so danger- ous to the public health as the case under notice. He had heard that most of the piggeries in question were made of wood, which was not a healthy condition of things. During the hot summer the stench was " too awful to think about." _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman JAMES EVA'SS : I think Councillor Bellien should surely allow the Health Committee to mind its own business, and to give its servants legitiraate time to make investigation. Councillor PEMBERTON objected to the attitude of Al&amp; rman Evans. Any member of the Council bad a perfect right to interfere with the work of a committee and that should never be questioned. (Hear, hear). Alderman JAMES EVANS : I did not say a member had not a right. The MAY on remarked that after the committee had dealt with the matter it would come before the Council on the minutes. Councillor Haitian would get satis- faction one way or another. Councillor BELLIAN : That is all I want. The minutes were confirmed. NEW ENGINE FOR HOUGHTON GREEN PUMPING STATION. TO 'BORROW £5030. The Water Committee had resolved that application be made to the Local Government Board for sanction to borrow £5,6C0 for the provision of the new engice required at Houghton Green pumping station.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>COAL TENDERS.</head>
        <p>COAL TENDERS.</p>
        <p>In moving the confirmation of the minutes of the Water Committee, Alderman Joinz EVANS remarked that three local firms tendered for coal at the same price, and the engineer was instructed to report on the quality of the fuel. However, the miners' unrest caused two of the tenders to be withdrawn. The tender of the Warring- ton Slate Company was then considered. The company were not prepared to let them have the full amount of 45 tons per week, but the committee had agreed to take 25 tons per week, which, with the assistance of the stock of coal in hand, would meet their requirements for a considerable time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>minutes were approved. THE BREAKDOWN AT THE ELECTRICITY WORKS.</head>
        <p>The minutes were approved. THE BREAKDOWN AT THE ELECTRICITY WORKS.</p>
        <p>The Engineer, at a meeting of the Electricity and Tramways Committee, reported that the lighting and power supply was interrupted from 9.23 to 9.35 pm. on the 19th instant, owing to a fault which occurred on the rotor of the turbo alternator, and that the No. 5 engine was very promptly run up and the supply resumed. It was resolved that the committee express their acknowledgement of the prompt and energetic way in which Mr. R. L.iEdgar, the engineer-in•charge, had acted on the occasion. . . .</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST moved the confirmation of the minutes, and,referring to the interruption in the supply of electricity, said it was owing to the practical know- ledge and experience t,f their engineer that the lights were only out a few minutes. Thanks to the foresight of the committee and the engineer they had also a duplicate engine which enabled them to cope with emergencies,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>TRACIME§s</head>
        <p>TRACIME§s</p>
        <p>Councillor lIA ‘,‘ 'Malik referred to a resolution ot the committee, instructing the Tramways Manager to prepare a report on the working and cost of motor omnibuses for linking up the tramway service. Should it not be trackless trams ? _</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETIIURST said the report should cover both motor omnibuses and trackless trams.</p>
        <p>TRAM "COMFORTS."</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON asked when the Committee intended to have the trams put in reasonable order. During the bard winter weather, they were in just the same condition as in midsummer. All the windows were open and not a single catch would act. The public provided the profits and ought to have some considera- tion. Recently he had been riiiing in trams in different towns and found them more comfortably adapted for winter travelling than the Warrington trams. Alderman SMETHURST said the Committee bad already changed the system of ventilation three times, but nothing would satisfy some people. If the trams that Councillor Pemberton travelled in elsewhere were so comfortable, it was a pity he did not go to sleep and stop there. (Laughter). It would have been no bad job for some of them. (Laughter). They would have had less talk and more work. (Laughter). The committee were doing all they could to satisfy the travelling public and the receipts from the trams were going up £2O and £3O a week. Councillor DICKENSON : Then it would not be a bad idea to cease robbing the public and commence a system of halfpenny stages. The MAYOR : " Robbing is a rather strong term. The minutes were approved. WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETYMIIST, proposing the confirmation of the minutes of the Bridges Committee, remarked that they were now making slight progress at Warrington Bridge—(Councillor Pemberton : Hear, hear)—and he thought they were doing as well as they could, consider- ing the weather. Lie should like to see more men on the ground. The Mayor seconded. Councillor Pit:MtErefON said be was pleased to bear the Chairman of the Bridges Committee state that some progress was to be made at last, but he ought to ccuitev to the contractor the intimation that more men should be put on. There were plenty of men for that kind of work. Now that the frost had gone they ought to push on with the scheme. HOWLEY FOOTBRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Ancosit drew attention to the " appalling " delay that had occurred in connection with the Howley footbridge. He thought the burgesses of Fairfield, St. John's and ll'owley Wards should have more consideration. _ _ _</p>
        <p>-117ie-fo-\-:.;-&amp;-a—u-K7 said he had Leen expecting a reply from Mrs. Hewitt, the owner of the land, for the last' three coelcs. OPEN SPACES AND TOWS PLANNING. A PADGATE-LANE PURCHASE. The General Purposes Committee considered tkndera for open spaces in the neighbourhood of Padgate-lane and Manchester-road, arid recommended the acceptance of Mr. Peter Lawton's tender for the sale of a plot of freehold land containing four and three-tenths acres or thereabouts, at £sllo'per acre, on the following con- ditions :-1. That the land be used as an open space or recreation ground. 2: That the Town Council adopt the town-planning idea OA, reduce the )ridth of' the carriageways and allow wider footpaths) in the streets on the south side of Oakwood-avenue: 3.. Mr. Lawton (at his own expense) to plant trees on the footpaths- in the streets referred to in elanse 22and That the pur- chase he completed, subject to the approval of the Local Government Board, within three months of the date of the next meetihg orthe Council. Alderman TINNION moved that the minutes be refetred back, in order that they might know what the meaning of the 2nd clause was. It onStituted a very important departure in the construction of streets. Councillor PEMURTON must rise tit it point of order. There is a mistake on the agenda.' Alderman TINNION I declinete give'way. Contien- ing, he said that under the new conditiowth4 *odd</p>
