        <p>:f‘ It is Invaluable for Delicate Children."</p>
        <p>be paying more attention to the footpaths than to the carriage-way. His experience as Chairman of the Paving and Sewerage Committee was that the most important thing they could do was to give adequate carriage-way as well as adeq late footpaths. The v% hole trouble in connection with Warrington Bridge was that the carriage-way had been too narrow and they had to spend £20,000 or £30,000 to widen the bridge. Councillor liouEnTs, in seconding the amendment, said it was understood that the condition as to the town planning idea and clauses 3 and 4 were not condi- tions absolute. They were submitted for the favourable consideration of the Council but were not incumbent upon them as conditions of purchase. It should be borne in mind that, once they had adopted the new rule, they could not stop at an experiment but would have to apply it to all the suburbs. Councillor PEMBERTON said Alderman Tinnion had been beating a dead horse, as there was no intention to incorporate the clause to which he took exception in the conditions of purchase. The committee were not as stupid RS he would make out.</p>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION : I am glad to hear of your improvement. (Laughter.) The MAYOR said he grudged money for open spaces in new districts when it might be spent so much more usefully, from the sanitary point of view, in improving some of the bad parts of the town. They were going far afield and shutting their eyes to really urgent and necessitous work in their midst, while they contemplated work the necessity of which was debatable. He did not want to be disloyal to the com- mittee but it would be inconsistent on his part not to object. Some parts of Warrington would be a disgrace to any town. He had no doubt there were other towns as bad or worse, but the Council ought to give atten- tion to the parts which needed it most. Councillor JACKSON IfoLmcs said it was high time Fairfield Ward had a turn at recreative schemes and he trusted the amendment would not have the effect of throwing back the purchase. Councillor LOCRRIL said he was certain that the gentleman who was offering the land would not be desirous of imposing any conditions that would not satisfy the Corporation's -requirements. If Alderman Tinnion had been present at the negotiations, he would have been satisfied with the explanations given. He (the speaker) agreed with the Mayor that there were many dark spots in the town but they might have been prevented if our ancestors had exercised reasonable foresight. It was to prevent such trouble in the future that he supported the proper development of the suburbs.</p>
        <p>Eventually it was decided to refer back clauses 2 and 3, the remainder of the minute being approved.</p>
