      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>TOWN PLANNING.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural Dib- trict Council was held in Bewbey Chambers. Warringtnn, on Wednesday afternoon. Mr J Backhou3e presided, and other members present were --Messrs 0 Mather Houghton, A Knowles, Ball, Brookes, Rev A M Mitchell, Dr Murray, J C Sutton (clerk), 13 S Newcombe (surveyor), H H Human (assistant surveyor), F Kerfoot (inspector), and Dr Sephton (medical officer).</p>
        <p>310I;SINd AND TOWN PLANNING. At a special meeting of the Council the subject of the next line of procedure following upon plan No. 2 under the Housing and Town planning Act was fully considered and it was decided as a preliminary to make application to the Local Government Board to sanction a scheme that all owners and occupiers interested in the properties included within the scheme be called together by another notice. To enable this to be done a complete register of such owners and occupiers would be necessary. The preparation of the register was thoifilot to be work outside the ordinary duties of the Council's official staff. It was considered that the assistant overseers as collectors of rates were the best qualified outside persons to obtain the required inform- ation and were appointed to act with the surveyor. At A further meeting by request Messrs Fairclough, Foster, Smith and INright, assistant overseers were in attendance and after a conversation upon the nature and probable extent of duties to be nerforrned,acd also s.k tigni ittspay tO them for COnparcAM official work,it was unanimously decided to appoint each one of them to prepare a list of p4ettonS interested within the parish and that the resolutioti filati apply to Mr. Cooper, assistant overseer for Winwick who was unable to attend before the Council. It was also resolved to pay reasonable remuneration to the persons employed on satisfactory completion of the work.</p>
        <p>11OLLYBUSII-LANE REPAIRS. Mr. NEWCOMBE (surveyor) reported on the cost of putting liollybush-lane in good condition and stated that it would be about £4OO. It was decided that the Surveyor make the necessary arrangements for carrying out the work. EFFECTS OE TILE SNOW STORM. . . .</p>
        <p>The Surveyor in his report, which was adopted, stated that the snow ploughs did excellent work after the heavy snow storm of 18th inst. By evening practically every secondary and district road in the district had been traversed. That was accomplitned for the first time without additional manual labour.</p>
      </div>
