      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>WES r LANCASHIRE CHAMPIONSHIPS.</head>
        <p>WES r LANCASHIRE CHAMPIONSHIPS.</p>
        <p>By the hind permission of Lord Derby, the 21st annual championship of the West Lancashire Association (formerly Ltverpool and District C.C.A.) were held at Knowsley Park on Saturday last. Although devoid of ploughed land, a capital course had been mapped out, but owing to the liost, the going was rendered very hard and many reputations were severely shaken, an shown by the appended results :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>JUNIOR RACE.</head>
        <p>JUNIOR RACE.</p>
        <p>(Distance about 7,', miles). Time. min. sec. 1 W Wharton, Ravenhead H. and A.O 43-11 2 R Young, Wallasey H. and A.O 43-32 3 .1 Dutton, Runcorn Tally Ho 43-45 4 F Shadwell, Warrington A.O 43-52 5 S Chamberlain, Lancaster Park, H. and A.C 44-1 6 C Thompson, Liverpool Boundary 7 A Gardner, Lancaster Park 8 J Hands, Warrington 9 W Thorniley, Warrington 10 C H Spence, Wallasey 44-34 11 J E Towers, Lancaster Park 44-33 12 T 11111, Ravenhoyd 44-37 13 1' Jones, Runcorn 14 A G &amp;ohs, Wallasey 15 Jlt Crane. Ravenhead 16 E Scott, Warrington 4- 9 17 11 Wilcock, Ravenhead 45-11 18 H J Yates, Ravenhead 45-12 19 W Litherland, Warrington 45-17 20 P Scott, Wallasey 45.19 Team placings.—Warrington A.C. : Shadwell, 4; Hands, 8 ; Thorniley, 9; Scott, 16 ; Litherland, 19 ; Rowlinson, 20 ; 85 points. itavenhead : Wharton, 1 ; Hill, 12 ; Crane, 15 ; Wilcock, 17 ; Yates, IS, Platt, 22 ; 83 points. Wallasey: Young, 2 ; Spence, 10 ; Scoins, 14 ; Scott, 20 ; Baker, 24 ; Bale, 32 ; 102 points. Runcorn Tally Ho, 149 points. Lancaster Park, 166 ; Sutton, 255 ; Sacred Heart, 261 ; Liverpool Boundary, 282 ; Richmond, 347 ; St. Helens Recs., 363 ; also ran Lancaster Primrose, Liverpool Harriers, Liverpool Gymnasium Harriers, Ryland&amp; W.R.C.H., and St. Helens Parish Church. Preston were absent. _</p>
        <p>In tho scrimmage at the start S Holbrook, W Thorniley and W Litherland, all of Warrington, were knocked over and lost considerable ground. The former was early compelled to retire,and although badly spiked Litherland ran through. The race for individual honours from the outset confined to the five who eventually held the leading positions and Wharton getting away after 2 miles had been covered won by 50 yards from Toting who beat Dutton in the run home. Shadwell made up consider- able headway in the last half mile and beat Chamberlain for the fourth position. The team race was keenly contested and eventually resulted in a tie between Warrington A.C. and Raven- head. At 31 miles the team points totalled : Ravenhead 83, Warrington 99; at 51. miles Ravenbead 78, Warring- ton 86 ; at 7 miles Ra-venhead 79, Warrington 82 but these counts did not include the runner officially placed sixth in the race and there is little doubt the runner referred to did not cover the complete course so that 0 Gardner will probably be included with his club- mate,Chamberlain, in the list of those entitled to com- pete in the " Northern" by virtue of finishing in the first six places although not of the first 4 placed teams. SENIOR RA CE. Time. (Distance about 9;*l miles). Mins. Secs. 1 A G Roberts, West Cheshire 33 27 2 WII Bigg, Sutton H 53 42 3 H Holbrook, Warrington AC. 54 10 4 J Guntrip, West Cheshire 54 27 5 3 T Rimmer, West Cheshire 6 A Siddall, Warrington 54 51 7 E Harrison, Sutton 55 2 8 J Smart, Sutton 55 36 9 S Welding, Sutton 10 R McCaughey, North Liverpool Gym 35 41 11 IV Bolton, Warrington 12 D Brown, Sutton 13 T F Turton, Warrington 56 0 14 J Morris, Sutton 15 F H Small, West Cheshire 56 36 __ _ .</p>
        <p>16 T M rendes, West Cheshire 56 37</p>
        <p>17 C Holland, glued Heart . 5(1 43</p>
        <p>18 CI A Walker, West _Cheshire</p>
        <p>....anCr, 11 My M McGann- Sacred Heart</p>
        <p>W W Knowles' Seemed Heart . . Tearn I. Sutton: Hogg 2, Harrison `2, Smart 8, Welding 9 Brown 12, Morris H. Tot‘l 52 points. 2, West Cheshire : Roberts I, Guntrip 4, Rimmer 5, Small 15, Furniss 16, Walker 18. Total, 59 points. 3. Warrington AC.: Holbrook 3, Siddall C. Bolton 11, Turton 13, F Antrobus 21, W Antrobus 22. Total 76 Points. 4. Sacred Heart 140. North Liverpool Gynasium and Wallasey failed to finish teams.</p>
        <p>With the exception of W A Jeffrey, a candidate for Olympic honours, who retired before half the distance had been covered, the race for the individual position was always confined to the first six. Suited by the going Begg and Roberts led alternately until a mile from the finish, when Roberts came away and won by about 60 yards. Holbrook overhauled Guntrip in the last half mile, but although full of running, failed to find the leaders in the gathering darkness. Siddall ran steadily throughout and did well to 11111 J T Rimmer to la few yards on a course so agreeable to the ex-world's I champion. Bolton finished strongly and should reverse the positions with many in the "Northern." Turton gave a glimpse of the form of which he is capable and should improve his position. F Antrobus, W Antrobus and II K Jepson completed the course although lin difficulties from shortly after the start and their efforts were confined to finishing a team to qualify for the Northern." Vose retired before two miles was covered. Parsonage and Ditchfield were absent and as the only available reserve was required to make 1 instead of 10 in the junior race, Warrington seniors only totalled 8 instead of 10 runners. Sutton held the whip hand practically all the way and although prob- ably not so good as last year were able to make the Rudge-Whitworth Cup their own property by winning for the third time so the " collier lads would not be afraid to go home in the dark" after their hard earned victory.</p>
