        <p>New Vicar of Frodsham.</p>
        <p>The Dean and Chapter of Christ Church, Oxford, have appointed the Rev. Miles Weight Myers, who has been Precentor of Christ Church Cathedral and chaplain of Magdalen Collate, to the living of Frodsham, in succession to the late Rev. Canon Blogg.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd George's vin- THE CRUSHING dication in the City of OF his financial administra- BONAR LAW. tion was absolutely crush- ing in its exposure of Mr. Bonar Law's Albert Hall misrepresenta- tions and miscalculations. He showed that Mr. Bonar Law, in accusing the Government of increasing national expenditure, had reckoned-in the local taxation grants which now pass through the Exchequer accounts, though no more money is paid under that heading—a trifling error of eight millions! And Mr. Sonar Law also reckons as in- creased expenditure the money required to pay out of current income charges which the Tory Government used to meet by borrowing—another trifling error of three millions! The difference between Liberal and Tory finance is that the Liberal Govern- ment pays its way, and the Tory Govern- ment did not even try to do so. It is impossible for Mr. Bonar Law to point to any item of Liberal expenditure and say that a Tory Government would reduce it. The Tories proposed impossible amendments to the Old Age Pensions measure which would have increased the cost far beyond anything which the Exchequer could meet. They announced at by-elections that they had in their pockets a " Better Bill " than the National Insurance scheme. Did they mean that they proposed to reduce the State contribution and throw the extra bur- den on the working man? And as for the one unproductive item of expenditure—that is, upon armaments—the motto of the Tories would seem to be that of certain turf agents—" No Limit." When Mr. Bonar Law undertook to prove that the efficiency of the Liberal administra- tion, in which even political opponents who are not blinded to facts by too violent prejudice believe, was a sham, and in his corrosive " analysis proceeded to attack Lord Haldane's work at the War Office, he forgot that vituperation would carry no weight against expert opinion. The Tory Times holds no brief for a Liberal Govern- ment, but their military expert says:— "When we are told by Mr. Bonar Law that the weapons of our Army are utterly inferior to those of other nations, the first thought that occurs to us is that some members of the Unionist Party deserve to be hanged; for our present rifle and our present field-gun were both introduced by the Unionist Government." And the Army and Navy Gazelle, a purely Service organ, has made the scathing com- ment on Mr. Bonar Law's "-howler ' that soldiers would like to see " this inexperi- enced politician " refraining in future from talking on subjects of which he is totally ignorant. Not content with charging the Gov- ernment on grounds of pure ignorance with inefficiency and financial maladministration, Mr. Bonar Law and the Tory press, follow- ing in his tracks, have charged the Govern- ment, on grounds of pure malice, with gross corruption. It is true that social legisla- tion on a large scale has necessitated the creation of new staffs of officials. But Mr. Lloyd George has shown that elaborate precautions were taken to ensure that poli- tical views should not be taken into account in the choice of suitable candidates. As a matter of fact, though there are (as the last General Election showed, when the effect of the plural vote has been considered) a good many more Liberals than Conservatives in Great Britain, the new posts have been filled with at least as many Conservatives as Liberals, and probably the Conservatives have now the advantage. That is what Mr. Bonar Law calls the "spoils system."</p>
        <p>We call our readers' DUMPING attention to MrParker's IN GERMANY—A article on our exports SUGGESTION, and imports with Germany, which will be found on page 6. In view of the fact that during the past decade the imports or "dump" of manufactured goods has increased in Germany 364 Millions (69'6 per cent) and in the United Kingdom only 20 Million (18'2 per cent), we would suggest to the German' Social Democrats the desirability of holding Dumping Exhibitions. Perhaps Lady Greenall would kindly lend the German working men the stock of mottoes, literature, &amp;c, used in Warrington. In the translation the word Free Trade could be substituted for Tariff Reform and so altered could be used to prove to German notectionists ( whom recent elections show to be in a minority) how much better the country would be under Free Trade, and how futile tariffs are in keeping manufactured goods out.</p>
        <p>Mr. Aubrey White SARCASM, POLITICS poses as somewhat of a AND CHARITY. humorist, as well as an</p>
        <p>• election agent, and when he refers to the " Examiner's' sarcasm," we feel that something is demanded of us. A !little investigation of what Mr. White had to say to the Junior Conserva- tives last week convinces us that his best humour is of the uncOnscions variety. For instance, Mr. White said very seriously but enthusiastically : "He doubted whether any man in England could, in the peculiar circum- stances, have fought the election at Warrington as well as did Mr. Harold Smith. The election came shortly after his adoption. . . . Then he took part in a theatrical performance for charities, and the election. came on top of that."</p>
        <p>The italics are ours, but we have to thank Mr. White (Mr. Smith's election agent) for dotting the i's and crossing the t's so clearly. We always had doubts about that theatrical performance "for charities." Now we know on definite authority that it was part of the Tory campaign. The cloak of charity covers a multitude of sins</p>
        <p>There was very little LICENSING BUSINESS, beyond formal business at the ahnual licensing sessions yesterday. One licence was very properly objected to by the Chief Constable on the ground that the licensee had been convicted for three serious offences during the year, and the case will be taken at the adjourned sessions. All other licences were renewed, and one off-licence granted to Bolton's Brewery. From the Chief Constable's report to the Magistrates we learn that there is a reduction of two licences as compared with last year, but there are still 190 left, giving an average of one licensed house to every 379 inhabitants. There is a decrease</p>
        <p>'LINSEED COMPOrND ' for Coughs end Colds. A pro yen efkacy ; 91d. &amp; is. lid. Of Chemistsonly.-Adv</p>
