      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>NUTS 81 SLACK, WASHED</head>
        <p>NUTS 81 SLACK, WASHED</p>
        <p>NUTS, BEANS 8/ PEAS</p>
        <p>of 3 in the total number of persons pro- ceeded against for drunkenness, and a decrease of 48 in the resident offenders, but Warrington still stands second highest in ten Northern towns for the number of prosecutions for drunkenness. Only Middlesboro' beats our percentage of 804 per 1,000, and neighbouring towns like St. Helens and Wigan come far belowus with such figures as 4.79 and 376. Is Warrington really more drunken than these towns, or has the activity of our police and the leniency of others, something to do with this displeasing disparity in figures!</p>
        <p>We have frequently WOMEN DRINKERS. heard complaints of the</p>
        <p>amount of more or less secret drinking done by women in Warring- ton, and the number of sick, burial, or tontine clubs at public-houses which are responsible. The magistrates have, none too soon, caused inquiries to be made into the number and nature of these women's clubs, and at the sessions yesterday, it was announced that the clubs would be discontinued in conse- quence. Whether the magistrates have really grasped the extent of the evil and taken sufficiently severe measures to check it, we take leave to doubt. Strict surveillance will, most certainly, have some good effect, but unless the sharpest eye is kept on the delinquent publicans, the women's clubs will be gradually revived. Some of the gravest scandals have originated with these inducements for women to enter public-houses and to get into the drinking habit. Warrington is not alone in tackling the evil, for we notice that the Birmingham Licensing bench gave expression to even stronger terms of disapproval on Wednesday. The Chairman said: In their visits to public-houses the committee had been unfavourably impressed by the number of women who frequented them during the absence of their husbands at work. They were to be found in passages in the out-door depart- ments, snugs, and dark corners away from public observation.</p>
        <p>A great deal now depends on the sincerity of the Trade's endeavour to prove worthy or a good conduct mark! .</p>
        <p>Mr. M. Nicholls, Chief</p>
        <p>OUR POLICE. Constable of Warring-</p>
        <p>ton, has submitted his annual report to the Watch Committee, and gives the 85 members of his force an unqualified testimonial for good conduct. Two constables "retired compulsorily," so we may reasonably presume that the black sheep have been driven out and the blue flock is now without blemish. During the year the police have been well and fully employed. The number of crimes reported was 228—the same number as in the previous year the amount of property stolen or fraudulently obtained was £6Ol, and the amount recovered, £384, thus leaving a modest margin for the light-fingered or dishonest fraternity. Twenty nine juvenile offenders(an increase of 7) were apprehended; while there were 1511 people proceeded against for non-indictable offences. Among many miscellaneous duties the police restored 337 children to _ their parents, and apprehended 259 horses, cattle and sheep which were found straying on the highways; no less than 173 stray dogs were seized-76 being destroyed, 31 sold, and 65 restored to owners. Careless people left 356 articles lying about during the year, and of these 196 went back to their owners, 65 to to the finders when owners could not be traced, 13 were destroyed, and 82 are still unclaimed. What is to become of these latter we are not informed. There are many other serviceable duties which the police performed: they collected £983 for special services, licences, registrations, aud so forth, the motorists alone bringing £124 grist to the mill. Finally, no less than 72 members 'of the Force hold St. John Ambulance certifi- cates. A most efficient and useful organisa- tion, these burly men in blue</p>
        <p>"Ulster will fight" ULSTER DID NOT was not borne out by FIGHT the happenings at Belfast on Thursday. True, there was plenty of organised factionism and unseemly hooting in the streets, but Mr. Churchill was not deterred from the delivery of a calm, statesmanlike and authoritative statement on Home Rule, which heralds the dawn of a new political era for Ireland and points a safe way for Irish Unionism in the future. Probably the most striking feature of the pronouncement was the fact that it was plainly pre- conceived and initialled by the Cabinet. Mr. Churchill sketched with great care and thoroughness the lines on which the Government's Home Rule Bill will be drafted, and Mr. Redmond rose immediately and said :—" I accept every word of it. I accept every one of the safeguards to be inserted in the Bill." So much for the illusion that the acutest Parliamentarians who have ever sat in the British House of Commons will aid the Tories to destroy [their hopes on the very threshold of fulfilment. The prospect for the passing of the measure on which the Govern- ment have staked their political existence is immeasurably brighter as the result of Mr. Churchill's reasoned epitome, and is not in the least dimmed by the fumes and smoke of such firebrands as Sir Edward Carson, Lord Londonderry, and Captain Craig. These gentlemen should, by now, have a full under- standing of what is meant by the phrase "exploding a damp squib." • -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>NEW SCHOOL AND RECREATION GROUND.</head>
        <p>NEW SCHOOL AND RECREATION GROUND.</p>
        <p>The Lovely-lane Scheme. Mr. Edgar Dudley, F. 5.1., an inspector of the Local Government Board, held an inquiry at the Warrington Town Hall on Thursday evening into the application of the Corporation to borrow £O,OOO for the purchase of land in Lovely-lane for the 'joint purposes of a recreation ground and the site of a public elementary school. There were present : Councillors C Jackson- Holmes and J Skelland, _Messrs J Lyon Whittle (town clerk), John Fairhurst (borough treasurer), J Houghton (chief clerk in the borough treasurer's department), T Longdin (Ir)rough surveyor), A ki Ker (deputy surveyor), and J Moore Murray (director of education).</p>
        <p>Ihe TOWN' CLERK explained that the district in question was very congested and there had been a strong desire on the part of the Council to acquire a recreation ground. So long ago as 1907 they approached Lord Newton to obtain a site. The negotiations fell through, but they were revived last year when the Education Committee received notice of the intention ot the Board of Education to close a school in the vicinity and they were compelled to look out for a site for a new school. The Education .Committee passed a resolution asking the Town Council to purchase sufficient of the Lovely-lane site for the purpose of a school, but the Council thought it advisable to secure the whole of the land (7 acres) giving two acres for the school and appropriating the remainder as a recreation ground. The scheme was then fully explained to the inspector. It was stated that the cost of purchase was made up as follows :—Recreation ground,4,oBl; fencing, £125 ; school site, £1,670; street improve- ment, £ll5. The Town Clerk pointed out that Lovely-lane, which was foimerly an old country lane, had developed into a main thoroughfare and it was proposed to widen that portion which ran parallel to the site from 401 t. to 501 t.</p>
        <p>There was no opposition to the application.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS. The Chief Constable loves a joke, and certainly raised a laugh in the Court Thursday when he described a girl chal with stealing as" the daughter of a butc and the black sheep of the family." 41 0</p>
        <p>* - "The flag under which the tariff reforn are sailing their ship is not a flag of p] perity at all, but the skull and cross-bon( popular and democratic starvation," was of the most striking declarations of Ramsay Macdonald, M.P., in his speed Warrington on Thursday.</p>
        <p>o 0 We note that the Bridges Committe, inviting tenders for the supply of timber for the foundation of suspension bridge p in Victoria Park, and the labour require( driving same. This looks more like prog with much-needed and long- talked-of sch( 21, * Councillor William John Wilson, of La ford, has been explaining to the Junior C servatives some of the difficulties of the I Party. One of them was that "almost eN dissenting place of worship in the town a Liberal Club." It is probably just as 1 to say that every Anglican Church is a 'I Club, and considerably more to the poin say that every public-house is a Tory C mittee room—particularly on election d</p>
        <p>* *" But the one method, which would lea 4 success, says Councillor Wilson, " be to get the boys into their own fold train them up in the way they should Fancy the Junior Conservative Club "fold " for training boys in the way t should go. Residents in the neighbour] of Orford-lane will be rubbing their eyes wondering if the Tory party has gc monopoly of halos, as well as Union Jack</p>
        <p>* * Words of wisdom also fell upon the of Junior Conservatives from the lip: Councillor Dr. McKee. His dictum was " Young men ought to be secured for the that would maintain and build up the Ea and drawn from the side that would tr, destroy it." This seems altogether admirable, if a 1 commonplace. But the Doctor would su be wiser to eschew politics and devote attention to the medical aspects of Nati Insurance. The Empire is not tottering * 5 There were several funny things during the debate on the magistrates' cIE salary. For instance Councillor Roberts that Nobody had a right to make money out of a lawyer's work except lawyer himself." It is a common belief nobody does, though there may be an imj injustice to the legal profession. As a: however, the lawyer may safely be lef look after himself. _* *_*</p>
        <p>The bon mot of the Town Council mee on Tuesday is debited to Counc Pemberton. In the course of a discussio remarked "a celebrated barrister once the law was a 'hass.'" This brought immediate correction from Counc Henshall that the phrase originated with a barrister but with "Mr. Weller, sl —as he humorously put it. Counc Pemberton at once bowed to sup( literary knowledge and joined in the gen guffaw!</p>
        <p>* * * Our Tramways' Committee are consi ing a report on the working of motor cm buses and trackless trams. It has struc that a good way of avoiding the he liability of re-laying the worst parts of existing track would be to macadami over and change the system to " trackh The idea may or may not be practicable it is worth consideration in view of the cost per mile for tram rails and track,</p>
        <p>* * Residents in Grappenhall, Thelwall, Latchford are much concerned at prospect of a is. Id. rate for the £lB, sewerage scheme projected by the R District Council for these three paris Rates have been low in the district and t is considerable diversity of opinion at thEf need for such an expensive scheme E for so important a matter as sewering. parish meeting on the 20th inst. will probl be lively.</p>
        <p>We are a little puzzled to know why magistrates have refused permission for picture palaces to introduce a song du the interval. These popular entertainn houses are entitled to broader treatn than public-houses licensed for the sal drink, and the singing of a song would su be the least harmful of innovations. Simil, "snooker-pool" is prohibited at the bill halls, though there are no such restrict at the clubs. If billiards, why not pi Public facilities for reasonable enjoymeni in danger of being too much restricted.</p>
        <p>0 « No more scathing condemnation of ti "reform," from the workingman's standp( has been heard in Warrington than ' which fell from the lips of Mr. [Ran Macdonald, M.P., the leader of the Lal Party, in his outspoken speech at Co-operative Hall, on Thursday evening. frankly declared that Protection would ( add to the miseries of the poor and poi] out that, on the evidence of a Gen delegate to the recent Labour Party C gress, the outcome of the policy in Germ —that paradise of the tariff " reformers had been "high rents, difficulties in rai wages, starvation and destitution." answer to a critically inclined member of audience, Mr. Ramsay Macdonald unhesi ingly justified the support which Lat members have given to Free Trade pointed out that tariff" reform" would c divert attention from the real sc grievance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>BUTTERMARKET STREET WIDENII</head>
        <p>BUTTERMARKET STREET WIDENII</p>
        <p>Arbitration Proceedings. Important arbitration proceedings in connec with property required by the Corporation for widening of Buttermarket-street were opened at Town Ilall on Wednesday morning. The claim are Mesars. William Richardson and Spe Wallhead (executors and trustees cf the will of late Mr. John Craik) who are jo!nt tenants Councillor J. G. Craik of the shops numbered 1' and 48, Buttermarket-street. They were represe by Dr. Atkinson, barrister, of Manchester (instru by Messrs. Wallhead and Son, solicitors). Gordon Ilewart, barrister' Manchester (instructed the Town Clerk, Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) appeared the Corporation.—The arbit, ators were Mr. T. Wilson, of Manchester, on behalf of the Corport and Mr. Henry Hartley, of Liverpool, on behalf o. claimants. Mr. J. 11. Daimon, A.M.1.C.E., of Hu&amp; field, acted as umpire. At the commencement of the proceedings Gordon Flewart said an application had been a for the admission of representatives of the Press. The Umpire said the mater had been considi and, in the interests of both pal ties, he thou&amp; advisable that the proceedings be conductedin pri Claimants' case was supported by Mr. Rol Garnett, Mr. John Mainwaling, Mr. Wallis, of 3 chester, and Mr. Sidnei., I,Vallhead. The witnesses for the Corporation were: Mr , Stephen Mr W II Woodcock (assistant overseer), A T Haflaway (deputy town clerk), Mr Ernest Gut and Mr Albeit Warburton. ,The proceedings extended over two . days and award will be made known in due course.</p>
      </div>
