        <p>LAWN MoNvErts ground by special machinery. Send ..vour machines now before the rush.—J. S. GARLAND, '*l3 Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. Lecture on India. On Tuesday evening, a very interesting lecture on " Our Indian EmDire " was given by Mr. Garison of the Colonial Institu•e, London, in the Co-operative Captain G. R. Crosfield presided over a fairly -Pod attendance, and the lecture, which was beauti- fully illustrated, was highly appreciated. 'Hank Quay Station Passenger Department. The annual dinner in connection with the above took place at the Winmarleigh Cafe on Saturday night, February 3rd, when 58 sat down to an excellent repast. " After dinner an excellent programme was Placed in the hands of Mr. Bolton (station master), presided. Songs were afterwards rende red Messrs Harrison, Hale, Creswell, Gordon, Seed, Gilks and Smith, all of which were very cordially received. Mr. Shaw was applauded for Ids recitation "Nomination Day," in Lancashire dialect. Mr. Sam Atikland proved the star of the evening with humorous ncinirts and sketches. yr„VActuAl CLEANERS, thoroughly reliable machines Bridge 375. 6cl.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmongers, at., Warrington.—Adyt. Warrington Womens Circle. On Tuesday night the members of the above ass- embled in large numbers in the Club Room Bridge- by invitation of Miss Lina Plinston, who, a I:cell:11)er herself generously provided all who came ha liberal supply of tea and cake, sandwiches etc: wcieh were done justice to by her numerous guests. A musical pogramme had also been prepared, which Nether with an interesting sketch, games etc: made die's° Pass quickly and pleasantly until the hour of Persing when after a hearty vote of thanks from presentathileeening to Miss Plinston and her willing helpers, was brought to a close. ExPlosion at Church Institute. The keen frost at the beginning of the week was l:elponsible for an explosion ot the Parish Church Institute off Manchester Road, on Monday evening. It 1 appears that at 4 30 in the afternoon the caretaker ighted the fire which supplies the pipes userl in the heating of the building. She then left and on her 'return an hour and balf later found that there had en be 1., an explosion. Singular to say comparatively been damage was done, the contents of the fire having neen blown across the room, scorching the cloth of the billiircl tables. Fortunately the building was not set on fire by the live coals which were scattered •,°°llt the roost. The explosion occured about an hour before the time fixed for the annual meeting of the Institute. Warrington Band of Hope Union. , The monthly meeting of the Temperance Guild was n.reld Cairo-street Schoolroom on Thursday evening all the unavoidable absence of Mrs. James Dodds President, Mr. Thomas Dunabin presided. The !32eaker of the evening was Mr. W. A. Batley, of ',..,danebester, schcol lecturer of the Lancashire and hesloire Band of Hope Union, who gave a most Instructive address upon the effects of alcohol as a Btimillant. A short programme of songs, recitations was given by Miss Eva Tanner, Mr. Elliott and thr• G. Whitfield, after which the young people enjoyed eclselves with games. These meetings supply a e,ng felt want for the senior Band of Hope members 1740 are engaged in business and are unable to meet with th. ho e various Bands of Hope owing to the early iirs at which these meetings are held to suit the :eni3ger members. Christ Church, Latchford. The annual parochial tetparty in connection with hrist Church, Latchford, was held on Thursday eYening. About 150 persons sat down to a substan- tial tea. the arrangements Li. which bad been carried by Miss Atkinson. The subsequent proceedings, ;which took the form of a concert, were presided over )Y the Rev. G. C. Briggs. The programme included , 1,--e,nge by Mrs. Briggs. Miss Adams, Mr. J. 13. Poole; •W. Whittle and Mr A. Grounds (humourists.) A t'etohtHjçj was given by the Mivses Adams, Lofthouse, .clever conlilring Dorothy Browne, and was much enjoyed. A exhibition by Mr. Lloyd proved a' ',Popular feature. The duties of accompanists were ably carried out by Mr. Fry and Miss Fairclough. The Rattonal Association Friendly Society. hot.POt supper and social in connection with this ,-;:eie.tY took place at Atkinson's Cafe on Thursday len,i9g. There was an attendance of 1".? and the proved a great success Mr. Councillor I,deusliall presided and the speakers included Mr. Day (of the Board of Management) and Mr. Fulton-Smith. vote of thanks to the Chairman and speakers was .11.1.°P,-,c)sEid Councillor Dr. McKee, and seconded by Everett. Mr. Joel Forster presided at the piano. ttj• Albert Wilde gave three excellent concertina t ;7" Mr. T. H. Elliott ably rendered the song "nen the ebb tile flows". Mr. W Farrington r;:eate,i oreat amusement in comic songs, and Mr tilirrell for also sang v.ry effectively. Letters of apology o.,,alobetice weft., lead from Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., .0. and his Worship the Mayor. 111:, Rabbitt. 41* J. Rahbitt took part last week at Llandudno in e Prier_rlferraances of "The Yeomen of the Guard" given socier, the auspices of the local Amateur Operatic Y. The Liandndno press highly praise his rrise_rPretation of the character of Colonel Fairfax. 'ere "Dire justified and Visitor" say s:—"The Society f .- 10 ully ustified in going even as far as Warrington ditheeenro the services of Mr J. Rabbitt to play the .4 role of Colonel Fairfax. In his first number, a,,edife a boon"? he at once won the admiration cf the tCienoe with his fine tenor voice, which be used t 3 etfullest advantage, and succeeded in evoking the encore of the evening. He was even more succe- for I° other number ''Free from his fetters grim" , "Ad Which he was again loudly recalled. " The petfo'ertiser" after referring to Mr. Babbitt's previous rtnance of the same character at the Grand, St sava • . 11 anything he was more convincing, perfectly he '4Be, and an actor with a love for the stage which S fitly adorns ". vci`t`hlTE3isnlt " CYCLRS.—Special lines at £5 10s. or ,ePeed gear, 20 153.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, Geneliridge St., Wstrington.—Advt. Th Pal Booth's Visit Postponed. e visit of General Booth to the Hippodrome, nwitl4 was announced for Sunday, February 11th, has, indee_ .t uteiy, unforeseen circumstances, been Postponed a which has caused a great amount of dis- st72intnaent locally. But one of the General's able wog'? opicers, who is specially set apart for evangelical t 7 le announced t ) conduct two popular services e IliPpodroms, which has been kindly lent by Mr. at en,eghten, Staidly afternoon and evening next, o'clock and 6.31 Brigadier Llewellyn how 'a.re.el of London, the officer referred to, is vi the town for a month's salvation crusade 212c-1-clready increased interest is being centred in the te 141 services the popular evangelist is conducting. Lis is Musical and an attractive and fluent speaker and res,,;:aoY and bright style has yielded successful tatiTie, in increased attendances and in enlisting siasm among his own people and practical 43. pathetic interest among the outsiders.</p>
