      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <p>Sergeant Barnett's Retirement. A respected police officer, in the person of Sergeant Thomas Barnett, has just retired after upwards of 23 years' approved serviee.— At a meeting of the Watch Committee, the police surgeon having certified that Sergeant Bardett was medically unfit f9r further police duty, and the Chief Constable having certified that the sergeants' service had been diligent and faithful within the meaning of the Police Act, 1890, it was resolved that there be paid out of the police pension fund to Mr. Barnett a pension for life equal to 26/30th8 of his annual pay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>" The Greatest Scoundrel Living."</p>
        <p>Warrington theatre-goers have had expetience of Mr. A.M. Loader's abilities as a playwright in short sketches and episodes, but this week they have had their first opportunity of seeing a fully-fledged drama from his pen. " The Greatest Scoundrel Living "is its promising title, and expectations of excitement and thrills are not disappointed. Mr. Loader does nothing by halves, and the drama is highly sensational throughout, with just those comedy touches which are necessary to give relief to the prevailing atmosphere of murder, romance and villainy unashamed. For the most important incidents of the play we are transported to Greece where the hero,the scoundrel and a minor third party fight desperately for the possession of hordes of treasure stored in the cellar`of a tumble-down cottage. Before that we have had the hero's escape from prison and execution by an ingenious ruse reminiscent of the Count of Monte Cristo. Unexpected situations succeed each other so rapidly that the audience is kept on tenter-hooks and everyone awaits with interest the unravelling of tho tangled skein of events. Mr. Arthur Robinson is a cool and calculating Percival Haines, the scoundrel, and, without indulging in over- exaggeration, he is able to arouse in the occu- pints of pit and gallery a whole-hearted detestation of himself and his deeply-laid schemes. It is indeed a convincing study which does him great credit. Mr. Wilfred Blair impersonates the hero- lover, Frank Fielding, with great earnestness and success, and Miss Mary Yorke is a general favourite as Rose Moulton. A capital comic element is pro- vided by Mr. Ernest Whitrnee (Tom Masters), Miss Winifred Barton (Bessie Bright) and Mr, Will Kirk (Sam Grub). A FLANAGAN REVIVAL OF "TWELFTH NIGHT" NEXT WEEK.</p>
        <p>The echo of Mr. Richard Flanagan's Shakespearean revival at the Queen's Theatre, Manchester, will again be heard in Warrington, next week at the Royal Court Theatre. " Twelfth Night" is coming as complete as when it was staged at the Queen's, Manchester, in January, 1910. It then had a marvellous run of over four months, during which period fully 200,000 people patronised the theatre. School days will be recalled next week with the enchanter's lyrics, which once in youth we learnt by heart. Who does not readily recall the opening melody : If music be the food of love, play on; Give me excess of it, that, surfeiting, The appetite may sicken, and so die. That strain again ! it Lad a dying fall : Oh, it came o'er my ear like the sweet sound That breathes upon a bank of violets, . .</p>
        <p>Stealing and giving odour ! And so the charm of " Twelfth Night " begins to work. Then we renew our acquaintance with the characters that are as famous almost as their author. Here is the love-sick Duke of Orsino, the tender Viola, the cold Olivia, the iisterou3 B•deh, the prankish Matia, and the vain Malvolio. But this is running on too far in anticipation. Let the play itself revive these memories.</p>
        <p>The company responsible for its production next week is remarkably strong. The principals are co tistes who have made reputations in hypercritical Manchester and all-that-matters London.</p>
        <p>They are given, with their parts, without further comment :—" Sir Toby Belch," Mr Ryder Boys; "Viola," Miss Ethel Griffies ; " Malvolio," Mr Wilfrid Beckwith ; "Olivia," Miss Ponsonby llartshorn ; "Maria," Miss Una Rashleigh. The pl.,y is mounted and produced as only Mr. Flanagan is capable of doing; the gorgeous scenes glowing with colour and variety fascinate the eye with the rmst beautiful and realistic pictures which, com- bined with tbe magnificent and costly costumes, form an ensemble of a most rare and delectable character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>MR. R. H. PEARSON'S CONCERT.</head>
        <p>MR. R. H. PEARSON'S CONCERT.</p>
        <p>Visit of Stella Carol.</p>
        <p>Mr. 11. H. Pearson's enterprise in arranging the high-class concert held in the Parr Hall on Wednes- day evening, did not meet with that recognition at the hands of hi 3 fellow-townspeople which it undoubtedly deserved. One wonders whether any- thing can dispel the local apathy in matters musical whicn so persistently damps the enthusiasm of those who seek to cater for refined artistic tastes. Seldim have provincial audiences the opportunity of hearing a stringer concert party than that which Mr. Pearson brought to Warrington on Wednesday, yet the Parr Hall was barely half-filled, the better-priced parts presenting a disappointing array of empty seats. However, the audience did its utmost to atone for numerical short-comings by discriminating appreciation. Chief interest was focussed on the first appearance in our town of Stella Carol, the young vocalist whose sudden rise to fame has created a sensation in the musical world. Gre t thinp,s were expected of her, and she completely captivated the assembly by the beautiful quality of her voice, its wonderful range, and the cultured and skilful way in which she uses it. There was no trace of self-consciousness or affectation, a choice variety of songs' being given with an ease and natural simplicity which were altogether charming. She had not the slightest difficulty in negotiating the high notes, while light runs and delicate trills were most artistically vocalised. 'lbis feature was particularly noticeable in " Couplets de Mysoli " (Pelicien Darid) and " Lo ! hear the gentle lark" (11281 top where the voice and the flute obbligati 'played in musicianly s.yle by Mr. HenryFransella) were heard alternately and in harmony, with eminently pleasing effect -in fact the flexible, flute-like tones of the voice bore such a marked resemblance to those of the instrument itself that in some passages the listener might have been pardoned for mistaking the one for the other. The Bach-Gounod setting of " Ave Maria" was interpreted with marked devotional feeling. Another rich treat was afforded by Mr. Fraser Gangs, a Scottish basso with a robust yet pirfectly controlled voice, and a p ,wer of vividly realizing the varying moods and expressions of a sombre, moving composition like " Ruhe meine Seele " (Strauss) and a cheery. breezy sea song like " The Admiral's Yarn " (Tinbens). The delightful abandon of Mr. Ganges's rendering of the latter item called forth vely coidial manifestations of pleasure, as did the stirring, martial swing of " The two grenadiers "(Schumann), and the quaint humour of " Green prow the rushes," given as an encore. Mai Dorothy Webster, contralto also shared largely in the honours of the evening. :She is the possessor of a voice of exceptional richness and power and, although her conception of " Mon coeur s'ouvre b ta voix ' (Saint-Saens) was not characterized by that dramatic intensity usually associated with the song, its relliement, correctness of det and lack of exaggeration stamped it ass distinctly artistic performance. Miss Webster was happier in her two later songs "Poppies for forgetting" (11, Coningsby Clarice) and I think (Guy d Hardelot) which were very beautifully given and evidenced the talent and temperament of the true artiste. Miss Lilian Foulis is a violinist of superior attainments, her fine technique exciting general admiration. Her rendering of" Souvenir de Moscou" (Median:ski) suffered by a certain coldness and restraint and the tone was occasionally a little rough. " Serenade Espagnole " (Streleeci-llenley) was very daintily and effectively played and con- stituted one of the gems of the evening. Mr. Clement Hatvey, who played the accompaniments with much taste and sympathy, also distinguished himself as a solo pianist, Ins virile and painstaking performance of " Polonaise in A Flat ' (Chopin) arousing a tumult of enthusiasm. As an encore piece he played Rachmaninoff's massive Prelude in C sharp minor in trasterly style.A further enjoyable touch of variety was imparted to the concert by Miss Amy Watts whose clever character recitals showed firstsclas elocutionary abilities and a rare sense of humour. It is a long titne since we heard anything more amusing than her description of the doings of the habitues of the theatre pit, and the extract from Mark Twain's " Innocents abroad" was given with that imperturb- able sang frold so typical of the American tourist.</p>
        <p>'LINSEED COMPOUND' with warm water is an excellent gargle for sore Th'oat, Colds, Coughs, &amp;.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>GOLDEN WEDDING.</head>
        <p>GOLDEN WEDDING.</p>
        <p>A very interesting event was celebrated in the village of Padgate on Tuesday last, when two of the oldest and most respected of its inhabitants (Mr. and Mrs. William Bennett, of Lyndhurst, Bruche Avenue), celebrated their Golden Wed- ding. Mr. Bennett .was born at Springfield, Pad- dington, on the 21st August, 1835, and has lived in the immediate neighbourhood all his life. His father, the late Mr. William Bennett, was left an orphan at an early age, and spent his youth in the village of Walton; and the family are very proud of the fact that he deposited his early savings in an unsuspected crevice in the Old Tom Paine's Bridge until they reached the sum of £2O, when he bought his first cow, commenced to trade in cattle, .and founded the extensive business which has been carried on by the family for three generations, and is now known all over the north of England. „Mrs.-Bennett is the eldest daughter of the late Mr. John Jackson, of Moore, and was born at the quaint old gabled farmhouse near Moore Station on the sth Octo- ber, 1832. They were married at the Parish Church, Warrington, on the 6th February, 1802, by Thackeray's model curate," the Rev. William Quekett, and we are informed that no wedding bells rang on that' occasion owing to the fact that the magnificent spire was then in process of erection, air'id that one of the guests at the wedding breakfast. was the late Mc. J. B. Edelst 11, the last Borough Reeve of Warring- ton, who presented the town with the clock which stood so long at Market Gate. On returning from</p>
        <p>their honeymoon, which was spent in London, Mr. and Mrs. Bennett settled down at Tidy Lane Farm, Padgate, subsequently removing to Bruche, where they spent the prime of their life, and afterwards to their present home at Lynd- hurst.</p>
        <p>They havc six children, two sons (of whom Alderman . Bennett is the eldest) and four daughters, and it is a source of great thankful- ness that, although five are married and there are nine grandchildren, the family circle is still unbroken. Most of them-Were able to be present at Tuesday's festiVities, and the guests included Mr. John Bennett, of Giange Mount, Padgate,, ho celebrated his golden wedding some three years ago, and is the eldest surviving member of the family; Mrs. Edwin Richardson (Mrs. Bennett's sister and bridesmaid); and Miss Andrews, a very old friend. The interest of the occasion was heightened by the fact that the youngest member of the family, the baby daugh- ter of Mr. and Mrs. Walter Stovin, of Dexthorpe, Lincolnshire, was baptised the same afternoon by the Rev. John Gunnell. Mr. and Mrs. Bennett were the recipients of numerous telegrams and letters of congratulation, al congratulatory address, and several handsome presents, including a silver centre piece from their friends and fellow-worshippers at the Pad- gate Wesleyan Chapel; and we are sure that our._ readers will join us in the hope that they may long be spared to enjoy the affection and esteem of all their friends and neighbours.</p>
        <p>genuine attempts to secure rights. All the time the strikes were going on the officials were getting paid while the men were out of work. He did not think the Bill eras a party measure, and everyone in that House should support it. (Applause.) Mr. W. WATSON (Soc., E. Birmingham), said the chief objection they had against the Bill was Clause 8 where it said that the men were to pay £5 and the masters to pry a fine not exceeding £250 in the event of the breaking of any of the agreements, lie could have agreed with most of the Bill if there had been a provision for gages to le increased on a sliding scale. It was quite right and just that the Arbitration Boards should be called in before a strike took place instead of waiting until afterwards. Criticising the tactics of the Opposition, the speaker said that Mr. Booar Law said absolutely nothing in two hours. (Opposition laughter.) He did not lay down a solitary word of policy. Mr. W. .J. WALKER (E., Bermondsey) said be rose with great pleasure to support the measure. Although he did not agree with the Bill in detail he was strongly in favour of the principle of arbitration in the settle- ment of all disputes. Strikes and so forth caused the greatest harm to everyone. The cause of them- was infinitesimal and the loss was so great. They caused untold harm and the people concerned—whichever way the decision went—had noteing to gain but everything to lose. When they went into the fieures, the annual waste on strikes skid lock-outs was appalling. There was another aspect : the dislocation of the works gave the foreign competitors the opportunity—for which they had been waiting for a long time—of getting into the home markets. The cones qnence was that the Britisher lost a vast amount of valuable business. It sees regrettable that while there was so much to be done for the benefit of the worker the Socialists were preaching that the strike was the panacea of all evils. The present labour troubles wee due to their sinister influence. Naturally those people objected to anything of a legislative character because it would men that the leaders would have to go out of the limelieht.Had a body of men the right to strike ? An individual had the right to withdraw his labour if be was not entirely satisfied. If a body of men simultaneously withdrew their labour what would the result be ? He maintained that no body of men, whatever their grievance was, had I the right to deprive any other person of his means of earning a (living. The Socialists objected to that conciliation scheme. They told them that their leaders would sell the men. He was exceedingly surprised at that because the men were not their own masters Mr. C. PLINSTON (C., Darliogton) said he did not think matters had reached such a state that we could not sleep in our beds for fear of what might happen before we awoke. He was doubtful whether anyone could disagree with the principle of arbitration. Before they adopted a scheme, however, they should endeavour to educate the different classes of workers as well as the musters, to accept the findings of the Courts of Arbitration. Continuing, the hon, member said that to his mind sympathetic strikes were totally unsympathetic. He would like to know if the members of the Arbitra- tion Boards would be paid or if their services would be voluntary. He believed, in conclusion, that the great solution of the question of the improvement of the worker would arrive when they adopted a different fiscal policy. (Applause). The PRIME MINISTER (Mr. E. Wynnialones) in rising to support the measure said he believed the Bill was a great step forward in social reform. In that measure they made every attempt to bring the worker and employers together before they declared a istrike. The difficulty in the pist had been that they I could not gat them together. There were men now I who were able to discuss questions at issue on level terms with the employers. (Hear, hear). If the men's representatives and the employers were to meet together and take ue the problems, strikes would be a thing of the past. Strikes were the only weapons in the bands of the workers at the present time. (Opposition" No's.") Under the Bill they asked fir public opinion to be brought to bear. Continuing, the Premier said that the member for East Belfast had asked why did they not give Ireland the benefits ? It was for a very simple reason mei that we that Ireland had been granted Home Rule in that House and she would be able to settle her disputes in a satisfactory way. Provision was made in the Bill for the inclusion of every trade if necessary, should disputes arise. The Opposition party had said that the only solution for the people's benefit was Tariff Reform. It was no good and they should not be hoodwinked. It wee absolutely wrong for the members of the Opposition to play with the vestion as taey did. Why did Tariff Reform net solve the problem in Pro- tectionist countries ? It hid been sail at the previous meeting that the press It Liberal legislation was bastard Secialism. He was of the opinion that Tariff Reform was besterd Toryis-n. (Laughter). It had been asserted that Socialists were the only party who had got a remedy, but how would the socialisation of the means of production out an end to disputes ? Mr. R. BRISCOE, (C., Hereford City), contended that there was nothing in tbe Bill to compel the disputants to give notice before declaring a strike or lock-out. Ile would vote against the Bill on the ground that it had been badly drafted and was utterly unworkable. The HOME SECRETARY (Hr. Mark Howarth) said it generally happened when they brought a Bill from that side of the House taey had the support of one party. On the present occasion they had a triple alliance against them. It was fortunate that the Socialist representatives in that House did not repre- sent the Labour Party of the country. It had been said that the cause of the unrest in the country was the broken promises of the Governmmt. (A VoICE One of the causes). It had also been said by one member that the 818 was Socialism but hearing low murmurs from the Socialistic members, he had substittrel the wards bastard S ocialism. (Laughter.) Continuing the Home Secretary stated that he was very careful when beibrought in that Bill not to hurt any party in that House. He had thought it to be very important and far above any ordinary discussions. Tao Government w odd have been prepared to accept some amendment of the Bill. Speaking of the member for Warrington (Mr. England), he would challenge him to mention any promises which the G overnment hid broken. He had said they had d elle nothing for the working class of the country. Did he oppose the Old Age Pensions Act : the Budget of 1908,the principle of the Insurance Bill? In regard to the charge of Sociolis is. he thought the Socialists had answered that for themselves. He had put the Bill before a pro ninent trade u sionist and he had assured him that the principles embodied Ira the Bill would find favour with most of the members of his society. Toe members of ths Arbitra- tion Boards would not receive any payment for their services. The Bill was not going to settle all the social problems before the coultry ; but it would make it compulsory so dismiss what was in dispute before a strike was declared.</p>
        <p>The Bill was passed on a division by 15 votes to 14. Next Tuesday evening the President of the Board of Education (Rev H Ogden) will introduce a Bill involv- irg drastic changes in the licensing laws.</p>
