      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>TRADES DISPUTES BILL PASSED.</p>
        <p>The debate on the Trades Disputes Bill introduced by the Home Secretary (Mr. M. Howarth) was con- cluded at the meeting of the Parliamentary Debating Society on Tuesday evening. Both sides were fairly represented and a good discussion ensued. Mr. P. DAVENPORT (C. West Salford) re-opened the Opposition attack by saying that the Bill as designed on paper, could not be put into praotice with success, if at all. The Chancellor of the Exchequer was highly indignant with that side of the House because they had dared to speak against the Bill. He had accused them of having a "Yes—No " policy—(hear,hear)--of having to say " Yes " because they dare not say " No," and vice- versa. They were in favour of the objects for which that Bill bad been designed, but they did not think it provided the means of bringing those objects into effect. In Use first place the Boards which would be set up throughout the Kingdom could not be applied to disputes in connection with a ganeral strike. In the case of the recent railway strike, which extended throughout the country, the question would, under the Bill, have come under four or five of the Arbitration Boaras at one time. Therefore, unless they all Came to the same conclusion it would result in there being mcre friction than before, because if under one of the Boards some of the employees had obtained better terms than others had under another Board, the latter would very likely strike until they got as good terms as the others. In the Bill there was no machinery provided for the election of Boards either for the men or for the masters, He could not sae how the workers could be represented on the Board. It was a question whether they would be able to get arade unionists and non-unionists to work together in the election of delegates. Then again the Government themselves appeared to think that the Bill would not bring abut the objects they desired because they knew that a settlement could net be arrived at without the expression Of public opinion. With regard to the suggested fine of £5 on workmen who broke agreements, It would be impossible to enforce it and the country could not afford to keep all who could not pay, in prison. (Laughter). Mr. G. TOULAI IN (L., Abingdon) said he thought the Bill would provide the trade unionists of the country with a means of working out their owe salvation. He referred to the customary attitude of the Tories of professing to agree with the principles of Liberal measures while criticisiog them in detail. He believed that certain prominent Tories did more to create a Socialistic feeling among the workers of the country than all the fiery talk about exploiter and exploited from membero of the Socialistic movement itself. Take the Tory party's attitude with reference to the TatY Vale judgment. They, under the leadership of Mr B dfour,ratified that daciaion which placed trade unions in a hopeless situation, and when the Trade Disputes Bill was passed under Sir Henry Campbell Bannerman 's administration, Lord Lansdowne would have liked the Lords to reject it, but be advised their lordships. that as the Bill was not a suitable ground for a quarrel with the Government they must therefore let it pass. Sir Frederick 13 inbury had tabled a bill only this year to repeal the Trades Disputes Act. The hostile attitude of certain prominent Tories who gave evidence before the Railway Commission of last year would leave an impression on the minds of railway workers that their directors were against them and that they were not likely to gain any- thing by peaceful methods. Ile for one thoaght that the majority of the members of the party opposite had surrendered to publio opinion in the matter of Trade Unionism. And the hon. member for Warrington was an example of that belief, The late Tory member for War- rington, he believed, held opposite views. That being an I established fact, the members opposite ought to support this bill, and make it possible for the workers to peacefully work out their own emancipation, and thereby make it impossible for the Social- ist agitators to appeal to the worst instincts of those whose conditions were not all that c euld be desired. The bill was an olive branch held out to both employer and employee, and the only alternative to it was, he believed, the condition of things that was over- taking Germany 'at the present time. Germany was, according to the tariff reformer, the heaven of the working man, but it was beooming the home of the Socialist ; two conditions which hardly coincided. They would soon see the German worker pointing a pistol at the bead of his employer and demanding his money or his life. They must mind they did not force the Enaliala workman to d 3 the same. (Applause), Mr. IL E. De WTHORNE (C., E. Belfast) said he did not think the Bill as it stood would fulfil the objeots for which it was intended. His first objection was that Ireland was left out. (Hear, hear.) It was not right that Ireland should ba left out of a provision that was for the benefit of the whole community. The Govern- ment had been about to establish Home Rule in Ireland for a long time but they did not know when it was going to come to pass. In the meantime, seeing that they had not done it, why not give the same oppor- tunities to Ireland as to England, Scotland and Wales a He would also liko to know why any trade should be left out of the soope of the measure ? The labourers who earned from 18s to 23a par week were left to look after themselves.</p>
        <p>Mr. ROONEY (L., Birkenhead) said that on the pria- ciple of every man minding his owa business, there was no necessity to include Ireland ia the Bill. Continuing, Mr. Rooney said they ware living in troublesome times. They were living in fear and trembling and they did not know the mordent whoa they would be plunvd into darkness. Tney did not know whether all the men might go away on 3 night and leave all the industries at a standotill. It was a serious state of affairs and they should not let it go on without some measure to stop it. The Government did not intend to take the masters' side any more than the men's. (Hear, hear.) They wanted to see fair, play for both sides. He believed in trade unionism and that every man should be protected. (Hear, hear.) If trouble arose between masters and men they had 'got to bring in some measure in order to alleviate the troubles and help them in their disputes. If the men ware to he " sacked " at a moment's notice they would revolt against the masters and would refuse to go. On the other hand why should the men leave the masters at a moment's notice? In a big Concern, where they ' had large contracts to work off, it was really serious when the men could put d )wn their tools and leave off for an indefinite period. (Hear, hear.) Everyone must suffer, not only the strikers but all people. (Hear, hear). He was of the opinion that a great many of the striker had been brought about by agitators. (Hear, hear.) Oa the other hand come of them hld been</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>MR. RAMSAY MACDONALD, M.P</head>
        <p>MR. RAMSAY MACDONALD, M.P</p>
        <p>Condemnation of Increased Armaments THE EVILS OF PROTECTION,</p>
        <p>Mr. Ramsay Macdonald, M.P., leader of the , Labour Party, addressed a large meeting in con- nection with the Independent Labour Party in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Thurs- day evening. Mr. J. rodmore, who presided, said there had been a remarkable growth in this country of the social conscienice, and nearly everyone was touched with the spirit of Socialism. • Mr. Ramsay Macdonald, referring to the pro- gress of the Socialist movement, said if Socialism ever came it would come by the steady change in public opinion influencing legislation, step by step, stage by stage. .it was the only way in, which secure gains were made by society. 'Socialism, propagating itself through the Independent ' Labour Party in the country, working through politics in conjunction with combined labour, was the faith and method by which the working classes were to be emancipated. 'Turning to the political situation, Mr. Macdonald said that in. the coming session the Labour Party was going to play, as usual, its steady, valuable part. It was not going to be hurried, cajoled, or misled. It knew what it wanted; it knew. what it could get, and it was going to do its best to get it. (Applruse.) With regard to foretgn polities, the general as- sumption of the man in the street who knew nothing about the Labour Party, was that they were nothing but a little, insignificant class party that thought about wages, hours, labour conditions, and nothing else. That conception of the Labour Party was grostesque id its imperfec- tion. There was no party in British politics to-day that had got a wider international outlook than the Labour Party. (Applause.) 'What party, for instance, had its international organisa- tion like theirs? They met the workmen Of France, Germany, Russia, Austria, Belgium, ' America and Japan in their ,international con- gresses; they passed their common resolutions% and in the times between those conferences they had their standing international committees that spread abroad information that was of common interest to all countries. " Who cheered the German Socialist victories the other flay?": asked Mr. Macdonald, " with the same enthusiasm that the Berlin working classes did? We did; you did; the Labour Party in this country did. (Applause.) Interest in foreign affairs is the very breath of our body. We cannot live with- out it. We don't deserve to exist without it. What we are going to say about foreign policy is this : That, as it has been conducted in your name during the last month or two, it has been an absolute disgrace to our country. ("Hear, hear.") What has happened? Yesterday we were told from Berlin that the German Reichstag is going to discuss proposals for INCREASES IN ARMAMENTS.</p>
        <p>Why? Because diplomacy has broken down." In a few months, continued Mr. Macdonald, they would be discussing estimates in the House of Commons providing for an increase in our arma- ments. They would have their Tory members and their Liberal Imperialists coming and telling them that as good, brave Britons they must be armed to the teeth, that " Britons never will be slaves'" to anybody—except to their next-door neighbour if he happened to be a capitalist. (Laughter.) They must continue '(they would be told) to build Dreadnoughts, to go on enlisting their men and, as one of their prominent War- rington citizens was always telling them, they must go in for universal conscription. What folly those people talked ! What political children they were ! What did it all mean? That they were conducting their foreign affairs in such a 'way that they failed, that they could not come to good understandings, that the reason upon which they prided themselves so much was not influenc- ing their foreign relationships, and that, as the sign, Seal and badge of their failure, they had to go on competing with Germany regarding armaments. That was nothing to boast of; it was something to hang their beads in shame about. It was an absolute confession of failure. The policy of armed aggression was good enough for Birmingham or Belfast—(latighter),—but it was not good enough for a civilized community. The Labour Party was not going to help dip- lomatists, ambassadors, foreign secretaries and permanent secretaries at the Foreign Office to go on repeating their failures., . They were going to put every obstacle they possibly could in their way, and when they found the people were sufficiently enlightened not to be carried off their feet by _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>IMPERIALIST BALDERDASH.</head>
        <p>IMPERIALIST BALDERDASH.</p>
        <p>they would take far more care to .settle their differences in an amicable, friendly and neigh- bourly sort, of spirit. An increase of armaments this year was absolutely inevitable;• and the Labour Party, probably with a mere fraction of enlightened Radical opinion with them, would have to lift up their voices in protest against those who, had necessitated the increase.</p>
        <p>" I hope Mr. Haldane's visit to Germany," said Mr. Macdonald, "-is really official, and that it is the beginning of a new chapter in our relations with that country. (Applause.) Al- though on many points of politics I fundamentally disagree with him, there is perhaps no man who is a member of the Liberal Party who would be more acceptable as an ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>AMBASSADOR OF PEACE</head>
        <p>AMBASSADOR OF PEACE</p>
        <p>in Berlin than Mr. Haldane, whose German con- nections, interests and enthusiasms are known to all his friends and to the public generally. But while we have our foreign relations con- I ducted in the shortsighted way in which they had been during the last six or seven months— Mr. Haldane or no Mr. Haldane—we will come to no arrangement with Gerrnany at all." Mr. Macdonald also touched upon the partition of Persia,- and said our policy in that connection would receive from the Labour Party as effective a challenge as they could possibly give it. The primary and central interest of the Labour Party, however, was the social condition of our people. Thanks to the Labour Party that had become the concern not merely of themselves, but of the Liberals and Tories as well. The work of Labour Party had been so fruitful that no politi- cian, whatever his party colour, could afford to overlook the interests which they had made popular. In a striking passage on the fiscal question, Mr. Macdonald referred to the pathetic posters of the out-of-work man and desolate home which were so prominent during the last election. The tariff reformer, aiming at big profits, high rents, the creation of trusts in everything that was of common use and consumption, desiring to put up prices under a protecting wall, knew perfectly well that every farthing he gave to the Tariff Reform League funds would come back, to him like</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>BREAD CAST UPON THE WATERS</head>
        <p>BREAD CAST UPON THE WATERS</p>
        <p>if the tariff reformers got a majority. He knew perfectly well that those subscriptions were not subscriptions at all, but investments. (Applause.) But in order to get their majorities, in order to touch the hearts and win the allegiance of the wage earners, they had to post those bills upon the walls and to persuade them that tariff retbrin, had got something to do with the poor man's misery and hopelessness. Yes, it had something ta do with them; it was going to make them both much worse. The most effective attack upon the policy of Protection that he (the speaker) ever heard, from the workingman's point of view, was delivered in Birmingham the other day by a German representative, who shewed conclusively by figures and facts that could not be, disputed that what Protection spelled out to the working classes in Germany was in- dustrial difficulty, high rents, difficulties in rais- ing, wages, starvation and destitution. Every time that the. trade union movement in Germany demanded higher wages, the ECOS'OMIC INFLUENCES OF PROTECTIOSI _ HAD TO BE FOUGHT, and fought bitterly. God knew they had great difficulties in raising wages in England. But give them Protection and they would have capital more organised, better protected in order to fight the worker; and better concentrated, and all the forces that now could be arrayed against them would be embattled with ten-fold more efficiency, and their worewould be harder, and their battle far more uncertain. MR. BONAR LAW made a very Original remark the other day when he said 'The road to hell is paved with good intsntions." (Laughter.) No party in the State it the present moment was more liberal in sup- plying paving stones for that place than .the</p>
        <p>tariff reformers. (Laughter.) He did not care what they intended to do, but he knew perfectly well that if they created a protective wall round the country, instead of making things better fOr the working classes, it would make them worse. As a matter of fact the flan°° under which the tariff 'reformers were 'ailing their ship was not a flag of prosperity at all but. the skull and crossbones of popular and democratic starvation. (Applause.) There was not a single Labour, Socialist, or working class party in the world, whatever its name, that was in favour of Pre- lection. Warrington, added Mr. Macdonald, was a typical industrial town, and as such ought to be °represented by a Labour member. (Applause.) Socialism wa not Midsummer madness, or a - humaiitarian emotion that entered their hearts as they sat contemplating the beatifies of the sunset. It was- a well-defined; economic creed with moral and political implications. As such, it was the ideal which they had placed before them, to guide and inspire them in their path through public life. In a passing allusion to the Insurance Act, Mr. Macdonald said they were told they were going to get r.iinepence for fourpence by it. They would do nothing of the kind. At the very best they would get 9d. for about did. When the Act came into operation they would have three con- tributing factors--the workman, the employer, and the taxpayer: The employer would immedi- ately begin to devise means for shifting his 'Ar- den on to the consuma, the workman would also begin to devise mans of fixing his burden on to profits—bigger increases of wages would be demanded on account of the insurance levy-----and the man who was paying his share of the State's contribution in the shape of direct taxation would do his best to shift that burden on to the man who paid through indirect taxation. The great weak- ness of political aetion was that, whilst they made reforms, the small class which' held econo- mic power could shift part of their burdens—not all of them—on to the shoulders of those who had libnefitted originally by the reform. - Mr. • Macdonald defended the policy of the Labour Party in loyally abiding by majority rule in Parliament and declining to degrade the House of Commons by rows and squabbles. Continuing,. he said that the very people who warned the coun- try against the "Red Peril " were prepared to go at by-elections and offer them the expenses of " Red Peril " candidates to split the vote, in order to get their man in. Nothing provoked them more than to find that all their best laid plans never came off. With regard to Mr. Bonar Law's assertion that the Labour Party was "IN THE POCKET OF THE GOVERN-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MET,"</head>
        <p>MET,"</p>
        <p>Mr. Macdonald said the Labour Party in the House of -Commons was as independent to-day as ever it was, and until it had converted either the Government or the Opposition to its own view, it would always remain as separate from them as it was- ever intended to be, or as it ever had been. The Government knew that they had no power over the Labour members, that they accepted none of their Whips, that they voted against them or for them .when it suited themselves. All parties in the House of Com- mons knew that Mere considerations of party allegiance had never ,brought the Labour Party into any lobby, but only considerations of politi- cal interest, and of how best to serve the people from whom they had received their mandate. (Applause.) Questions Questions were invited, and Mr. Macdonald was asked whether, in view of the fact that under both Free Trade and Tariff Reform the workers were exploited, members of the Labour Party were justified in going on to Free Trade plat- forms, asking the workers to support that policy any more than tariff reform? . Mr. Macdonald said the justification came in here :We were a Free Trade country, and if e began to tamper with Free Trade and experiment with tariff reform, it would land us, in a quarter of a century of that kind of experimenting, dur- ing which attention would be distracted from-the real grievance and the real root of the matter. They were in favour of Free Trade being main- tained, simply in order that attention might be concentrated upon what the real economic griev- ance was. Free Trade was also a sort of economic gateway to peace—far more more so than Pro- tection. (Applause.) Further questioned, Mr. Macdonald said there never was an alliance between the Labour Party and the Liberal Party, and there was no alliance to-day:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Lovers of a strong variety enteitainment are well satisfied with the fair provided at the Hippodrome during the week. Mete is a capital company of vaudeville entertainers and their efforts have called forth round after round of honest applause from the large houses which have assembled nightly, Florrie Groves and Co. present an exciting dramatic episode entitled "Thou shalt not steal," with great success. The story is of a cashier who has beea entiusted with a large sum of money and endeavours, without the knowledge of his trusting but nertous wife, to steal the money in the night when he believes her to be in bed. She has, in his absence, given shelter to a tramp who, on bearing a noise, comes ta the lady's assistance. The husband enters the room, and nnt knowing who he is, the tramp promptly shoots him with a revolver. The acting is very powerful and as Alice Beaumont,Miss Florrie Groves plays the part with much skill. The Five Bombaya scored in a sing- ing and dancing act in which they introduced several new and difficult steps. A feature of their entertain- ment is the shadow dancing which has a most pleasing effect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>NEXT WEEK, "THE BAD GIRL OF THE FAMILY." _ .</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK, "THE BAD GIRL OF THE FAMILY." _ .</p>
        <p>Next week's attracti-o-n--J—the.above theatre will be Frederick Melville's latest and greatest success---" The Bad Girl of the Family," which created such a sensation at the Aldsvych Theatre, London, W., where it played to crowded houses for over a year. Mr. Frederick Melville has given the playgoing public much to thank him f r, but in this effort he has excelled himself, which is saying a deal. The produc- tion is one of the most fascinating that has appeared at the theatre for a considerable period. The plot is cleverly conceived, and the situations interwoven with great cleverness, and there are not a few incidents that form a new feature in drama, the bedroom scenes calling for special mention. A rich vein of comedy runs through the piece, and it contains all the essentials of good drama without entering on the ridiculous side, and grips one from the rise to the hall of the curtain. ItJr. Frederick Melville is sending a strong company, headed by, Miss Florrie Kelsey, a 'dramatic artist well known to Warrington audiences, in the part of Bess Moore, the bad girl, supported by Mr. It. Marius St. John as Lieutenant R. Marsh, R.N.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>CROSFIELD OPERATIC SOCIETY. Next Week's Performances of " The</head>
        <p>CROSFIELD OPERATIC SOCIETY. Next Week's Performances of " The</p>
        <p>Mountebanks."</p>
        <p>The Crosfield Amateur Operatic Society, which has already raised about £4OO for local charities, will make its annual public appearances at the Parr Hall on Wednesday, Thursday, Friday and Saturday next when the original comic opera "The Mountebanks" (by Gilbert and Cellier) will be performed. Mr. Charles D. Law, who has gained such distinction in dramatic circles, has charge of the production which promises to be most successful. The opera differs considerably from" Lea Cloches de Corneville," which was given last year, as it has not as strong a dramatic element and calls for a greater degree of gaiety and abandon. The scene is laid in picturesque Sicily and the plot is conceived on Gilbert's usual whimsical lines. The music is a strong feature and Mr. Arthur Trow, who conducted last year, is responsible for this important side of the enterprise. The cast includes:— Miss C. Hastings, Miss C. Rigby, Miss I. Jenks, Miss M. Blackburn, Messrs. E. Walker, W. Tynan, J. E. Newns, If. Foden, S. Bowyer, A. Davis, T. Lawton, H. Griffiths anu C. Dagnall—most of whom have achieved successes in the society's previous produc- tions. The company and orchestra number over 80 performers. Friday will be a special gala night when the patrons will include the officers from Orford Barracks 'and the local Territorial battalion, Mr. Harold Sioltb, M.P., and Mr. A. H. Crosfield. The proceeds of the performances will be given to the' W,arrington Infirmary, the District Nursing Association and the Citizens' Guild of Help. Captain G. R. Crosfield is the vresident of the society, Mr. B. S. Tovvnrcre director and treasurer, and Mr. G. hon. secretary. LIPEED COMPOUND for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles. 91d., is. lid. Of Chemists.—Ad</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>.S *- A BREATHE-ABLE—MEDICINE IN HANDY TABLET FORM . FOR Coughs,Colds SoreThroat&amp;Bronchitis As a Peps tablet is dissolved in the mouth a powerful medicine is breathed • straight into the bronchial tubes and • • lungs. This novel remedy soothes in- fammation in the throat, loosens phlegm, stops coughing, destroys germs, makes breathing easy, and promptly banishes all chest soreness. '</p>
        <p>4</p>
        <p>ARMEU BURGLARS ARIL WARRINGTON. SENTENCES AT THE ASSIZES.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes on Monday, Thomas Kelly (32), a sailor, and Charier Arthur liornblower (21), a butcher, were charged with breaking into the booking o:tice of the Great Central Railway Station at St Helens and stealing 45 11 I,rl on the 9:h of December. Prisoners a imitted the offence. Kelly was further charged with oeing an habitual criminal. To this charge be pleaded n3t guilty. Mr. Linden Riley said two days before the offence to which he had just pleaded guilty prisoner had brokeninto two steam laundries at Southport and stolen money from each of them. Between the 6th and Bth of December he bought in Liverpool a revolver, and the methods he employed at St. Helens to gain the mastery for the time being of the hooking clerk at the Great Central Station included the me of a mask and a revolver. When he was arrested at Warrington he threw the revolver into the Mersey. Prisoner had landed at Harwich on the 28th of November. Within eight drys he had begun this run. The voyage which he had just completed commenced seven chys after he had come out of prison after serving a term of penal servitude. On November 9 prisoner wrote a letter from Valencia to a friend living in Warrington, in which he said : " You did not do right to me when I was in trouble in Prestos.. lam a wiser man now. I can always pick up something in these parts. lam now a wiser man. Last month I was in Algiers. There is a bit of life there. The Arabs sit at the tables with their women and wire, each man with a revolver in his hand. I was never in such a place for trafficking. You can sell anything, even ship's brass and tools." As far back as Jantury 13, 1891, prisoner was convicted for stealing, and ordered to be birched, and in the same month he was sent to pi ison for ten days and for three years to a reformatory. He had since been many times convicted Selecting only the most striking of his convictions, Mr Riley said prisoner was sentenced to three years' penal servitude at the Kendal Quarter Sessions, and on January 10th, 1909, in respect of four offences, he received four sentences of three months each to run consecutively, at the Wigan Quarter Sessions, and he had later received a sentence of three years' penal servitude. Between the first and last of these con- victions there were something like seventeen or eighteen offences against prisoner, mostly for stealing. He contended that prisoner had deliberately adopted the life of a criminal. Once or twice the p)lice had found prisoner work, but he had never kept to it, but had always left of his own account. Evidence supporting this statement was given by various witnesses. With reference to the theft from the Great Central booking office, Thomas Tierney, who said he was a porter at the station en the 9th December, gave evidence of the offence to which both prisoners had pleaded guilty. Kelly was wearing a handke•chief over his face. He presented a revolver, swung him off his chair, and tied Lim up with a rope. They then burst open the drawerr As they left, prisoner Kelly said if he made a sound within a quarter of AO hour he would he shot. Charles Edward Hornblower, brother of the prisoner Hornblower, said prisoner Kelly bad invited his brother to accompany him to a place where they could get plenty of money. He had only to show a certain thing to a certain person and he would go down on his knees. Witness attempted to persuade his brother not to go with Kelly, but he could not be dhsuaded. Mr. Elleringtoo, chief constable of St. Helens, was called and cross-examined by prisoner. In his replies he said he had given the detectives instructions to find out all they could to his advantage. He had tried to find prisoner work, but he would not keep it. During the cross-examination, prisoner said, " Dot.% laugh here, you are not in the local court now." At a later stage be asked, " Don't you at St. Helens try to be magistrate and solicitor as well as chief constable? The question was ruled out by the judge. Kelly was found guilt y.of being an habitual criminal. lie was sentenced to three years' penal servitude and five years' preventive detention. Hornblower was sent to gaol for six months on the charge of armed burglary. IHE BLACK SHEEP OF THE FAMILY.</p>
        <p>At the Warringlon Police Court on Wednesday, Maggie Hornung of Rusho!me-road, Manchester was charged on remand with stealing several pieces of imitation jewellery, a watch, and a blouse value in all 255. the property of her sister Lilian Hornung, 39, School Brow, Warrington. The. chief constable stated that the prisoner pleaded guilty to stealing the watch and chain but denied having stolen the other articles, She was the daughter of a butcher,and appeared to have been the black sheep of the family. She had been in prison a number of times and bad only been released recently. The matter was reported to the police and Detective Holland made inquiries and found prisoner bad visited the shop of Mr Tyrer, pawnbroker, Butt ermarket-street. The assistant refused to take the -watch in pledge.—Prisoner said she took the watch and chain hut the other articles were her own. The blouse was given to her by her sister. She bad taken the watch and chain to get money to go to Manchester. She was sent to prison for two months. KICKING A HORSE.—Cbarles Birchall, Hume-street, was summoned for having been guilty of cruelty to a horse by kicking it. Another carter who was present at the time when the alleged cruelty took place said he was working at Crovaeld's wharf on the 29th January. and saw defendant brutally kick the horse several times He nearly knocked the horse off its legs when it got up' Evidence was given by two other witnesses and prisoner who said the horse was stupid and not very strong, was fined 10s. and costs.</p>
