      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before J. W. Hayward (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst and Bolton, Messrs. S. Wright, W. Robert; W. Furness, 11. Thornton, G. Pilling and T Locker. _</p>
        <p>NEGLXI T OF FAMILY.—aObn William COOS, Irobley's Lodging House, Widnes, was charged with neglecting his wife and two children.—Mr. A. Browne, who appeared for the N.S.P.C.C., said the prisoner had left his wife on several occasions and bad worked on and ofL She had been in the position of having to keep herself and children on ss. Od. per week. The prisoner was arrested in a public house at Widnes on Saturday Eight. He asked for a free summons for desertion on ;behalf of the wife.—Prisoner who pleaded guilty was sent to prison for two months with hard labour and a free summons was granted the wife. WIFE NEGLECT.—Thomas Morris, 159, Hyde-street, South Shields, was charged with neglecting his wife and five children, on January sth.—.Prieoner, who was well dressed, was rent to prison for two months with hard labour.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Councillor If. Roberts (in the chair)' Councillor Jackson-Holmes and Mr. I'. Locker. Boys THRFT OF EGGS.—Henry Robinson, (18) 27, Ilarbauld-street, was charged with stealiag 25 eggs, value 2s. 7d., the property of Ellen Marshal', 7, Barbauld-street, on Sunday. Ho pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that the prisoner appeared to I have come to Warrington and had stopped at the model lodging-house with another youth, who had since got work and left the place. On Sunday afterncon on no less than five occasions the prisoner went to the shop and inquired the price of articles that were on sale there. He appeared to have stolen a few eggs each time and sold them for one penny each to the other inmates of the lodging-house. He had some remaining, which be gave away.—The case was adjourned for seven days' for inquiries to be made regarding the boy's mother, who it was stated was in Manchester. THURSDAY.—Before Mr A J Jolley (in the chair), Messrs W Roberts, E Woods and Dr Naden. DomriSTIC UNOAPFINESS.—John Rigby, 316, Knots- lord-road, was summoned by his wife Margaret Rigby, /or assault.—Sbe stated that her husband came home</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>DANT= IMMO is miol• Palm. DIMIIILOVIS RATS &amp; MICE War Astoria. SW&amp; IrksL. 21••• ?dm. LI. P, Pius Leo— DANYSZ VIRUS, Ltd., Box 216 IL 1•••••••1111•••• Larks. NAL ••• •8 Olimi•••</p>
        <p>on Tuesday under the influence of drink. He took the baby in his arms and sat down in a chair. On reaching for big pipe the baby slipped between his knees' and he closed them to try and save the child, with the result that its legs were broken. She said the accident was the result of drinking, and complained of the poverty prevalent in the home. He thereupon knocked her on the floor whilst she had the child in her arms, and gave her a black eye.—Prisoner said his wife had aggravated him.—Mr Wainwright (N.S.P.C.C.) having given evidence, prisoner was sent to prison for two months with hard labour.</p>
