      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>St. Paul's C LB.</head>
        <p>St. Paul's C LB.</p>
        <p>The monthly church parade in connection with this company took place on Sunday, when there was a muster of 52. The bugle band was in attendance. The Rev. J. H. Bankes preached, and simko upon the need for a greater observance of Sunday. The company officers present were Captain Hemhall and Lieutemnts Bunch and Ifenshall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>New Rectnr of Lowton.</head>
        <p>New Rectnr of Lowton.</p>
        <p>The Earl of Derby, as patron, has promoted the Rev C. Musgrave Brown, M.A., to the rectory of Lowtcn Newton-le-Willows, in succession to the late Rev Webster Hall, M.A. The new Rector of Lowtcn has been for the last fourteen years vicar of St. Clement 's Toxteth. Previous to his appointrcent to St. Clements he was from IE9I-3 vicar of St. Albsnw, Revinston. Liverpool. He was curate of St. Luke's, Liverpool, from 1589.91.</p>
        <p>Liverpool District Lancashire Congregational</p>
        <p>Union.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting was recently held in Wa vertree Church, when there was a large attendance of ministers and delegates. Mr. E. Sborrock Eccles J.P. (lluyton), treasurer of the district, presided, and presented the financial statement for the year, showing the income to be £450 13s 10—a slight decrease on the preceding ye.,r —and the grants to aided churches £523 10s. Mr J B Leach, J.P. (lluytcn), was Elected chairmen for the year ; Mr Eccles WbS re-elected treasurer ; and the Rev J Yonge (Warrington) secretary. The appointment of seven representatives on the Council of the Congregational Unica of Englsnd and Wales was made, consisting of the Hers G A Brock, G E Cheeseman, and J Yonge, and Messrs W 3 Boothroyd, J.P., J B Leach, 3.P., L B Phillips, and Colonel W W Pilkington, J.P., D.L. The Rev. A. J. Viner, secretary of the County Union, presented the scheme for raising £1,050 to discharge the debt of the union and provide for the development of the work among young people, and it was unani- mously resolved to commend this proposal to the churches of the district. The gratifying announce- ment was made that £2OO Lad already been promised by members of the County Union executive towards this object. Mr. E. Shorrock Eccles having started the movement with a gift of £25. The fund now stood at £377 without the issue of a public appeal.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. Mr. Boyle, of Widnes, presided at the meeting of this society on Sunday, when there was only a small attend- ance. He spoke of the possibilities each member had in helping to increase the membership of the society. It gave him much pleasure to be [present, as he lived in Warrington many years ago. Mr. Miller, of Widnes, gave an address upon Christ's words to the young man This do and thou shalt live." Mr. M. C. Eachern took ipart in the meeting. Mr. W. Parker sang " Calvary," and his efforts were much appreciated.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church. " Old-fashioned Methodist Services " were held at this Church on Sunday, when an augmented choir led the singing of a selection of old Methodist hymns to old Methodist tunes. Mr. F. Hickman presided at the organ, and an orchestra was in attendance. The preacher at the morning service was the Rev. Ebenezer Morgan, and at the evening service the Rev, Hugh NNilliamson• There was a good attendance at a Home Missionary meeting held in the- Bold-street Wesleyan Church, on Thursday evening. Mr W Clegg, who presided, said he could assure Mr. Collier that that pulpit had been occupied from time to time by many worthy men, but none more worthy than Mr. Collier himself. Speaking of the work of the church being carried on at all times he held that some of the churches seemed to hang up the key of their building on Sunday night as much as to say they did not need the church until the following Sunday morning. Continuing the Chairman said there was one great problem in social reform and that was child labour or blind alley employments and he horsd Mr Collier and his co-workers would find some solution. (Ap'plruse,) The Rev S F Collier, of Manchester, in an interesting address stated that during the last 10 years the sum of 250,C00 had been spent in home mission work. That was including everything, not only the central depart- ment but the things which appeared to be minor but which were of the:greatest importance. The Methodist denomination bad done a good work in tll parts and in all classes of people. Speaking on the question of child labour the rev, gentleman said the 20th century was the century for the child. The child question needed solving as soon as possible. The children of to-day were not drilled into a religious life as they were in bygone years. It was a matter for regret that so many children patrolled the streets at a late hour at night. It was the children who should be taught and the parents should look after their religious education more than was the case at present. (Applause. The rev, gentleman de ilt at some length with the Methodist Home Missions and at the close he was accorded as hearty vote of thanks.</p>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church. On Sunday night last Mr. Reid delivered his fourth address in the "Immortality " serle9. Ile spoke of rian's instinctive love of the individual life, of the more primitive and afterwards more advanced con- ception—in the course of his evolution—of the after life, of the certainty of an operative field for every existing faculty, and of the impossibility that God will fail to meet a faith which can rise so high. Mr. Reid proceeded to the conception of man's right to immortality on the basis of his relationship with God. He argued that the child, eepecially the weak or unfortunate one, has every right to the parents' best ministrations, and in the paternal relationship existing between God and those made in his own image, a relationship most clearly emphasised in the incarnation, God stands pledged to immortality, or He becomes inferior to His own creation,which, on the face of it is an impossibility. " Westminster Abbey and John Bunyan." The Rev. John Yonge, preaching at the Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sunday evening, on "West- minster Abbey and John Butuao," said that in his own day, which was the nth century, John Bunyan was deemed worthy of 12 years in the common gaol. Now in the 20th century, that judgment was reversed and cancelled by his memory, and his works being enshrined and illuminated with a memorial window in the greatest of national cathedrals, the historic Abbey of West- minster. Bunyan surely had a message for the present age. Doubtless he held and propounded many views of Scripture doctrine, which was now regarded as out-worn and even outgrown, but as certainly he also stood and suffered for matters of religious faith which the centuries had not effaced, but had even clarified and confirmed. If ' that new homage to his memory should call present day attention to his true faith and immortal message, if it should set people in quest, and in study of his " Pilgrim's Progress " and even his " Holy War," to say nothing of His Grace Abounding to the chief of sinners or others of his works, it would do much to stir the modern mind from a widespread spiritual lethargy and a frequently supercilious mental conceit, to startle conscience and to quicken the soul. The rev, gentleman then described the steps which led to the promotion of the memorial and also the service which took place at its dedication.</p>
        <p>He would like, he continued, to direct their earnest thought to the rich and pathetic significance of all that act, and speech, and united worship in the historic National Abbey Church of Westminster. Just dwell upon it for a moment or two. Archbishop, Dean, Clergy of the Established National Church, which in the end of the 17th century, scorned, persecuted and imprisoned John Bunyan in the common jail, as it also did many more in succeeding days, now joining with ministers and members of the Nonconformist Churches in a solemn service of praise and thanksgiving to God for John Bunyan and his many immortal writings. Was It not a most significant and a most eloquent event? Could they not see how a higher and broader and truer Christian faith was steadily developing ?—how it was breaking down barriers of explosion, bridging over ecclesiastical differences and bringing men of differing Christian communities into nearer relations and larger mutual understanding ? Was not all that full of promise and a sign and the dawn of a still better and happier day, when "Ephraim should not envy Judah nor Judah vex Ephraim?" He (the preacher) believed that that the Anglican Communion of the present day contained within its borders a steadily increas- ing number both of clergy and laity who were becoming tired and sick of the tyranny and narrowness of ecclesiastical exclusiveness, and were quickly "leavening the lump" of both Anglicanism and Non- conformity with simpler faith and broader charity! Surely it should become the filed policy of themselves, as Nonconformists, whilst sacrificing no vital principle, either of spiritual faith or of Ecclesiastical policy, to cherish and promote the entirely neighbourly and friendly and pacific spirit. As such a temperament extended in all the Free Churches towards the establish- ment, and its ceremonies and doctrines and its clergy and laity, so would the lhappier day be hastened in its advent. That Bunyan incident therefore, was a very considerable and significant "sign of the times " for which gratitude was due to Almlghty God, and they might hail it with most intense delight, and deeply ardent hopefulness. Dr. Clifford, in the close of his address at Westminster by no means had used language of exaggeration when he described that occasion as 'one of the great days in our national history."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>LIBERAL WOMEN.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WOMEN.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER SUCCESSFUL WARD SOCIAL.</p>
        <p>CRITICS OF THE DUMPING EXHIBITION</p>
        <p>There was another remarkably successful gathering of Liberal women in Bold-street Wesleyan schoolroom on Monday evening when the Women's Liberal Association held a social for its members in the Fair- field and Bewsey Wards. Nearly 400 sat down to tea which was followed by an excellent entertainment. In the absence of Mrs. Dodds, the chair was taken by Mrs. Edmund Osborne (hon. secretary of the associa- tion) and amongst others present were : Councillors J. T. T. Locker and J. Skelland, Mr. P. Peacock, Mr. J. Jarman and Mr. Harry New. Admirable arrange- ments had been made by Mrs. J. T. Locker and MIS. T. Pemberton (ward captains), and members of the committee. A highly interesting programme included sorgs by Miss O'Brien and Miss C. Wilkinson ; humorous recitaticin by Mrs. Barlow ; comic songsl by Mr. Nurse and a recital by the Misses Lomas and Ridgway. Miss Weaver played the accompaniments. The musical items were interspersed with speeches. Mrs. OsnottNE said she was sorry that Mrs. Dodds, who should have presided, had not recovered sufficiently from her accident to be present, but she wished her (Mrs Osborne) to convey her kindest regards and to svy that if it had been at all possible, she would have been with them. They all sincerely hoped that she would soon be well again. (Hear, hear.) Mrs. Dodds had received the following</p>
        <p>TELEGRAM FROM MR. CROSFIELD : " Deeply regret longstanding engagements prevent my wife and myself attending meeting to-night. Trust you have quite recovered from effects of accident. Best wishes for very pleasant and enthusi- astic meeting. Tories may scoff at cheapness, 'out this bitter weather reminds us the more forcibly how cruel and wicked it would be to pinch the people of food and clothing by taxation. Very cordial greet- ings to yourself and all friends in Bewsey and Fairfield Wards.—Crosfleld." (Applause.) They appreciated Mr. Crosfield's kindness in remembering them, and could assure him that when the time came for active work on his behalf the women of the Warrington Womerfa Liberal Association would be ready to do their share. (Applause.) Their membership was growing rapidly and, although they could not boast of a membership of 3,000, like a certain local League, they could boast, if need be, of showing their opponents how to do things properly. (Hear, hear.) All their ward teas had been splendidly attended end their latest venture—the Fancy Dress Ball and Carnival—was magnificent. She was told it was the finest fancy dress ball in the North of England. (Applause). Mr. JARM.'iN referred to the placard recently issued by a local newspaper describing certain political happenings as "A WHIRLWIND WEEK OF POLITICS." . . _</p>
        <p>He had not been able to discover that a whirlwind had ever done any good—in fact it sometimes did a tremendous amount of mischief. There had been a great deal of dust in Warrington and when they encountered it in the political world he would advise them to treat it as housewives generally treated dust in their houses—keep as much of it as possible outside and it any did manage to get inside, sweep it away at the earliest opportunity. Whirlwinds had a tendency to get bold of things—sometimes individuals—that were not very stable and whirl them round and round until they became quite giddy and did not know where they were. (Laughter.) Or people might be picked up, carried for some distance and eventually dropped. If the " whirlwind" was the best description the Tories could apply to the dumping exhibition and other recent effects, there was not much to be said f)r them. The dumping exhibition was supposed to have made a tremendous stir. Ile took an opportunity of attending it and of getting into conversation with some of the people there. He did not Corns, away converted—he did not expect to. (Laughter.) He could confirm one statement made in Mr. New's letter in the "Examiner" —that they could get a suit made in England for 251. and made under proper conditions of labour too. There was no neccessity to buy a German or any other foreign-made suit if they did not want it. He was told at the exhibition that when we got tariff reform we should keep such goods out of England altogether. Ile asked "How are you going to do it"? and the reply was "BY TAXING THEM." His informant added that a tax of ten per cent. all round would exclude the foreign articles and find employment for the workers at home. "But what about your revenue ?" he asked. "Oh, we shall get that just the same " was the answer. (Laughter.) He (Mr. Jarman) failed to see how they could have it both ways. He was also told that by keep- ing out cheap foreign goods they would enable the British manufacturer to capture the home market. The people of America had had experi- ence of "capturing the borne market "—it meant that having got rid of foreign competition, the home ' manufacturer was able to charge the people whatever I price was suitable to him and, having made his profit in that way, he cculd afford to sell more cheaply abroad, lie did not see how a policy of that kind would benefit us. There were other little theories about helping the Colonies by putting a small tax on butter, cheese, &amp;o. He wanted those present to realize that the effect would simply be that the prices of those goods would he raised. They could not tax articles without making them dearer and housewives must bear that fact in mind. Alluding to the Insur- ance Act, Mr. Jarman attributed Tory successes in several by-elections to misunderstandings and misrepresentations with regard to that measure. The Act did not mean that any thrifty man would have to pay one farthing more than be paid now. The man who was already paying more than 4d. a week to friendly societies could have his State insurance included in his present payments ; he would not have to pay twice over. The man who had hitherto been thriftless would now be compelled to make some pro- vision for his wife and family when he was sick. The contribution might in some cases mean a pot of beer, a cigar or packet of cigarettes less. The man would be bound to save the money, because it would be stopped out of his wages. The friendly societies would get hold of it</p>
        <p>BEFORE THE PUBLICAN GOT THE CHANCE and that would not be a bad thing. (Hear, hear.) Employers might talk about reducing hands and wages but he believed that when the scheme came into force they would find they could pay 3d. per week and still have something left in hand. Mr. ,Jarman concluded by urging the members of the association to help to keep the Liberal flag flying in Warrington. They had nothing in their cause to be ashamed of—in fact the present Government had a finer record than any which bad preceded it. (Applause.) Women had a great influence—as was evidenced by the anxiety of the Tories to capture them for tariff reform—and he urged them to exert it on the side of right, truth and justice, and therefore on the side of the Liberal party in Warring- ton./They could look forward with confidence to the nett election—which they hoped, for the sake of tbe country, would be long distant—believing that the result of the contest in Warrington would be far different from that of December, 1910. (Applause). Councillor LOCKER said it must be very encouraging to the officials of that section of the party to find the women so much interested in the work of Liberalism, as was proved by, the splendid attendance at that gather • ing. The Liberal party was doing a great work on behalf of those who had to struggle to hard in daily</p>
        <p>life for a mere existence. Their opponents had recently been exhibiting many of the products of foreign nations, hut they forgot to tell US what kind of" dumpers " we were. The exhibition was designed to show us the goods which were dumped into this country and which we should not purchase. If other countries looked in the same way upon the goods that we manufactured and said : "We will not have anything from you "it would be a sorry day for all of those present. In the Fairfield and Bewsey Wards were the two huge works of MESSRS. Rriasps EROS. LTD. AND THE PEA RSON AND KNOWLES COAL AND IRON, CO., LTD.</p>
        <p>He believed there was was not the slightest doubt that for every £lOO worth of goods which those works pro- duced for consumption in the United Kingdom, they send £3OO worth abroad. If we were to look so selfishly at the question and say : " No-cne shall send anything into this country" it would be a sore day for us if other nations turned round and said You shall not send anything into our , country." If we put a tax against the foreigners, they could easily raise their tariffs still higher against us. At the present time we were able to compete in all the markets of the world in a manner which was astounding to our rivals. (Applause.) With regard to the Insur- ance Act it had been represented in some quarters as a scheme to extort from the working classes money for which they were not going to receive a genuine benefit. With men of the type of the present Governwent at the helm, they might rest assured that the working classes would get value for whatever they had to pay. The fact that the employer had to contribute his share was a guarantee that the worker would receive a larger amount weekly than he was previously table to obtain in case of sickness. When the Workmen's Compensation Act was introduced, it was said that it would ruin trade, but that prophesy had been forgotten and to-day employers of labour willingly contributed their quota to accident funds wdich enabled injured workmen to obtain compensation during incapacity. The Insurance Act would be another great step in the direction of making homes happier and more comfortable. (Applause). Mr. IlAurtv NEW referred at the outset to the local aspect of the political situation. "We don't look a very gloomy section of people," he said. "We have pasred through a very serious and grave condition of things during the past few days in Warrington—in fact it is almost a privilege for some of you women to be here, because I hear there is a smashing process going on. (Laughter.) A threat has been made that someone is going to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <p>2/9 a box, 6 boxes, 13/9 ; of all chemists and stiires,or fro% FoBter-31cC1ellern Co., B,Welis St, Oxford St., Landon, 17. Don't ask for backache and kidney pills—ask distinctly for DOAN'S Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mr. Cawley had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>SMASH EVERY PREF. TRADER.</head>
        <p>SMASH EVERY PREF. TRADER.</p>
        <p>in Warrington. (Laughter). It was made at the dumping exhibition. (Laughter). Some of you look as 'if you will want some smashing." (Laughter; Continuing, Mr. New said it was a fine thing for the Liberal women to be carrying on the campaign, in view of the splendid signal which had been hoisted by Mr. Lloyd George—(load applause)—ho proclaimed to the financiers of the City of London that, in spiteef the criticisms and invective hurled against him, he was determined to be the banner bearer of progress and democracy and he would not lay down his armour until the fight was won. (Applause). It was going to be a stiff fight. They had a great programme which no other party dared to undertake and they bad had to face fierce opposition not only from the Conservative side of the House but from the House of Lords. The uncontrolled power of the latter Chamber was now,he was glad to say, a thing of the past. (Applause.) Referring to the Insurance Act, Mr. New said that any Government—whether Liberal, Tory, Labour or Socialist—which brought forward a measure for the emancipation of those who had to fight against the terrible scourge of consumption whilst contending with poverty in the home, deserved the hearty support of every right-thinking man and women. With regard to Home Rule, he asserted, as an individual and not as a representative of a party, that he would rather be beaten at the polls whilst fighting for the emancipation of the Irish people than win by adopting the opposite policy. Ile wanted to see the Irish free and independent and a great deal of unnecessary strife and expense saved by allowing the people of that land to govern their own local affairs. Men like Sir Edward Carson, well versed in the Jaw, were actually inciting people to shed blood, create riot and break up woeful meetings—and all the time professing to be lovers of free speech. The cry of separation was a bogey, for the supremacy of the Imperial Parliament would remain inviolate. Mr. Balfour recently di elated that the Liberal Party was in power because John Redmond, the dollar dictator, decreed that it should be so. Yet Mr. Balfour would never have been Prime Minister of England if it had not been for the Irish votes which supported him against the Liberal party when be assumed that office. Again speaking of the DUMPING EXHIBITION,</p>
        <p>Mr. New said its promoters preached that success was to be achieved by taxing the foreigner. Almost twenty per cent, of the houses in this constituency were practically slums and the very women who were sup- porting the policy of tariff reform, which meant taxing their commodities, were the very people who needed the support of the Liberal party most. The Liberals were undismayed by the attempts which their opponents were making to discourage them. They read that there were 6,000 members of the Tariff Reform League. Than why did they give them only 2,000 pies? (Laughter.) He supposed the other 4,000 had loaves and fishes. (Laughter.) It was rather significant that there were not 600 present at the first social in the Parr Hall, although two wards were combined. It was not only numbers that the Liberal party wanted, but real assets in the shape of people who were Liberals because they believed )n the cause. (Applause.) Flag-waving did not constitute patriotism and the people who were going to make the country greater were those who were working to make the lot of the poor brighter, and who were prepared to sacrifice something for others. (Applause). A hearty vote of thanks was passed to the artistes, on the proposition of Mr. P. PEACOCK, seconded by Councillor LouwErt.</p>
        <p>It was announced that a silver-mounted flower vase, presented by Councillor Jackson-Holmes in a competi- tion for the best ironed linen collar bad been won by Miss Willett, Dalton Bank. A prize of two pictures (given by Councillor Dickenson) for the best Ob. loaf was awarded to Mrs. D. Leishman, 15, Catherine-street.</p>
      </div>
