      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <p>Mr. J. Cawley, of 28, Lloyd-street, Warrington, says :—" My kidney trouble began with pains in!the back, and later I noticed that the kidney excretions were disordered and painful, containing gravel, and there was a frequent desire to relieve the bladder.</p>
        <p>"I hadirheumatism in both arms, and felt so helpless with it that I could not lift anything. I was always weary about the legs; my head ached badly at times, and the least exertion exhausted me.</p>
        <p>" Medical treatment did not give me relief, and then I used Doan's Backache Kidney Pills. lam thankful to say a short course of them put me right ; they made the water clear, and the pains gradually went away. Now I am absolutely cured. (Signed) James Cawley."</p>
        <p>g;II Be sure you get the sa</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>UNITED KINGDOM AND GERMANY. EXPORTS AND IMPORTS OF MANUFACTURES FOR LAST DECADE.</head>
        <p>THE UNITED KINGDOM AND GERMANY. EXPORTS AND IMPORTS OF MANUFACTURES FOR LAST DECADE.</p>
        <p>Recent economic controversies have created a growing interest in the comparative com- mercial progress of the international trade of different countries. The question to the front is, which is more advantageous to the general well-being of a nation—a policy of free im- ports or taxefl imports to " protect " in- dustry? It is much to be regretted that party politics have made it so difficult to locate the discussion in its proper sphere, rit., the •econotnic. Lord Wolmer seems to realise this when he remarks: "If Tariff Re- form had not been enunciated by the Conser- vatives the Liberals would have adopted it long ago." It would be just as appropriate to say that if Liberals had not become the party defenders of Free Trade many Conser- vatives would never have deserted the policy of Peel, Disraeli, and Salisbury. There may I be truth in both statements, and it should be the effort of those who discuss this problem to treat it entirely as an economic question, and to eliminate from the controversy political bias. •</p>
        <p>Protectionists advocate a scale of taxes on: imported goods, graduated on the principle that the goods which call forth most labour should be the most heavily " protected," the object being that in the international ex- change tariffs should be regulated so as to</p>
        <p>F</p>
        <p>oblige other countries to send raw material for manufactured goods. All Protectionist countries in the making of their tariffs have this in view. In the revisions of the German tariff, especially in that of 1906, this principle was closely followed'. Let us see from the figures available whether this tariff has accomplished its purpose of reducing the proportion of manufactured imports, and,. in contrast, whether this country, in adhering to the principle of free imports, is becoming (as prophesied by Mr. Chamberlain and others during the last eight years) so hampered by the tariffs of other countries as to be forced in its international exchange of goods to ex- port proportionately less Manufactured goods. In comparing our progress with that of Ger- many. we are putting ourselves against the best Protection can produce. Of the pro- tected countries Germany is making most pro- gress in export of manufactured goods. The accompanying diagram shows that progress made in the exports of manufactured goods during- the last decade by the United King-</p>
        <p>increased its proportion of exports of same, while German imports and exports. of .inanu- ::: factures occupy the same proportion as they did ten years ago. The high tariff wall has failed to protect. The difference is not great, ~...; but, according to Tariff Reformers, it ought / to be on the other side. It must appear 1.:... strange to such that an open door can keep„---.4'.' out manufactured goods as well or better :',.' ' than a tariff wall. I am well aware of the many other factors :.4 which influence the oversea trade of, a ~,4% oountry, and have no wish to press The de, *1 duction from the above figures too far. But in as far as the effects of the two. fiscal,, Policies are operative and deterniinative, the figures point clearly to the desirability of adhering to the polity of free imports. They show that Germany's new scientific tariff has . failed in its purpose, and, as in other pro- tective countries, the promises made and hopes entertained when the tariffs were intro- . &amp;iced have been unfulfilled. ~ - CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
