      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>• ANGLING.</head>
        <p>• ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ASSOCIATION (N.A. CENTRE). Angling last week end was a blank as far as the set board is concerned. We heard of several members who made the journey to the Gowy, but up to writing, there was nothing to report, the weather being so severe that even if they. had anything to register they would have been deserving of them. Up-to-date we have as many new members on the books as old ones, this being a sign that the member- ship is going to increase greatly this season. There have not been many old members for their cards as yet but taking the weather into consideration, and sport being so poor, we are not greatly surprised. No doubt they will call at the club house in time for the opening season, as a great majority of the Anglers do no winter fishing stall. Monday, February 12th,being a general meeting night as was stated about a fortnight ago the society is expecting a great number to be present. This being the first general meeting of the season. Much depends upon it to make the society a anemia for the year, and we expect to distribute all cards for 1911 as we are assured they will have reached the Secretary by that time..</p>
        <p>We hear rumours of other clubs wishing to affiliate to the Northern Anglers. If there is any truth in this, so much the better for the Warrington centre. The larger the membership the better facilities can be given. The advantages offered are quite within reach of all workingmen anglers. Members pions° note th..t the report of this society will be found every Saturday in the Warrington "Examiner." A. S.</p>
        <p>!The-</p>
        <p>AUSTRALIAN MUTUAL PROVIDENT SOCIETY.—Tho London Office (37, Threadneedle-street, London, E.C.) has received advice by cable that the new business transacted in 1911 in the ordinary department exceeded £6,300,000. This constitutes a new record for the Society. The business is confined to Australia, New Zealand and the United Kingdom.</p>
