        <p>• clout and Germany. To assist the eye in the comparison, I. have pushed up the German index column on the right so that the line start together at 1901. It is right- to say that the classification in different countries, although similar, is not identical, but- each is consistent with itself: that is during the period named (while there have been a few minor changes) goods. called " manufactured " at the .beginning of the decade. are practically the same. at the end. For our purpose, therefore, the rate of pro- gress is .correctly . illustrated .by the lines in the diagram. It will be seen in the earlier. years of the decade the progress of Germany was slightly more rapid than Our own.; but, significant to note, after 1906, the date of the last scientific tariff, when Germany ought, according' to Tariff Reformers, to .htiVe advanced more raiNdly, has fallen behind in confirmation of the .expectation f Fret" Trad'ers. •</p>
