        <head>'URES OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>'URES OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>ROYAL THANKSGIVING AT ST. PAUL'S CATHEDRAL. c. nesday the King and Queen went to Canons, the Cathedral clergy, and the Lord Army, and various public bodies. Our picture st ity te attend a Service of Thanksgiving Mayor and Sheriffs of London. Inside .the shows their Majesties leaving St. Paul's. The kaje,t; 4111's for their safe return. Their great building there was a congregation of King and Queen have taken their scats, and 'es were greeted all along the route by a 5,000, which was representative of both Houses Princess Mary and the Prince of Wales are cei.yed °f t ashouting and cheers. They were re- of Parliament, the Diplomatic Body, the Navy, entering the carriage.. the ilk Cathedral steps by the Dean and çp CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>" HOW DO YOU DO?" OR TO THAT EFFECT. Rudyard Kipling sang his song to tell us that" East is East and West is West, and never the twain shall meet, "by which he meant that Eastern people and Western people are so different that they don't under- stand each other. There is much truth in this, and we see the differences in the little things of life as well as in the big things. There are many illustrations in the Bible. When Pharoah asked Jacob "How old are thou ? "He replied "the days of the years to my pilgrimage are three score years and ten, few and evil have been the days of thy servant" &amp;c. What Englishman would have answered like that? It takes a long time to ask a question and get an answer in the East and a longer time still to go through the formalities of a greeting. That is why Jesus told his disciples to salute no man by the way. It was because they had an urgent work and must not waste their moments.To show what "saluting by the way "is like, I am going to describe the conversation which takes place when two Eastern men meet in the street. "Peace be with you this morning." "And with you be peace." "How do you do ? " "Without any ill." "Are you well ?" "Thank Allah 1" "And is your health good?" " It is good." "And have you no ill in your body ? " "I have none." "And your bones, how are they ?" "They are indeed strong." "And your little bones ? " "There is no ill to them." "And the marrow in your bones, is it well ? " "It is well." "And your limbs, are they well ? " "They are sound, praise be the Prophet." "And the whole of your body, is it well?" "It is well." "And your forehead, how is it this morning ?" "By your life, truly it is well." "And how is your nose?" " It is free from harm, I am grateful to you. " And your ears, are they well ?" "They are well, may the Prophet be blessed." And so on, until almost every part of the human body has been alluded to. Just think how full of contempt these folks feel when they hear Westerners saying "Hello old man! How do? Going strong —that's right." They think it is unbecoming and irreverent and have not any words strong enough to express their disdain.</p>
        <p>THE KING AND QUEEN'S RETURN. l'heir A Itt tpo lajesties landed from the " Medina " had entered the Palace, the thousands of ond,,rtsrneuth on Monday, and proceeded to people who had assembled in front, cheered and erowds: Where, despite the intense cold, vast sang " God Save the King" and "Home, tueiti gathered to welcome them. Outside Sweet Home." This brought their Majesties blarkalirlm Palace there was a scene of re• to the balcony, where they again acknowledged e enthusiasm. After tire King and Queen the greeting of their subjects.</p>
