      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>ROMANCE OF THE BRIDGEWATER CANAL</head>
        <p>THE ROMANCE OF THE BRIDGEWATER CANAL</p>
        <p>LECTURE TO THE WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>By Mr. A. MOUNFIELD.</p>
        <p>I think that in my choice of title for the present lecture I have already awakened some little interest in the minds of my friends and fellow members of the Warrington Society.lt remains for me to show that in the most tame and tranquil object in our local landscape there may be unsuspected interest and possibly real romance. It is, of course, of the conception and making of the Canal that I shall chiefly speak, and the first convincing thing I have to say upon the point of romance is that if Francis, the second Duke of Bridgewater, had not been jilted in his youth, there is no probability that the Bridgewater Canal would ever have been made. In taking to canal making as an alternative to matrimony— assuming, of course, that the alternative must be taken—the Duke made an admirable choice. So at least I hope to show.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>FRANCIS, DUKE OF BRIDGEWATER. _</head>
        <p>FRANCIS, DUKE OF BRIDGEWATER. _</p>
        <p>Suffer me first to give some necessary, though not very entertaining details, as I put our hero upon the stage. Francis. the second Duke of Bridgewater, was the fifth and youngest son of the third Earl and first Duke, and was born in 1736. His early career took an unusual turn. His father died before he had reached his sixth birthday. His mother soon married again, and seems to have contrived to forget her family. The children were all delicate, and consumption claimed them so rapidly that at the age of 12 Francis had succeeded to the estate and title. There seemed so little likelihood that he would live to mature years that steps were taken to set him aside in favour of the next heir. He received little education and grew up without home guidance or discipline. But after his seventeenth birthday had passed, his guardians seem to have realized that he was not destined to follow his brothers to an early grave, and began to atone for past neglect by sending him upon an educational tour abroad. They made a wise choice of companion in selecting Robert Wood, a traveller who hid written upon architectural subjects, and the journey seems to have had a stimulating and broadening effect. The outstanding point of interest in the tour is that it took the Duke to the South of France where he saw the canal of Languedoc, and watched the process of an experiment in inland navigation; but whether immediate interest was stirred there is nothing to show. His life on his return ran in the grooves that were common to the eighteenth century aristocracy. He had his London house, and his racing stud at Newmarket. Sometimes he rode himself, though his friends discouraged him on the ground that they feared his wispy figure would be blown from the horse, like a straw before the wind.</p>
        <p>It will be safe to make the comment that these are things which "eminent manufac- turers" don't do to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>DUKES' LOVE AFFAIR.</head>
        <p>THE DUKES' LOVE AFFAIR.</p>
        <p>It is not surprising that a young duke of abundant leisure should have time to fall in love. He fell in love once and for all. It was the first time and it was the last. The young lady was Miss Elizabeth Gunning, the youngest and most beautitul of three extraordinary sisters. This maiden had already given a thrill to soceity by permitting herself to be carried away from a ball by the Duke of Hamilton and married at midnight with a ring taken from a bed curtain. She was now a merry widow with strings of lovers in her train. Nor were the lives of her sisters less free from adventure, and Walpole declared that he would not venture to marry any of them for thirty years" for fear of being shuffled out of the world prematurely to make room for the rest of their adventures." Arrangements were in progress for the wedding and the day was fixed, but unhappy rumours began to reach the Duke concerning the conduct of Lady Coventry, the eldest sister of the bride. The Duke, whatever his weaknesses, took the best view of his position, and asked the Duchess to cease further intercourse with a sister who, to put it in the most indulgent terms, was much more attractive than good. Elizabeth's standards were little higher than her sisters. She answered pettishly, and broke the engagement.</p>
        <p>Many striking things marked his apprentice life, and he quickly showed himself the superior not only of his fellows but of his master and when at the age of 26 he set up without capital as a master-millwright it was felt that he would make his way. But there were many barriers. He could derive little help from books, for he could scarcely read; he must do everything himself, even to the felling of his own timber, and he had no means wherewith to travel and learn.</p>
        <p>It would seem sometimes as though Nature gives us object lessons to show what she can do without the aid of Art. Such an object lesson was James Brindley. This boorish youth, who could never learn to read or spell, was to startle the world by joining Manchester to Liverpool, the Mersey to the Severn ; by the cutting of hundreds of miles of water channels that turned com- merce into new channels and stirred it to fresh and vigorous life: _</p>
        <p>But canals- were not his first study. Water mills, grinding mills, and pumping gear engaged him. Then it was the steam engine, just growing to usefulness under the hands of James Watt. But the steam engine he made, with its wooden cylinder, was a sore " werrit " to him, and he wrote in his pocket book after its second failure: "To run about a drinking is. ficl." Possibly this was for his own private warning, and the eighteenpence is underlined as though to con- vey to his sober moments that giving way to despair was a bad thing financially.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>TURNING POINT.</head>
        <p>THE TURNING POINT.</p>
        <p>The Duke gave no clueto his feelings in any writing that has come down to us, but if we are to judge from his conduct, the event put a line of cleavage through his life. Henceforward he is a man of business with a mind full of commercial affairs, and the pro- blem that first claims him is that of getting coals cheaply into Manchester. At Worsley he has an estate under which are promising coal seams. Manchester is a growing town, yet so execrable are the roads, that coal can only be carried on pack horses, and the carriage costs as much as the coal. It is true there is a Mersey Navigation Company, which employs men to drag boats along the Mersey and Irwell, but it is a sleepy and stupid company,and its charge to carry coals for five miles is 3s. 43. per ton.</p>
        <p>MEN AND THINGS IN MANCHESTER. We shall best understand the Duke and his scheme if we take a journey into the past,,and survey the Manchester of a hundred and fifty years ago.. There is no better way of doing this than by dipping into the pages of our own Dr. Aikin whose "Forty miles round Manchester" is such a fine storehouse of local eighteenth century facts. The town with its 20,000 inhabitants had built up a fair business in coarse cottons and fustians. The spinning jenny had not arrived, and the work was done upon the spinning wheel. Not a single factory chimney reared its head to pollute the air,and fishes swain unpoisoned in the Irwell. Pack horses travelled the narrow muddy roads and kept up the only communication with the outside business world. A stage coach ran to London three times a week taking 4 days for the journey, but there were few roads •in the country upon which a coach could run, and certainly very fe'w in Lancashire. _ THE "EMINENT MANUFACTURERS." . . .__</p>
        <p>Dr. Aikin's sketches of the beginnings of Manchester's commercial life are very enter- taining. "An eminent manufacturer" he says "used to be in his warehouse before six in the morning accompanied by his children and apprentices. At seven they all came in to breakfast, which consisted of one large dish of water porridge, made of oatmeal, water and a little salt, boiled thick and poured into .a dish. At the side was a pan or basin of milk, and the master and apprentices, each with a wooden spoon in his hand, without loss of time, dipped into the same dish, and thence into the milk-pan and as soon as it was finished they all returned to their work,"</p>
        <p>I WILL GIVE YOU 6d. GENUINE TESTIMONY. DIMS LILY ELSIE writes: "It is excellent and most beautifully perfumed, I shall always use it." MISS ELLEN. TERRY writes:</p>
        <p>" It is excellent." • MISS IRENE VANBRUGH writes: " Quite excellent and most pleasant to use." MISS JULIA NEILSON writes " I like it very much." MISS FANNY BROUGH Writes: "Most excellent. Delightfully refreshing." MISS GLADYS HUXLEY Writes: ' It is the BEST I have EVER used." NOTHING need be added to convince you that THE ELSINE SKIN FOOD COMPLEXION CREAM Is THE BEST. It thoroughly Cleanses the Skin of ALL Blemishes, Softens, Beautifies and Preser aes. Price 2s. poet free, or cut out this advt., send with P.O. is. 6d. and get the sixpence as offered above. Sole Proprietor_ ROBERTS, PHARMACIST, FOWY, CORENWA LI</p>
        <p>IRISH PRESBYTERIANS AND HOME RULE. Some of the speakers at the convention of Irish Presbyterians in Belfast the other day, purported to base their opposition to Home Rule on social and economic rather than religious grounds, as the pur- pose of the meeting was to send forth an appeal to the Nonconformists of Great Britain. The orators doubtless conceived that it was more judicious to try and ofkr arguments not wholly b Iliad on religious rancour and bigotry. Thus the Rev. Dr. Prenter treated the question largely from the purely political standpoint, The Union has brought progpenty and contentment, Home Rule will bring beggary and strife —that in substance was the argument of this reverend gentleman. Dr. Prenter and his colleagues assume that the Non conformists of Great Britain are entirely ignorant of the elementary facts of Irish history. That is not good electioneering. " Home Rule ' we are told " stands for mere shadow and sentiment, the Union stands for substance and solid sense " and it appears that " a whole century of national peace has been the priceless gift of the Union. Even in the Ulster Hall this statement must have been heard with om e amazement. To glore over the facts of the Irish National Movement during the last hundred years might have been psrmitted to a champion of the Union, but it was msrely childish to I ignore these facts and to state as a fact that which the history of Irish affliections since the Union belies. 1 During the period in which Ireland has enjoyed " civil peace "the population of the country has been reduced from eight to four millions, two million people have died from successive famines, trade industries have decayed, and in agitation after agitation, thousands of Irishmen—Catholic and Protestant alike in their creed—have suffered imprisonment or death 1 in asserting their principles and securing rights now admitted by all parties. In such assemblies as that where Dr. Prenter orated, the definition of civil peace is very wide indeed. THE VOICE OF THE BIGOT.</p>
        <p>But despite the attempt to formulate a reasoned objection to the demand of the mass of the Irish people,the voice issuing from the Presbyterian conven- tion is the voice of the bigot. The resolution gives us the true measure and quality of the protest. It states: " Under Home Rule as foreshadowed, the Parliament and the Executive alike are certain to be controlled by a majority subject to the direction of the authors of the Ne temero and Motu Propria decrees, against whose domination all safeguards designed for the protection of the Protestant minority would be wholly valueless." By such appeal, the Englishmen, Scotch- men and Welshmen who are addressed as "fellow Nonconformists" are to be captured, and the work of the Nonconformist Unionist Association of England speeded But there is small hope in the breasts of Irish Unionists that the movement will seriously affect the body of opinion which was ranged on the side of Plime Rule twenty-six years ago and which is still solid in her cause. "In face" says an Orange Journal' of Protestant unity in Ireland against the separatist aims of the Nationalist Party, it is no wonder that Irish Protestants resent the attitude of English Nonconformists and deplore their lack of sympathy with them in this crisis." Mr. J. MacVeagh, M.P., has shown why the Nonconformists of Great Britain are free to declare for justice, while Irish Presbyterian clergy have so little of the spirit of Non- conformity as to give their continuous support to sectarian ascendency. They received as compensation for Disestablishment no less than £709,597, which , was forthwith invested in Irish land securities, and their opposition to Home Rule is motived by the same interests as is the opposition of the parson of the Established Church and the landlords of Irelani. . _</p>
        <p>Anyway we think the Irish people MighiTaTe.been spared the professions of anxiety contained in this resolution and in the speeches. The gentlemen that predict a regime of religous intolerance speak of their anxiety to" co-operate with Irishmen of every creed on the advancement of the social, moral and intellectual prosperity of our common country. We may as well ask quite frankly where is the evidence of that desire in the proceedings we .are passing under review? Over the principal meeting the Right Honourable Thomas Sinclair presided and in his speech he flourished the sentiment just quoted. But the same right honourable gentleman was closely allied with the Marquis of Londonderry in formulating the edict against free speech in Belfast. Where does the amity, co-opera- tion, S:c ,come in ?Not only the individual right honour- able gentleman just mentioned, but also the members of the Presbyteries have on this occasion associated themselves with the boycotting intimidators of Belfast, and in that city have stood by and backed up Privy Councillors who in their unimaginative insolence transgress every law of decent public con- duct. The speeches of the Convention we examined very closely, and it is clear to us that they are calcu- lated only to fan the fires of bigotry lighted by Lord Londonderry and Sir Edward Carson when appeal was made to the Orange mob "to take steps to prevent " Mr. Churchill's meeting. This is the real significance of the Presbyterian protest, and it will not be lost on the intelligence and insight of. the English Nonconformists to whom it is chiefly addressed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>FRUITS OF OPULENCE.</head>
        <p>THE FRUITS OF OPULENCE.</p>
        <p>Such plodding labour and such frugality could only end in prosperity as anybody could have foreseen, and then Dr. Aikin has the unhappy task of sketching the terrors and perils of opulence. These are his words relating to the year 1758: "An evening club of the most opulent manufacturers was established at which the expenses of each person were fixed at four- pence halfpenny—fourpence for ale and a halfpenny for tobacco." This surely was "opulence" run mad, and one wonders whether the voice of the</p>
        <p>prophet was entirely silent. But this was not all. A year or, two more and a dancing assembly was held once a week in a room in King-street at a cost to members of half-a- crown the season.</p>
        <p>Prophetic voices must surelyhave dragged in comparisons with ancient Rome</p>
        <p>COAL FOR MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>Yet in winter this opulent Manchester shivered for want of coal. There was coal for the million at Worsley, but winter roads were knee-deep in mud and the water-flow of the rivers debarred the passage of the wretched hand-pulled little boats...</p>
        <p>The Duke's scheme was as brilliant as it was commendable. He proposed to form a canal from his coal workings at Worsley, drop it by steps into the Irwell, and convey barges to a dock made in Manchester. The idea was not a new one for the Duke's father had secured an Act of Parliament to permit him to make navigable for the same purpose the Worsley brook, but the scheme remained on paper until someone arose with genius enough to make it a reality. The genius was waiting, and it was the Duke's good fortune to be brought into contact with the owner of that genius at his moment of need. Now we bring our second actor to the stage, the imcomparable James Brindley. BRINDLEY THE EXTRAORDINARY.</p>
        <p>Brindley began his career in a tiny hamlet in Peakland, in 1716 and grew up an uninstructed youth. Apprenticed to a mill- wright it was only the necessities of trade that caused him to teach himself to write.</p>
