        <p>• JAMES BRINDLEY, Engineer of the Bridgewater Canal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>AN HISTORIC FRIENDSHIP.</head>
        <p>AN HISTORIC FRIENDSHIP.</p>
        <p>Such was the man who was -brought to Worsley to discuss canals with the Duke of Bridgewater. Some painter ought to make it his subject ;for I think no friendship has so powerfully affected the corner of the world in which we live. Brindley was an oddity, so also was the Duke. Neither man—if I may coin a word in such an august assembly —was classifiable in the ordinary way. The Duke had schemes and he had money. Brindley had neither, nor had he scholar- ships but he had genius.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>_BRINDLEY'S SCHEME.</head>
        <p>_BRINDLEY'S SCHEME.</p>
        <p>This quality the Duke was quickly to discover. The canal scheme as the Duke propounded it was instantly declared by Brindley to be unworkable. No trust must be put in a river for purposes of navigation by reason of its liability to flood. They must travel a self- contained stream ; it must be carried across the Irwell by an aqueduct and must enter Manchester to a dock specially cut. At the Worsley end it must be burrowed into the cliffs so that the boats would travel like moles underground and receive the coal from the workings. Brindley made no survey. He walked over the ground and said it could be done. A scheme so dazzling left the Duke for the moment speechless, but he lost no time in asking for an Act of Parliament confering powers to carry it out.</p>
        <p>(To be continued).</p>
      </div>
