        <p>LIVERPOOL GOODS GUARD KILLED AT ST. HELENS. Robert Henry Clague, goods guard, of 59, Sutcliffe- street, Liverpool, who was employed by the London and North-Western Railway Company, met with a shocking death on Monday night at St. Helens station. He was in charge of a goods train from Wigan to Liverpool, and bad to shunt some waggons at St. Helens. About seven o'clock he was directing the shunting at the north end of the platform when he either got his foot fast in the rails or slipped on the ice, and was run over. The driver, Kay, who had received the order to go on from the deceased, hearing a shout of " Whoa ! "pulled up, and on getting off the engine found the unfortunate man under the tender with both legs cut off and suffering from other injuries which had proved fatal. The deceased was about thirty years of age,</p>
        <p>The Rev.'Fred Ilibbert, formerly of Blackpool and Newcastle, commenced his ministry at the West End Congregational Church, Southport, on Sunday morning. An illuminated address and a sum of £5lO were pre- sented on Saturday to Dr. Boyd Carpenter on his retirement from the Bishopric of Ripon. While dancing at a ball at Hambleton, Holderness, last Friday night,William Ward, farmer,was seized with illness, and died almost immediately.</p>
