      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February, 10, 1912.</p>
        <p>'himself and -the world In which tie now lived. A. quiver had crossed his face, thE muscles round his mouth had grown taut with sudden suspicion, and though he had replied quietly to her question, Isobel had known that he knew she had learnt something. As she watched him talking to Stella she saw a sud- den expression of startled wonder flash into his eyes, and the next moment he had said quickly: "Where did Tennant get that picture from? " "I've had it for years in an unfinished con- dition, Mr. Hawke," said their host's voice behind them. "I was telling Miss Train° that I had been waiting till I had found the model I wanted—and now I have succeeded in the person of the poor girl in whom Miss Traine was interested the other night." • Clifford Hawke nodded carelessly. "Indeed? I asked because at first the face of the woman on the canvas seemed like</p>
        <p>someone I once knew, but now I come to look more closely at it I see that it was a fancied resemblance. I suppose," he went on, "that a great many of your pictures are the crea- tion of your own imagination, Mr. Tennant?' "I do most of my work from life," the ar- tist replied. "This picture is a sketch, taken from memory, of my own sister years ago in Australia."</p>
        <p>"Australia? Then you come from the Golden West? " Clifford Hawke said suavely. "Yes—from a remote up-country place at the 'hack o' beyond, called Tarragoola C'ennant replied. Then he stopped sucbi nly at an exclamation from Isobel Frant. She had gone strangely white, and her dark eyes gleamed with excitement. "Are you ill, Miss Frant?" he said quickly. She forced a smile to her lips. "No, thank you, I think it's the heat. I will sit down near the door and rest a little; I have been doing rather a lot." And as he stood chatting to her plea- santly, Jaspar Tennant did not guess that the cause of Miss Frant's indisposition was his reference to the place where he had lived with his sister in Australia—the "back o' be- yond" place called Tarragoola. It was the name of the place that the shabby man had mentioned in his letter to Clifford Hawke!</p>
        <p>In the meantime Clifford Hawke had (tuned to Stella.</p>
        <p>"Don't, you think we have been here long enough, darling? I'm afraid I'm not in th mood to admire Lady Mary's protege's daubs to-day. have eyes only for you "Mr. lOnant is an artist of great pro- mise," Stela said coldly. "I am enjoying my- self very much." "I am sorry if I seem ill-tempered," he re- plied quickly, "but I have had a wretched day in the city, darling, and I feel done up." She looked up at him sympathetically. 'I did not know, Clifford. Shall we go at once then? Take me home. I will tell Lady Mary that she must chaperon Isobel." A few moments later she had said good- bye to Lady Mary, and then stood for a moment by Jasper Tennant's side. "Yon said once—that first evening we met —that I might call one day, Miss Traine," Ile said; "does that permission still hold good?" She flushed.</p>
        <p>"Of course it does. I shall be very glad to see you, and please, Mr. Tennant, send me Lotty Bond's address, and I will call on her. You see," she smiled, "she is our common protegee." The colour ran up into his face. Their protegee! Heavens! how he wished they could use the possessive pronoun in every- thing else! But that could never be! She was engaged to Clifford Hawke, and such a girl as she was could not have engaged herself to a man she did not love.</p>
        <p>"I will call with Lotty's address one day next week then," he replied quietly. "Yea! Conte on Tuesday; that is not any At Home day, and I don't think I shall be doing anything, then we can have a long talk about the future of Lotty; she can't al- ways be an artist's model, you know !" When she joined her fiancé he was silent and thoughtful, only speaking when he told her to draw the rug closer round her. When they reached the house in Park-lane he fol- lowed her into her boudoir, and when they were alone he took her into his arms and kissed her with a rough passion that startled her.</p>
        <p>"Darling !" he said hoarsely, "I have been longing to see you alone to-day. Those wretched tea fights get on my nerves, and I hate the idea of other people claiming you even for a moment."</p>
        <p>"That is foolish," she said gently. "I don't grudge you to your friends, Clifford, or to your occupations, and I don't think it quite fair that you should want me to give my old friends the cold shoulder." suppose you think me a selfish brute," he said with a short laugh. "I aon't mean to be, darling, but if you loved me as much as I love you, you would understand how I crave to be • alone with you—to bold you in my arms, and forget that the rest of the world is outside.'</p>
        <p>She was silent. She knew she ought to have thrilled at the knowledge that her future husband loved her 60 passionately, but his words left her cold and his passion made her shrink from him. She had thought that it would be easy to love him when she grew accustomed to him, but now she knew that she could never love him as a woman should love the man she is going to marry. If only she had waited—if only he had paused to learn her own heart! _ . .</p>
        <p>A "I wanted to see you alone, he began pre- sently, "because I had two requests to make, my darling, and I know you will grant them because of the love I have for you." A startled thought leapt to her mind. "What. are they?" she asked, forcing a smile to her lips. He caught her hands in his and held them closely. In all his life this strong man had never before felt so weak and uncertain of himself as he did in the presence of this woman he loved.</p>
        <p>"I want you to fix a speedy date for our marriage," he said in a low, earnest tone. "I can't wait any longer for you, darling. There is no need for us to wait; I spoke to your father this morning; he will agree to your wishes. Come, say you will make me a happy man in six weeks' time "Six weeks!" She snatched her hands from him, and broke into a startled laugh. "Oh, Clifford! how absurd! It's impossible quite, quite impossible!" His eyes flashed with sudden impatience. "Why impossible? Surely you are not making the hackneyed excuse about frocks a barrier, are you? " "No—it's not that. But—but—oh, Clifford, I don't feel that I want to be hurried into marriage quite so soon," she answered. "You see, I—l don't want to lose my freedom just yet," she went on lightly. "If you loved me you would not refuse such a natural request," Hawke said. "Surely I am not repulsive to you, am I? What has changed you, Stella? Before, you were at least glad to make nie happy, now you seem to have slipped away from my influence, and it hurts, girl—God! ' be broke out pas- sionately, "if you were more of a woman and less of a girl you would understand how your reluctance hurts me! "</p>
        <p>She put her hand on his arm. In spite of herself she was sorry for his evident disap- pointment. "Forgive me, Clifford. Perhaps I am rather hard. Forget it, dear. I will marry you when you like. It was stupid of me to want to defer the day. After all, what doee it matter—l mean—why should we wait, as you say?" lie I,?oked at her with longing eyes. This girl, with the clear serene eyes of childhood, and her detached air of calm acquiescence, fired his blood more than any woman ever had done before. She was more desirable in her coldness than all the others had been in their abandon.</p>
        <p>"You shall never regret it," he said hoarsely. "I will devote my life to you, darling—every wish shall be gratified—every thought be anticipated! She endured his embrace with an outward composure that she was far from feeling, and it was not till her lover had left the house that she remembered he had not told her his second favour he had wished her to grant. In the meantime Clifford Hawko hurried home to the flat in the Albany. Now that the wedding clay was fixed he felt a great load off his mind. Of late days he had been shadowed by the dread of some calamity, Then Isobel. Frant's strange conduct had flung a new torment in his way. She had suggested so much knowledge of his past that he hai</p>
        <p>1:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>wondered what he would say could he see her now.</p>
        <p>She drew the letter out of the envelope and opened it. Then for a few seconds there was a deep tense silence in the room, broken only by the sound of a woman's quick breathing, and the faint rustle of paper. Presently she folded the letter and replaced it in its en- velope, and going to a small inlaid writing- table, opened a locked drawer, and thrust it far back, then locked it again. "It has fallen into my hands!" she whis- pered with an odd little smile; "some strange destiny has dealt me a trump card, and Fate shrill decide what time will be the best for me to play it. I don't think now that I have any lack of interest in my life. Clifford Hawke is nearer to me than lie is to Stella, because I am probably the only woman who shams a secret with him."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>BID FOR A BRIDE</head>
        <p>A BID FOR A BRIDE</p>
        <p>BLANCHE EARDLEY, Author of "Kitty _Bell TActress," "The Lady</p>
        <p>Killer," &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>SYNOPSIS.</head>
        <p>SYNOPSIS.</p>
        <p>CLIFFORD HAWKE, financier and secretary of the Amalga- mated Copper Company, is about to set the seal on hit ambition. At the age of forty he has the consent of the managing director of the same company to propose mar- riage to his only daughter, Stella Traine, a beautiful girl. Though scarcely sure of herself she accepts him. Hawke returns to his chambers, and receives a letter left during blsalusence. Though it comes from a former friend, Paul Steinway, Hawke is by no means pleased; evidently this man knows too much of the financier's former (lays. At the door of a house to which Hawke has driven Stella Train(' in his motor, a girl In the crowd faints. Stella asks Hawke to lift her up, but instead he sends for a servant,. A young artist guest comes to the fainting wornan's rescue and carries her into the house. Later Stella and Ihe artist—Jaspor Tennant—discuss what shall be done for the poor woman—L(oy Bond—and this !cads to an invita- tion for Stella to visit the artist's studio. In a conversation with his hostess—Lady Mary Stainer—Tennant refers to the subject which is his principal aim in life—the avenging of his sister on the man who deserted her and their child.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER IV.</p>
        <p>ISOBEL FRANT.</p>
        <p>Isobel Frant stood before the long mirror in her own room, and glanced intently at her reflection, then turned away with a little ;jgh of contempt. What did it matter to her that she was handsome and possessed a figure that all the other women in London envied? She was no one—just the shadow behind her cousin's radiant personality. There was no man to whom she was an ideal, and the one man whom she had wanted with all the strength of her passionate nature was ignor- ant even of the fact that she lived! It was a month since Stella had become en- gaged to Clifford Hawke, and during that time Isobel had been obliged to sit still and listen to the congratulations, and witness the meetings of the engaged lovers, and each oc- casion had driven the iron of jealousy further into her heart. To-day Stella had motored with her fiancé into BerksbTre to see the country place he was buying for her, and though she had been sAsked to accompany them, Isobel had re- fused, pleading a headache, and Stella had instead taken Lady Mary Stainer as her chaperon. laobel dressed leisurely, and then in an ex- quisitely cut coat and skirt, and irreproach- able boots and gloves, went out. She was restless, and full of suppressed fire that had to be tired before she could find release from her thoughts. "Shall I call a hansom, miss?" the butler said, coming forward to the door. She shook her head. "No, I will walk.' She left the house and turned into the Park, walking with a swift and graceful stride that drew admiring glances after her. Presently she slackened her speed and sat down on one of the chairs beneath the trees, gazing before her with thoughful, moody eyes at the crowds of people, and the carri- ages that passed and repassed with mono- tonous regularity. She knew most of the people in them, but was glad that they had not glanced her way; she was in no mood for the usual light chatter that she indulged in so successfully. Presently her attention was arrested by a man, who sat in a chair some few yards from her own. He had a thin dark face, burnt a deeper brown than an Eng- lish summer would have burnt it, and there was in his expression as he watched the long chain of motors and carriages an intensity that made Isobel wonder for whom it was that he was watching so earnestly. From his appearance she judged him to be in low water; there was an air of poverty about the well brushed coat, and his hollow cheeks told a story of mean living. Isobel was amused at her own interest in him, and found herself wondering who and what he was, and why he stared so patiently into the faces of the passers-by. "He might be waiting to see someone he hated!" she mused idly. "I don't think I'd like to be that person, whoever it is; that man would hate hard!" Presently the man rose, and moved away, passing the chair in which Isobel sat, and as she glanced after him she noticed that he had dropped a letter by the side of his chair. Almost instinctively she left her chair and went towards it, intending to restore it to the man, who was now some distance away; but the next moment she had uttered an ex- clamation. It was an unpostcd letter, and was addressed in a bold hand to "Clifford Hawke' Esq., The Albany, S.W." Isobel stared down at it with fascinated eyes. That the very man of whom she had been thinking —whose memory had driven her into the Park that afternoon--should also have been the subject of the shabby man's thought seemed almost like a miracle instead of a co- incidence. Her fingers trembled as she held the letter. She was gripped by a wave of excitement that utterly swamped the lethargy of the past few hours. What Should she do with the letter? It was her obvious duty either to leave it by the side of the chair where the man had dropped it, or, failing that, to post it to the man to whom it was addressed. But Isobel Frant was not a woman to whom simple methods appealed. She loved in- trigues and probing beneath the innermost secrets of people's lives, and she was more than interested in the life of Clifford Hawke, the man who was engaged to her cousin. lie had always held her imagination, and his stupendous luck and forceful personality had roused in her a longing to know the man as he really was; to learn the dark ways through which he had emerged into the broad way of the popular financier. And now she held her long-looked-for chance in her hand. In the letter addressed to him she felt certain that there would be something that Clifford Hawke would not willingly have made pub- lic—and if he knew that she knew it, would it not draw them together in a link of com- radeship? He was a man to whom loyalty might count a great deal, and from comrade- ship it would not be her fault if a closer link were not forged. As all these thoughts chased each other rapidly through her mind, two bright spots burned in her cheeks, and she hurried out of the Park, anxious to get away from the summer crowd. Once back in her own rooms in the pala- tial house of Park-lane, she drew the letter out of her pocket again, and turned it over with trembling fingers. As Blue Beard's wife might have hesitated outside the locked chamber, so she paused now before she pryed into the letter that belonged to Clifford Hawke. If, after all, it were only the usual begging letter that such men were pestered with, she would feel an utter fool. But the shabby man who had dropped the letter did not look that style of man. An air of defiant inde- pendence had marked his sunburnt face. As her long slim fingers tampered with the en- velope, which was a common one, a sudden abrupt jerk tore the badly sealed wrapper, and the next moment it was open, and a sheet of thin paper showed invitingly. • A gleam of excitement sparkled in her eyes. How little Clifford Hawke, on his motor trip • into the country with Stella and Lady Mary Stainer, guessed what she was doing. She</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>CHAPTER V.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER V.</p>
        <p>THE FACE ON THE CANVAS</p>
        <p>"It's so good of you to have come! I was afraid that you might have forgotten." , Stella smiled as Jasnar Tennant Welcomed her and Lady Mary Stainer to the studio on his show Sunday. "I never forget a promise," she said gdntly; "besides, I am interested in your pictures, Mr. Tennant; Lady Mary has been telling me so much about them." The young man's handsome face flushed. "It was very good of Lady Mary to men- tion them. I'm not doing very much just now, I have been so busy with other things!" She looked at him witlt interested eyes. "Have you done anything about that poor girl?" she asked presently. "I have been thinking so much about her since that night. I do hope you kept her address, because I might be able to help her." "I have both her address and her name," he replied, thinking the while how beautiful Stella looked and how strangely Bad her face seemed, in spite of her engagement to the man of the hour. He had not seen her since the day of Lady Mary's ball, and now the sight of her sent the blood tingling through his veins and reminded him that she was the promised wife of another man. "Where is Mr. Hawke?" he said abruptly. "I think he is bringing my cousin, Miss' Frant, in his car," she answered; "he pro- mised to call for her this afternoon as I have been lunching with Lady Mary." She turned away as she spoke and glanced at a canvas on an easel.</p>
        <p>"Is this something you are working on? Do let me see it, if I may?" _ .</p>
        <p>He hesitated. "It's oue I haven't touched for ages; I thought I would begin it all over from memory when I could find the right model to sit for me, and now I think I have."</p>
        <p>As he spoke he turned the canvas round, and Stella saw the face of a woman, young and fragile-looking, with pale gold hair and wistful blue eyes shining through tears. Pathos and helplessness were the keynote of the character that gazed through the deli- cate lines of the face, and Stella felt a twinge of curiosity shoot through her as to the identity of the woman. "Then you found your model?" she said. "Yes—can't you guess who?" She looked at him inquiringly. "Not--</p>
        <p>He nodded. "Yes—the little girl we both felt such,an interest in that night. Can't you see the likeness?" "Of course I can now. I was puzzled as to where I had seen the face before; it is very like that poor little Lotty Bond. lam glad you have made her of some use. Pro- mise me you won't let her want any more,". she went on in a low voice. "I will go and see her as soon as I can." "So far as I am concerned she shall not lack work," he answered gravely, "but I think a woman's sympathy now and then would be very welcome to her—poor little soul, she seems very homeless." "Who seems homeless?" said Lady Mary's voice behind them.</p>
        <p>"This poor girl who fell down outside your house the other night," Stella said quickly. "You remember, Mr. Tennant carried her in for me." As she spoke a slight colour stained her face. She remembered how Clifford Hawke had failed on that occasion—how far he had fallen below the standard of chivalry when he had refused to lift up the unconscious "She is sitting for me as my model now," Tennant said. "I was telling Miss Traine how I had found just the model I wanted for my unfinished picture." Lady Mary glanced at the picture and frowned slightly. She had seen that picture of Jasper 'Pennant's before, and had hoped that he had put it aside. She knew what he was when he got on the topic of the woman on the canvas.</p>
        <p>"So the little protegee is provided for, is she?" she said briskly; "in that case, my dear Stella, you need not bother about her any more. Now that Jaspar has found the ideal model he will keep her well provided for; and probably work her to death into trle bargain!' "But I am interested in her too!" Stella said in a firm, quiet voice. She knew that Lady Mary disapproved of her having a com- mon interest with the young artist, and she was annoyed that she should have shown so much interest as to rouse Lady Mary's pro- tective instinct. Yet, ever since she had met Jaspar Tennant his image had entirely come between herself and the man she was going to marry, forcing her to draw comparisons that were odious to her fiancé. Lady Mary looked at Jaspar Tennant. "What is this young person's name? " she said.</p>
        <p>"Lotty Bond, and she lives at 14, Vincent- buildings, Westminster," he replied, then turned away to greet some new arrivals. "Do you think Clifford Hawke would like you to interest yourself in this girl as she is now Jaspar's model?" Lady Mary said slowly as she .and Stella stood gazing at the canvas on the easel.</p>
        <p>The girl flushed hotly. "Why not? " she replied proudly. "Clif- ford has no right to interfere between me and any of my interests." She paused suddenly. The man they were discussing was coming towards them, tie had a cold grim look on his dark face that Stella was beginning to recognise as the sign of some inward rage. She wondered idly what it was that had upset him. Surely he had not quarrelled with Isobel? She looked at her cousin curiously. There was an air of secrecy about her—she conveyed the idea of possessing some knowledge that was hidden from the rest of the world. It would be too absurd to imagine that Clifford could be angry with Isobel? Why, he had hardly ever noticed her except in the ordinary way of courtesy. As Clifford Hawke's eyes fell upon her they lit up with a sudden light. "So you arrived safely! he said, that subtle note of possession creeping into his voice as he spoke. She nodded. "Yes. You have been a long time. Did you and Isobel have a spin in the Park first?</p>
        <p>Isobef -irant's voice-broke in. "Yes, I persuaded Mr. Hawke to take me for a breath of fresh air, Stella, dear, and being a kind, unselfish man he did'as I asked him, though I am afraid he wee horribly bored!" And Isobel laughed merrily. "I'm sorry if I made you feel that, Miss Frant," he said politely. "Believe me, I was most interested in your conversation, and was only too sorry when we were obliged to return from our spin." Isobel smiled as she glanced at him. Sae had taken the opportunity that chance had offered that afternoon, and had very elightly conveyed to the man whose secret she had diecovered that her knowledge of his past life was more extensive than the girl's whom he was going to make his wife. Ile had listened to her careless reference to "Australia " and be had not winced; then she had begun a discussion on surnames, and it was when she mentioned one name to him that her shaft pierced the thick wall he had raised between</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <p>When your head is dull and heavy, your tongue furred, your bowels costive, and you rise in the morning tired, with no relish for breakfast and dreading your work ; when you are racked with pains—in the head, chest, back, stomach_all over ; when you are bilious and unable to look at food; when you feel "done up," have no appetite, no energy, no interest or ambition—your stontaclt and lioor are out of order. As a result, the decayed products of undigested food are poisoning your • blood and sapping your vitality. It may be the weather, or overwork, errors in diet, or constitutional weakness ; but, whatever the cause, your stomach and liver need help, and need it sorely. Mother Seigel's Syrup, made from roots, barks and leaves, provides all the help they need. It will tone up your stomach, renew your appetite, make food nourish you, purify your blood, stimulate your liver to healthy activity, regulate your bowels, and give you new strength, new energy, new life.</p>
