      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>SEIGtiTiE;YREP</head>
        <p>SEIGtiTiE;YREP</p>
        <p>been for the moment silent With surprise, an !may, coming upon the top of all this, had been the scene at Jaspar Tennant's studio that afternoon. The face of the woman on the faded canvas had recalled a memory that belonged to the hated pact, and instantly there had sprung into existence a strong hate against the man who evidently knew the story of the woman of the painting. He had two fresh dangers to crush in the persons of Isobel Frant and Jaspar Tennant. AA he opened the door with his latch key, his manservant came forward..</p>
        <p>"There is a gentleman waiting to see you, sir," he said. Clifford Hawke frowned. "A gentleman? Who is it? "</p>
        <p>_ "He wouldn't give me his name, sir, he said it wasn't important. He is in the library." A moment later Hawke had opened the library door and entered the room. As he did so a man rose from a chair; a shabby tall man with a thin, sunburnt face. "So we meet at last! " he said slow'. "You don't seem pleased to see me. Didn't you expect me? " "I'm afraid you've made a mistake," Hawke began haughtily. The other laughed. "Not I. Don't try to bluff me. You are John Clifford—and I, my dear boy, am Paul Steinway !" (To be Continued.)</p>
        <p>The 216 Bottle contains three times as much as the 111 i size. Sold also in tablet form, price 219.</p>
        <p>in the colour of tha net, which may be In Wes I harmonise with the suit, in all black, or in black 111 white. In the latter instance the black net veils I' %bite. A further touch of novelty, and in some rils of luxury, is afforded by the three jewelled buttol that attach the jsbot to the coat. These may.be„ small diamonds, if the wearer can afford them. there is a wide cLoice in enamel artificial rends of , semi precious stones. Such a trifle is easily made,: home, and suitable decorative buttons now lying might be brought forth from hidden stores and uttll,l to advantage in this particular direstion. Fashion Brevities.—Dancing skirts are made ii yards round but are cut to look half the width. one-piece frock is growing steadily in 130" Debutantes of this sprirg are to wear the white with its garniture of lilies of the valley above gauging and ruching, populsr seventy years ego. 11,, coiffures will Le in keeping with the dresses but t10:1 carelessly and artistically arranged than during '1 mid-Victorian period. The sleeves of bodices P. blouses are longer for ordinary wear. Those on 001 for outdoors are a icier and finished with large cell , meet the glove. The corset for 1912 is to be made WI over the bust, and longer over the hips. It will be cerd pliable and flexible, and although the waist will be 01; clearly defined it will not be compressed in any 70, The strenuous life led by women of to-day preul the possibility of tight lacing, moreover as it is Cl recognized that nothing can be beautiful trbio.s directly opposed to Nature's laws, there ; 8 probability of the return of the wasp-waist.</p>
