      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>NURSE RAE ADVISES CUTICURA</head>
        <p>NURSE RAE ADVISES CUTICURA</p>
        <p>REMEDIES FOR SKIN TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>"It is now more than twelve months since the skin on my hands became rough and itching. I tried simple dressings and several patent ointments, but they only eased them for a time. If I did any washing the acute irritation afterwards was terrible. It would wake me up at night. The roughness of the skin turned to deep cuts. I have had to wear gloves night and day for a long time. I had to stay at home for a whole month and not take cases, because the dis- inrectants I used in my profession only aggravated the irritation.</p>
        <p>" I sent away for several samples of different cures at different times, but until I got Cuticura Ointment and Soap I had no real relief. After using the sample box of Cuticura Ointmant my hands became easier, but they had been bad for so long and the cuts were so very deep that I had to use four boxes of Cuticura Ointment before I bad a complete cure. I began to think they never would be light again, and if I had not persevered with Cuticura Ointment they never would. I have shown them to several doctors and have had ointment from them, but I only had temporary relief. I shall only be too pleased to recommend the Cuticura Remedies wherever I come across any skin troubles." (Signed) Nurse Rae, 232, Dudley Rd , Wolverhampton, Eng., May 9, 1911. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post- free from nearest dept : Newbery, 27, Charterhouse Sq , London ; It. Towns &amp; Co.' Sydney, N. S. W. ; Lennon, Ltd, Cape Town ; Muller, Maclean &amp; Co., Calcutta and Bombay; " Cuticura," Boston, U.S.A., Tender-faced men should shave with Cuticura Soap Shaving Stick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>POLITICAL AGENT ACQUITTED.</head>
        <p>POLITICAL AGENT ACQUITTED.</p>
        <p>MR. A. H. CROSFIELD'S EVIDENCE IN AN ESSEX TRIAL.</p>
        <p>At the Essex Assizes on Monday, John Hughes general secretary of the Gladstone League, was acquitted of the charges brought against him in regard to a child named Edna May Houghton, the six-year-old daughter of a licensed victualler at Saffron Walden.— Lord Burghclere and Mr. A. 11. Crosfield, formerly M.P. for Warrington, gave evidence as to the accused's good character. Lord Burgbclere said that as Mr. Herbert Gardner M.P. for the Saffron Walden Division, he had employed Mr. Hughes as Ms political private secretary for ten years. Sir Frederick Low : Have you bad any opportunity during those ten years of observing his character ? Yes, I consider he is a man of good moral character, high principles, and great intelligence. I think you have, as a matter of fact, come direct from the South of Prance on Purpose to give evidence in this case ?—Yes. _</p>
        <p>And against your doctor's orders ?—Yes. Mr. Arthur Henry Crestlaid, formerly M.P. for War- rington, said that he had seen defendant about once a fortnight for the past two years. " I have always felt," he continued, "and I feel now, a very great respect for Mr. Hughes, I have always found him a well-conducted man and a man of high character." Defendant gave an emphatio denial to the charges against him. .</p>
        <p>-lhe jury retired, and after an absence of twenty minutes, returned a verdict of not guilty.</p>
        <p>....::;:s'i:ii:';il:*::;';''</p>
        <p>_.....,„ ..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>HOW TO CURE RHEUMATISM.</head>
        <p>HOW TO CURE RHEUMATISM.</p>
        <p>Go to any Chemist or Drug-store, and ask !cil following prescription : One ounce of Infusion Gentian, one ounce Syrup of Rhubarb, one coil Liquid Barkola Compound, and five ounces of 511 of Ginger. Let these be well shaken together .° take a-teaspoonful after each meal and one at bedt" . This .simple recipe is the famous Berkola Prencr tton,which since it was first prepared by a well-kn? doctor a few years ago has done so much to rel'i the pangs cf Rheumatism, Gout, and all uric 11l diseases. Very many thousands of cases have 1° entirely cured by it, and its fame has extended over the world. It is especially useful in severe col cf Inflammatory Rheumatism and cures is hen a" thing else fails.</p>
