      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>"THE ROMANCE OF THE BRIDHEWAI CANAL."</p>
        <p>th. Mr. Arthur Mountie Id delivered a lecture before. members of the Warrington Society at the Old Acao on Monday evening, on "The Itqmance of Bridgeset Canal." Mr. C. Madeley presided over a good at" ARCO. of The LECTUWER told in a fascinating way the stor10( the canal enterprise, and claimed that Francis Delyor Bridgewater, more than any other man, was the ell 01 of modern commercial Lancashire. The But:60110,o the lecture is to be published, in the form of a feticg of articles in the " Examiner," the first of W" appears to-day. to In ope fling the discussion which followed the leo vf Mr. JOHN FAIRHURST made reference to the meellool traction on the canal and asked whether the orig Act allowed steam traction. aea Mr. A. KERR speaking as an engineer made refer:to to Brindley whose genius was the admiration 02 profession. Mr. lIALIAWAY had found interest in the lectxtr_j, description of the Duke's difficulties in securing Ina., Mr. Mounfleld had shrewdly said that the 30 gine pear tree would be regarded aa dirt cheap If it hid be bought for public works or public improvemeetto Alderman BENNETT paid eloquent tribute to tote lecturer saying never in that building, with memorable meetings had he listened to anything :lit such great interest and pleasure. It had not bee° • 0 surp rise however, for they all knew the lecto command of both fact and language. The addreseL'ei touched many chords and he bad been deeply imPre'rool by the oioture of the two heroic figures who 71e(1 carried through the canal undertaking. He oonolLtef by reading Mrs. Barbauld's lines on the Bridg" Canal. te. Mr. L. GREENING said he would not have reiss—,4o lecture for a great deal. It had been given wlttinf humour and skill and conveyed a •aat amotto'' information. Mr. OSNVALD LOFTHOUSE spoke eulogistica..lT 00y addtess, saying that one who could convey so such a manner was not only a man of genius, but r h great literary skill at his command. Mr. E. J. HALL gave an interesting address,and adore a good story of Brindley and his many discoursge°loo Mr. CitosvENott brought the discussion to a._. accorded to the lecturer.°,oo with a brief address and a hearty vote of thailL• 001* Questions relating to the dates of the earliest oil go the powers conferred by the Acts of Parliameo.te means of traction and engineering methods WI asked and to these Mr. Mountleld gave ii rep'''. TOO members adjourned for refreshment and f° informal discussion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The London Season.—London society is looking forward to an early and busy season, not taken up with one great event, as it was last year, by the Coronation. The King's return from India has been saddened by the death of his brother-in-law the Duke of Fife, and the bereavement of his sister but that most regrettable occurence has not interfered with the Ro3al pageants, in which London takes so great a delight, and which will be repeated on the occasion of the State opening of Parliament on the 11th. The King and Queen might well look forward to a long and restful holiday after the innumerable State functions and ceremonials that have been crowded into the past seven or eight months, but there seems little oppor- tunity for that with the Courts coming on and the Parliamentary season about to begin. Many leading hostesses are expected to entertain on a large scale, and the season will see a number of fashionable weddings. One of the most important—the marriage of the Marquess of Stafford and Lady Eileen Butler, have been fixed for April 11. As the elder son of the Duke and , Duchess of Sutherland, the Marquess, now in his 23rd year, will one day be the biggest landowner in the Kingdom. The Dickens Centenary.—Rochester has naturally taken a prominent part in the Dickens Centenary celebrations, for it was at Gad's Hill, three miles from that town that the great novelist spent the latter part of his life, and in his books there are many references to places of interest in the neighbourhood. Of Gad's Hill itself, Charles Dolby gives many interesting details in " Charles Dickens as I knew him." Each of the bedrooms, he says, was most comfortably furnished including a sofa, easy chairs, a large sized writing table profusely supplied with paper and materials, a change of new quill pens, and there was in each room a small library of books. There was always a comfortable fire in winter, with a shining copper kettle on each fireplace and on a side table cups and saucers, tea caddy, teapot, sugar and milk," Not only was everything done for the comfort of visitors, but life was very orderly, at Gad's Hill and except at table no servant was ever seen about after household duties were done. A Shakespeare Exhibition.—The year of the Shakes- peare tercentary, 1916, is still some way off, but there is not too much time for organizing and raising funds If the occasion is to be celebrated worthily. An exhibition designed to assist the movement for the provision of a national memorial is to he opened at Earl's Court in London in May, and will bear the title " Shakespeare's England." Mrs. Cornwallis West, who originated the idea, is taking an active part in the scheme, and the exhibition will afford an opportunity for the public to see London as Shakespeare knew it. Instead of the familiar type of exhibition, there is to be an exact reproduction of Elizabethan London, with its old-world streets and overhanging timbered buildings. The people will be in mediteval dress, the women in ruffs and farthin gales and the nobles, citizens and apprentices in all the attire of the period. There will be replicas of the Mermaid Tavern and the "Globe" theatre, in which Shakespeare's plays will be given precisely as originally produced. The grounds will be given up to a revival of the old time fair, with its sports, ganizs and tournaments, etc. This will give a completeness to the most realistic and interesting exhibition of Elizabethan London that has yet been attempted. Women's Recruiting Campaign.—E: ingston-on-Thames is the first town to organise a womens' recruiting campaign on behalf of the Territorials. When it is a question of raising a sum of money, by public sub- scriptions, it is the fashion to fix a certain time to do it in, and the Kingston ladies are to devote" a wet k " —February 10-17—to recruiting 133 men, in order to bring up the local Territorial companies to their full complement. There will be recruiting marches,a church parade, but where the ladies will come in most effectu- ally, will be at a Cinderalls dance, at the Drill Hall on February 14th. It has often been said that if the maidens would bestow their favours only on young men who were Territorials, the country would soon have that force up to its full strength. It is not, however, threats,such as that course would suggest, but blandish- ments which the Kingston ladies propose to employ, although they are not expected to go to quite the same lengths as did Jane, Duchess of Gordon, when she helped to raise the regiment that is now the Gordon Highlanders. Colours for the Spring.—The stranger within cur gates, more especially in the Asiatic, is struck with the absence of colour which is so marked a feature of our winter clothing, European visitors declare that we are afraid of displaying colours in our attire, although we use it freelyjin our houses. The reproach will be undeserved this Spring, at least to judge from the list of tints and shades, not to mention combinations of colour that are to be seen as the year grows older. Despite the superstitions attached to the wearing of the green " it will be seen in many of its most charm- ing and becoming shades, of which lettuce is foremost. Among combinations, purple and silver grey, violet and a peculiar shade of smoke, and wine red and gold are shown. Vivid crimson aud malimaison pink, and the loveliest mauves and heliotropes will be among the favourites. -</p>
        <p>Jabots and Frills.—The jabot of white lawn or cambric, is an item of womens toilette, which is beginning to slate by reason of its monotony. A novel and pretty accessary to be donned with severe tailor modes of satin, silk, or even navy-blue serge is a jabot of net consisting of two frills of equal width and length. These have pinked-out edges and when gathered are sewn on to a narrow ribbon band. The novelty consists not in the foregoing description, but</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
