      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Penketh.</head>
        <p>Penketh.</p>
        <p>PENKETII COUNCIL SCIIOOL —A grand entertainment was given in the Wesleyan Sunday scohocl on Thursday evening, by a number of the senior scholars of the above school. The event was so popular that the school was packed, which speaks well both for the training of the children by Miss F Watson and for the interest the scholars and parents have taken in the event4Dr Murray,J.P. was in the chair and an excellent miscellaneous programme was submitted, including the humorous play " Court of Hearts," in which the char- acters were : King, F Finch ; queen, 0 E Baker; knave, A Tarburton ; aces, A Goulden and C W Foden. Miss Watson accompanied. There were part-songs by the Band of Hope choir from Warrington, songs by Dolly Cain, Florrie Watson,Ada Hamor,George Tanner, Arthur Hamblett,Eva Tanner, and recitations by Jeanie IVarbrick, Annie Edwards, Violet Smith and Ethel Thomas. Votes of thanks moved by Mr W Loxley and seconded by Mr T Dunabin, close' a very successful evening. WEsLEVAN CHURCH', HOME MISSIONS.—In connec- tion with the above, the Home Mission Sermons were preached on Sunday last. The preachers were Rev. W. A. Beckitt (superintendent of the circuit), in the morning, and the Rev. H. Middlewood, the resident minister, in the evening. There was a very appreciative audience. Suitable hymns were sung.—The annual Home Missionary meeting was held on Wednesday evening in the Wesleyan Church. There was quite a large company present which reflects great credit on the devotional section of the Wesley Guild who were responsible for this meeting. The deputation from the Home Missions was the very popular minister, Rev. G. A. Parkinson (late of the West London Mission.) The subject he took for his disc3urse w s " Th 3 problems of a Great City " illustrated with lantern slides. The chair was taken by Mr. F. A. Harris (senior circuit steward.) The collections were in advance of last year. Mr. Woodward very kindly managed the lantern.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Grappenhall. THE SEWERAGE SCHEME.</head>
        <p>Grappenhall. THE SEWERAGE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>JOINT PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of the Parish Council was held on Thursday evening lost in the Council schoolroom. Mr F R Roberts presided, and there were also present :—Messrs A Lister Kaye, J Carter, F Fox, Dr Hutt, J Bradford, F Burrows, W Lomas and Chas li Hoult (clerk).</p>
        <p>CORRESPONDRICC E The Clerk read a letter From the It.D.C. stating that Mr Diggle (sewerage engineer) would attend the meet- ing to explain the whole sewerage scheme.</p>
        <p>PARISH MEETING.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr Lister-Kaye, it was decided to hold a meeting of the ratepayers on Tuesday, February 20th, 1912, at 8 o'clock to further consider the sewerage scheme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>IMPORTANT JOINT .MEETING.</head>
        <p>IMPORTANT JOINT .MEETING.</p>
        <p>As was decided at the last Council meeting, repre- sentatives of the three following parishes (Grappenhall, Latchford Without and Tholwall) neat met in Council to discuss the sewerage scheme. There was a large gathering of councillors from the three aforementioned parishes, as well as Mr G F Ashton (clerk to the Runcorn District Concil), and Mr James Diggle, C.F., of the firm of Messrs James D:ggle and Son, of Heywood and Westminster. THE SCREW: PROPOCNDED. . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Diggle was then asked to fully explain the plans of the scheme. This he did most exhaustively in an address which ocmpied more than 1 hours. After going into almost every detail of the scheme, and explanation of the disposal works—which will be situated near Lymm—he stated the estimated cost. The whole of the sewerage scheme, including side sewers, would cover a distance of about 81 miles. The total estimated cost would be about £18,500. lie here stated that the estimate was a full one and thought that it would be completed below and not in Excess of those figures. Mr. Diggle went on to say that the annual cost wonld be something like £l,OOO. This included interest and sinking fund, and wages for one man who wuuld be sufficient to look after the disposal works. The whole cost would be equll to a rate of Is. 23. in the £, estimated on a population of 5,000. Mr. Diggle then stated that he would be pleased to answer any questions put to him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>.1101113AR15ED WITH QUERIES.</head>
        <p>.1101113AR15ED WITH QUERIES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lister-Kaye : Will there be a Local Government Beard meeting? And will it be held in the parish? Mr. Ashton : Yes. Mr. L'ster-Kaye : I still think that a competitive scheme would have helped us in deciding as to whether we had the best and cheapest scheme. Mr. Diggle might be the very best engineer, and his scheme might also be the best, but although we have requested it of the Runcarn District Council we have had no oppor- tunity of getting a competitive scheme. Are we too late to g into this matter now ? Mr. Ashton : It cauld not be done now. Mr. Pilling (member the Runcorn District Corms. il) : We did n3t go blindfolded into the scheme. We had bad experieuce of Mr. Diggle's past work and were well ratisflod, and in taking the s sheme to him we felt sure it was in the hands of the right man. Mr. Burrows (member of the Runcorn District Cauncil) : The scheme has been before the Council for years, and there has been plenty of time to ask sooner for a competitive scheme. The Chairman : lam informed that competitive schemes are not usual in these matters. Mr. Holmes (Latchford Without) : As ratepayers we have not been consulted, the Runcorn District Council just placed the scheme before us and I suppose we must pay for it. Dr. Hutt asked for further information as to the dispcsal of sludge. Would nat the land treatment be cheaper ? Also he asked if preferential treatment would be given to residents who now had a sewerage scheme which had cost a good deal, and which found its way into the Ship Canal ? Mr Ashton answered the latter question and stated that the rate would be on the parish ; no preferential treatment would be given. It appeared a hardship but could not be avoided. Mr W Roberts (Latchford Without) : Some parts of our parish are disgraceful. Those of Us who have a • sewerage system have the annoyance of it being used by others. There is no doubt that some scheme is wanted. Whether this is the best or not we cannot decide here and now. We are told that there will be a Local Government Board inquii y,and any ratepayer will have the right to attend and appeal. lam certain of one thing—we shall all get kicked out when next we meet the electors. (Laughter.) Nevertheless I do think further that we have been too ill-formed on the scheme in the past. Mr It T Fairclough (Latchford Without) : Who will pay the largest share ? It is evident that the cost will be greater in some parishes than others. Mr. Ashton : The District Council will make an allocation against each parish, and they will be asked to pay accordingly. Of course the overseers will have a right to object and appeal against the allocation if it is thought unfair. Mr. Keirclough : Shall we have this information before the Local Government Board inquiry ? Mr. Ashton : Yes. TIIE EgGINEER REPLIES. Mr. Diggle then answered the question addressed to him. He said that the disposal of the sludge would be by a special tank system and would be far more economical than any other system. From these tanks the sludge passes on to the land and is dug in. Storm overflows will be disposed of in the canal. Houses draining into the canal will in all probability be taken in. They were expected to do so. The competitive schemes was not usual, and often enough such schemes were competed for by firms short of work. He might say that at the present time he was engaged on 48 large sewerage schemes, and would not have time to compete for schemes. Someone had said that the original estimate had grown from £13,500 to £18,500. The first scheme was incomplete. They had had to go half a mile further, and the first schema did not include the cost of the land. Further it had been stated that the Is. 2d. rate will te Is. 23. for ever. This was not so, as when the rateable value of the area increased the sewerage rate would in consequence, disminish. Mr. Diggle in further answering questions said that isolated houses would for the present no t be included. Mr Bradford : That is a hardship on those who live in isolated houses. Mr. Giggle : Quite so. But the scheme will provide for the greatest happiness of the greatest number.</p>
        <p>Mr Hutton (Chairman, Thelwall) in moving a vote of thanks to Mr. Diggle and Mr. Ashton for attending the meeting said that although he and others had personal grievances against the scheme en some points, they had none against the two gentlemen who had given them such valuable information on this great scheme. Mr. RT. Fakelough seconded. It was supported by Mr. F. R. Roberts and carried.</p>
        <p>Lymm. TiE DANCING SEASON.—Lent now drawing near the dancing season is getting somewhat congested. On Wednesday night a ball on behalf of a local Hockey club was held in the Drill Hall. Again on Friday night the same place was the scone of a further terpsichorean entertainment. Also on Friday night a ball was held at the Railway Hotel, Heatley, in con- nection with the Oughtrington Football Club.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MRS. A. J. ASTLEY.—We regret to have to report the death of Mrs. Arthur J. Astley, of Chester, who expired on the 6th inst, after an exceptionally painful illness of ten weeks duration. For over twenty years the Astley family took a foremost place in all matters connected with the Congregatiocal Church in Lymm, and sympathy with Mr. Arthur J. Astley in his sad bereavement will be widespread in this district.</p>
        <p>LYMIM WESLEY Monday last a most interesting lecture was given by the Rev. Harry Middlewood of Penketb, on "Wesley's England." Mr. Middlewood put before his hearers a vivid picture of the English life of nearly two hundred years ago ; showing that though the millennium has not yet arrived the present age is certainly in advance, both in religious vigour and social conditions, of the so-called " Good old time." Unfortunately, owing to the inclemency of the weather, there was not a large attendance, but those who had braved the elements were amply repaid for doing so. Lvatat LADIES HOCKEY CLUB.—The second annual dance in connection with the above was held on Wednesday night in the Drill Hall. There was a large number of members and friends present, including a contingent from Warrington. An excellent pro- gramme of music was supplied by Mr bkinrer's band of Altrincham. Messrs S Critchley and Fred Eaton were the M.C.'s, and the satisfactory per- formance of their &amp;ties contributed largely to the pleasure of the evening. The Drill Hall for this occasion had a very inviting appearance, having been transformed by judicious and effective decoration by the Misses Hazlehurst, Mcßride, Guest and Mabel Kirkpatrick. The refreshments and other details were in the hands of the Misses L and N Pearson, Hazlehurst,Guest,Katbleen Bate and Mcßride. The duties of stewards, &amp;0., were in the hands of Messrs J H Brazendale, T G Bate, Arthur Booth, Fred Chesters, W Sumner,Aithur Booth, W Kirkpatrick, W Hinton. and A C Smithson. Mr Watervvorth acted as hon. secretary. CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY CONCERT.—On Wednesday evening a large audience assembled at the Drill Hall, the occasion being a concert given on behalf of the Education Committee of the Warrington Equitable Industrial Co-operative Society Ltd. Mr J Massey of the Education Committee acted as chairm^n Other gentlemen on the platform were : Messrs Rushton and Smith (Finance Committee), Cook (Shops Com- mittee) and Ferguson (Education Committee.) The latter gentleman organising the programme which was commenced by Miss Jarman who played a pianoforte solo and who afterwards officiated as accompanist. Miss Brown, soprano, rendered "Annie Laurie ' and was so well received that she gave "Coming through the Rye" as an encore. Mr J Willett, the now well known tenor sang "Thorn," following whom came little Doris Welsby with" Are you anybodie's little girl in particular." Mr Clay and Miss Rigby, with "The Trumpeter "and "The fairest lily," respectively, were also warmly applauded. Mr William W Whittle humorist, was very cordi- ally received, his "Country Curate " demand- ing an encore which be obligingly satisfied with "A trip to Douglas " introducing " The boss of the house" and My wife's gone away," scoring a further success. Part 11. consisted of `• My dreams,' by Mr Willett ; " Spring is here " by Miss Rigby ; " Don't go down the mine Daddy," by Doris Welsby ; "Little Princess," by Miss Brown ; and perhaps the choicest musical item of the evening, the famous duet "Watchman what of the night," by Messrs Willett and Clay. Mr Whittle gave more food for laughter with "Jim " and " The Irish walk." The pro- gramme was a good one throughout, and every item was rendered in finished style. By their hearty applause, and frequent demands for more, the aadience showed not only their approval of the artists themselves,but an appreciation of the kindness of the Co-operative Society in providing the village with a really enjoyable evening. SONS OF TEMPERANCE FRIENDLY SOCIETY.—In con- nection with the Lymm Division of the above society a public meeting was held in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom on Wednesday night. Mr. G. L. Wellford in taking the chair stated that though not a member of the Society he took a sympathetic interest in it.—ln the course of a lengthy speech Mr Walter Davies, the chief secretary of the order, and president of the National ' Conference of Friendly Societies, said that it must not be taken that he was speaking politically. Regarding the five principles of the Insurance Bill, he said that in relation to the first point—" The need of the Bill " many friendly societies had said that there was no need of it, but on inquiry he found thatonly one-third of the population were members of friendly societies and any keneficent force which could only reach one-third of the people who needed help, and that help would be given by the Insurance Bill. He commended the financing of the Bill, dn ening upon the subject especially as it related to friendly societies. He said that by the formation of the Insurance Act Committee (Health), thiee fifths of which would be composed of friendly society members, would set up a new health standard for the country, and that the Bill would reduce at least an average two days sick rate per person. The second point, he said, was a further improvement on the first, namely, the use of the best doctors and medicines. Under the new cmclitions a person would not b 3 tied to a club doctor, but could choose the one he had most faith in whose name appeared on the approved list. If points 1 and 2 did not cure a person, then point 3 would be used, i.e., Nature, through the instrumentality of sana- toria. Friendly societies can do very little for their members in this respect, and no the Bill would be a boon. In regard to point 4 —maternity I eneflts— friendly societies are given the right to say how the 303. per week (which is given under the Act) should be spent. He pointed out that if the money were given direct to the wife, there was a possible chance for the husband to get it and spend it round the corner. Owing to lack of time Mr. Davies could net speak on the fifth heading of —lnvalidity.—A vote of thlnks, proposed by Mr. J. B. D. Smith and seconded by Mr. C Moss, was accorded the lecturer.</p>
      </div>
