      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 10 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>" NEVER FREE FROM IRRITATION."</head>
        <p>" NEVER FREE FROM IRRITATION."</p>
        <p>Her Face was so Disfigured she was</p>
        <p>Ashamed to be seen.</p>
        <p>ZA3I-BtiK THE SURE CURE FOR ECZEMA.</p>
        <p>Miss Rebecca Whitfield, a farmer's daughter, was so tortured with itching sores cn her head, body, and limbs that she was never free from irritation until a wonderful Zam-Buk cure ended her distressing plight. Searr End Farm, Twistleton, Ingleton, Yorkshire, where Miss Whitfield lives, is the well-known .` half- way-house "round the popular Ingleton Waterfalls. " A pimple en my scalp started the eczema," Miss Whitfield told a " Lancaster Observer " repenter. " Rubbing my itchy skin caused other pimples to appear, until there was quite a mass of these dry inflamed sores on my arms, legs, back, ears, and fore- head. My skin 1 urned and itched dreadfully. and I was so badly disfigured that I was really ashamed to be seen.</p>
        <p>" No matter what I did, I couldn't get relief, and two years after the cczema started I was worse than ever. _ .</p>
        <p>"Luckily, I now read in Zam-Buk Green Book ' of cases like mine which Zam-Buk had cured. My father made a special journey to Lancaster for some Zam-Buk and Zam-Buk Medicinal Soap. Having cleansed my sores with Zam-Buk Soap, my mother smeared the balm all over them. My skin was instantly soothed, and I got more relief than I had known since the eczema began. " Zam-Buk caused the scaly skin to dry up and fall away, and grew new healthy tissue in its place. My limbs and back got better first, my head taking longer. But Zanr-Bok finally removed every sore, and left my head and body sweet and clean." It is Zam-Buk alone which is daily performing wonderful cures like that of Miss Whitfield's and other equally as stubborn cases of eczema, ringworm, bad legs, poisoned sores, piles, etc. Coarse home- made salves are worse than useless; they are dangerous. Zam-Buk, which is soothing and antiseptic as well as healing, is sold only in sealed packages bearing the trade mark " Zam-Buk " clearly printed on the outside. Zam-Buk can only be obtained in is. 10., 2s. 9d., or 4s. fid. sizes. All imitations are worthless.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>St. John's Guild.</head>
        <p>St. John's Guild.</p>
        <p>On Monday ev-e;;ing-ihe-Q--ra-e.rs of the Guild listened to a most interesting paper by Miss Slader on "Hymns and their writers". Many old aLd new favourites were mentioned, and in most cases a brief sketch of the author was given. The broad platform of our hymnology was shown to be occupied by Established and Free, High and Low Churchmen, Quakers and Unitarians of various nationalities. Several appreciative contributions were made to the discussion, note beirg particularly made of the vital and sacred relationship existing between certain hymns and tunes Miss Davey, accompanied by Mr. A. G. Lewsey, rendered three or four of the hymns, and she and Miss Slader were accorded the best thanks of the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>WIDNES POSTMAN SENTENCED.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes on Monday Martin Edward Pierce (20), postman, who had been out on bail, pleaded guilty to having stolen postal packets at VVidnes, on August 29th and September sth, 1911 Mr. Nicholson, who prosecuted, said the letters mentioned came in the Widnes office and prisoner retained them. He was the son of a tradesman, and applied to the Post Office towards the and of 1910, and was given work as a casual postman at Christmas and at 'callous short periods of 1911, amounting to about three months in all,during which time he was in receipt 017 s lid per week for working two and a half hour's per day. A postal inquiry agent made investigations, and prisoner, in reply to his questions said "It was me that took the letters. I must have Oren a fool, that is the:size of it. I filled in the orders and cashed them myself. I did not need the money, for I could have done without it." His Lordship said postal servants were in a position of great trust, and it was highly important that letters, whether they contained valuables or not, should be properly delivered. Prisoner must go to goal for two months in the second division.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>WIDNES LICENSING SESSIONS.c7/1</head>
        <p>WIDNES LICENSING SESSIONS.c7/1</p>
        <p>The annual licensing sessions for the Widnes Division were held in Widnes on Tuesday. Superintendent Strickland, in his report, stated that the district contained ninety licensed houses the same as in the previous year. No licensee had been proceeded against and the houses generally had been well conducted. The number of persons proceeded against during the year for offences under the Licensing Act was 271, against 061 last year, being a decrease of 90. The population of Widnes was 33,436, giving an average of 382 persons to each licensed house. In 1907 the number of persons proceeded against reached the high figure of 456. The Chairman (the Mayor) congratulated the police and the public generally upon the excellent police report. With regard to the licences of the Manchester House (Mrs Sha mon) and the Railway Inn (Mrs. Thompson), the bench had agreed to adjourn these until the next sessions, and instructed the superintendent to give notice of objection or, the ground of redundancy. The other licences were all granted. RUNCORN TUGS DAMAGED. The tugboats employed for the purpose of towing narrow boats and fiats along the Bridgewater Canal from Runcorn to Manchester found it a matter of difficulty to force a passage through the ice during the earlier part of the week. On Tuesday the tug Salford had a plate punctured by the ice at Barrow's Bridge, Preston Brook, and sank. Captain Faulkner and the two "hands" got ashore safely. The tug Burford has also received damage to her plating, while the rudder of the Norton was damaged, and those of the Dunham and Walton badly strained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>PENAL SERVITUDE FOR ARSON. At the Liverpool Assizes on Monday George Barret was charged with arson at Rainford, near St. Helens. Mr. Lindon Riley prosecuted, and said prisoner set fire to a hay stack on Sunday week and went to the nearest police station and told what he had done. His lordship in sentencing prisocer to three years' penal servitude said prisoner had been twice previously convicted of arson, on the last occasion being given five years' penal servitude. ROMAN CATHOLICS AS IMPERIALISTS. MALTA'S OBJECT LESSON.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Father Hayden,S.J. of Lowe Houle Church St. Helene, on Monday night, delivered a lecture at St. Austin's Church, Thatto Beath, on " Malta : One of the Most Loyal Possessions of the Catholic Church and the British Crown." He said the tight little island had just provided a much-needed object lesson for all the great nations by which it was surrounded. Cardinal Bourne and the King and Queen had during the last few weeks visited Malta, and onoe again had the inhabitants of that picturesque place proved that they were true to the teaching of their apostle St. Paul, that not even from an angel from heaven would they accept another goEpsl than the ore he had preached to them.</p>
        <p>Right well did the Maltese demonstrate that it was not only easy, but perfectly natural to he at one and the same time devoted sons of the Holy Church and devoted subjects of the British Empire. Here, at least. there was no clashing of interests between Church and State. God had his rights and Cmsar his. It would be long before King and Queen and Cardinal forgot their intensely enthusiastic reception in Malta, and the rousing scenes of popular rejoicing that met their eyes on every side. And it was a good thing that hundreds of officers and men of the French squadron, which came to salute King George at Malta, and who were so royally entertained by the men of our fleet and the inhabitants should see with their own eyes that stead- fast loyalty to the faith did but deepen devotion to the flag. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>TALKS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>TALKS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>[Br A FAMILY DOCTOR]</p>
        <p>ADENOIDS.</p>
        <p>rn these days of medical inspection ot school children we hear much of a complaint called adenoids. Adenoids are made of the game tissue as the tonsils, and they grow up at the back of the nose. They cannot be seen by looking in the mount unless a special mirror is used, which can only be done by a skilled doctor. But tonsils and adenoids practically always exist together; so that when one sees large tonsils it is almost certain that there are large ade- noids,</p>
        <p>:o: EVILS OF MOUTH-BREATIIINO.</p>
        <p>The importance of breathing through the nose cannot he over-estimated, and as ado- noid prevent the ai from pass- ing freely through the nose the harm they do is very. grievous. Mouth-breathing leads to , catarrh, bronchitis, sore-throat, snoring, and even to mental backwardness. I will tell. you of a real experience of mine in a school. The class I was examining had been divided into two divisions, an upper and a lower; the upper contained the brighter children, the lower the backward ones. When I came to examine the two diyi6ions I found that none of the brighter children had adenoids, and that no less than eight out of twenty-two of the backward children had enlarged tonsils and adenoids. So if your child is backward, and you get bad re- ports from the school, and he is not moved into a higher standard at the end of the year, then you must be careful to ascertain that the ears, nose, and throat are quite clear.</p>
        <p>: o :</p>
        <p>THE EAR'S BACK-DOOR.</p>
        <p>Of course, there are cases where the ade- noids are enormous, Ca'SOS where they are of medium size, and cases where they are very small. In the latter group the doctor may say that an operation is not necessary at present, but that the child should be periodically examined and operated on if the adenoids get larger. You must not for- get the anatomy I have often• taught you. Remember there is a back-door into the ear from the throat; the ear-hole on the outside of the head that we can all see is the front door, and of course we must keep that clear by washing and occasionally syringing the wax out if necessary; but the back-door, which we cannot see, because it is tucked away at the back of the nose, is the really important door; if that is blocked by ade- noids it starts a catarrh, then an inflamma- tion, and finally a discharge of matter breaks through the drum, and we know the harm is done. The treatment of deafness and diseases of the ear is of 4-.1 not directed to the ear at all, but to the throat; remove the growth from the throat, cleanse the _back of the nose, free the back-door so that air can get in and Out, and the ear gets well of its Own accord.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>TO RELIEVE EARACHE.</head>
        <p>TO RELIEVE EARACHE.</p>
        <p>Earache may often be relieved by apply- ing a Mister the nize of a shilling on the bone behind the ear. The blister (Thould be left on long enough to raise a watery bleb, not merely to make the skin red. At the same time put hot water or a few drops of hot oil in the car. The most convenient way to do this is to put a teaspoon into boiling water to make it hot, and then pour the Oil out of the bottle into the hot spoon. Leave it in the spoon for a minute and then pour it into the ear. This may be repeated.</p>
      </div>
