      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <p>Musical Society's Concert. Sir,—The somewhat extraordinary account of lase Wednesday's concert published last Saturday in ths columns of your contemporary, the " Guardian," it chiefly remarkable for the very unfriendly tone which extends throughout the entire article with regard to the society's chorus. Whatever may have been the faults in the singing on that occasion—and I do not deny there were faults (for whoever heard a perfect performance?) - there is no doubt that the reporter for That journal has given a very unjust and inaccurate account of them. This perhars is not of much con- sequence as far as those members of the public and aupporters of the society who were present at the concert is concerned, as the majority of .these will be able to form their own opinion on the matter. But there may be others who have read the article, and to such I think a very unfair and misleading impression as to the society's choral capabilities must have been conveyed. Criticism should be governed .by know- ledge, but it sometimes happens that opinions are made to act as substitutes. Your ccnumporary s critic " Mnsicus " begins by stating that last week's riorogtamme (which was mainly composed of works by Parry, Grieg, Mencelssohn and Rossini) was only just redeemed from being " abso- lutely commonplace." Surely this is a rather curious opinion. Then he goes on to say that "the thanks of the virtuosi are due to the society for the opportunity of hearing an artiste, '&amp;c" Who are the Warrington virtuosi? Is" usieus " one? Doc s he know the meaning of the word? The chorus is accused of making "an unpardonable lapse" in pro- nouncing "England" as though it were spelt "Ingland." And yet I venture to say that the mem- bers of the chorus were perfectly correct. Any other pronunciation than that given by them of the word would strongly savour of pedantic priggishness. No- one shou'd object to fair, honest, and reasonable criticism, but when criticism is allowed to degenerate into a conglomeration of carping abuse (I had almost written a stronger term), surely it is time to protest. I do not fail to notice the appreciation of the work done by the orchestra, but the unpleasant tone adopted and complete want of sympathy displayed. in connec- tion with the chorus, is a matter wh:ch wi I, I am sure, be resented by every member of the society. In these days of cheap and light entertainments it , is a sufficiently difficult thing to maintain any tnfer- I prise that works mainly on artistic lines; but if in addition to the apathy of the pubmi, rosical societies must also contend again,t the uncalled-for and unjust -antagonism of the press te difficulty will become still more zericus.—Yours, FRANK H. CROSSLEY. Conducter Warrington Musical &amp;clay. Liverpool, February 51h 1912. Does Efficiency Benefit' the Workers? Sir,—Mr. Parker's letter in your last issue is certainly gratifying to myself, seeing that he practi- cally throws away his original contention as untenable, that owing to competition the workers have received the benefit of efficiency in production. Ho says, " Thus, the fruits of efficiency have been largely monopolised,and what has been diffn,:ed is proportion- ately small, and much less than is just. Now in this lam in general agreement, etc." To those who have followed this corre,ponderce, it will be quite cbvious to see the change in opinion that Mr. Parker has undergone, from the foregcirg statement to that owing to competition the workers had received the benefits of efficiency in production. I do not dispute thrtt the worker's position is relatively better than say sixty years ago. But I hope Mr. Parker will forgive me, whose age and experience and opportunit'es have been more slender than his own, if I say that that is an example of Mr. Parker's loose thinking, For it does not truthfully follow that because the productive capacity of this nation has increased, and the worker's position is a little better than formerly, that the latter is the result of the former. I put it plainly enough in my last letter. I said that if cumpetition in trade has banded the benefits of production over to the worker, com- petition for work amongst the workers must have reduced the wages of the workers to the bare cost of living, thus negativing any reduction in price of com- modities. I did net say it was true, as Mr. Parker wished to infer, because he did not seem to grasp the thread of my argument. But what I wished to point out was that what applied in one case must apply in another. Even now, I do not believe that the worker's position has been improved because of increased capacity in the produc- tion. Just as I pointed out that the owners of the implements of production had frustrated the law of competition, so have the workers frustrated to a small degree tbe law of competition for work. The obvious benefits the workers have received are mainly due, I believe, to the work of trade unions who have</p>
        <p>organised a great portion of the workers' labour power to increase its value. Just as Mr. Parker points out that we now have a better sanitary system, and again pointing this out as a result of greater efficiency, I believe this benefit is only incidental because the more educated upper and middle classes could plainly see that in order to save their own skins from contagious diseases, improved sanitary arrangements must be used. A glaring example of this contention may be instanced in the position of fever and small pox hospitals and the Infirmary. The wealthier of our population have made ample provision for dealing - with contagious diseases by placing all c:st upon the public. There is no slackness of funds to hamper the work of dealing vviih contagious diseases. No, this is the middle and upper classes' concern and proves that they have a bit of commonsense by socialising the cost of eradicating contagious diseases. Take the case of the Infirmary. The middle and upper classes don't use it. It is no concern of theirs, so they tun all their eni.eavours in making the workers pay for it by stopping pennies a week or a month out of their starvation wages, or giving to collections in theatres, uocessions, .&amp;c.—again proving that their patriotism is very often mixed up with their cash. Another point of disagreement between myself and Mr. i Parker s that I do not accept the value of his C 330 by judging the point of controversy from the position of the workers of sixty years ago. Professor Thorold Rcgers who has had exceptional opportunity of studying the workers' conditions in past centuries states the M 283 of the people to-day " are more destitute, whose homes are more squalid, whose means are more uncertain, whose prospects are more hopeless than those of the poorest serfs of the middle ages, and the meanest drudges of mediteval cities." That is where I Ole my stand. Some of tle so called , -primitive trit es do now satisfy all their material wants ' with about 4 or 5 months labour a year. Someone might say : " But they have not any intellectual attainments. They do not read or write," and so forth. My contention is that the first foundation of a race is its physique. For our existence 41epends upon cur physical conditions and any nation not possessing physically virile people is a grossly ignoranerace, not possessing the elementary knowledge of life, in spite of the fact that they may possess tha medium of getting all the knowledge required. If we judge England by this standard, the so-called primitive man is fav superior to us. (This ' - by the way.) This discussion was originated by the statement of Mr. Parker that the worker had only to • produce more in order to get a better standard of living. Then Mr. Parker went on to prove to his Own' satisfaction that the worker had got at least some of the benefits.lle now says that whether he has or not is irrelevant to the point. This point can only be judged • by economic tendencies of to day and not of happen- ings ten, twenty, or sixty years ago. And nobody • doubts that to-day the workers' position is becoming f: worse. What concerns us, is that it is by our old friend efficiency. Yes, I say, efficiency in production, for in spite of the general opinion I asseit that the trusts or combines are more ' efficient in the production of wealth than , the old small companies' each with its own plant, , machinery and general disorganisation and over- lapping. But for the life of me I cannot see where better organization in manufacture is going to benefit the worker. Mr. Parker may please himself whether he reads this 6: or not but my belief is stid that the evils from which 6: we suffer are due to private ownership of the means of production and any satisfactory 'solution can only come about by pecple owning the land and implements and working them for their own benefit. I certainly agree with Ur. Parker that we have had, ample opportunity for the discussion of this question and that we understand one another better, or rather that he understands myself better 230 W. And seeing that Mr. Parker is thinking of dis- continuing this correspondence I might not have</p>
        <p>" al _ 11r11-iuil 'l'44 til 74.7101 V 4. tarp 5 I Ecticr to the NEDITOR L The</p>
        <p>ig ,</p>
      </div>
