      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, February' 10, 1912</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, February' 10, 1912</p>
        <p>mother opportunity cf thanking him for giving his time in discussing these questions with an individual of my standing. My opportunities for understanding these questions have certainly not been as great as those of Mr. Parker and although we may disagree upon matters economic, it gives one great pleasure to know there are men like Mr. Parker who are willing to di=cuss these problems with men at the other end of the stick. John Raskin says" Above all, a nation cannot last as a money-making mob ; it cannot with impunity, it cannot with existence, go on despising literature, despising science, despising art, despising nature, despising compassi.n, and concentrating its soul on pence." Your &amp;c., LABOR OMNIA [Correspondence on this subject must now cease.—ED." Ex. "j</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith's Bad Memory. Sir,—l find Mr. Harold Smith has been saying some very funny things at the Parr Hall on January 23rd. He speaks about boycotting in Ireland, and from his speech he would have us believe that both himself and the party he belongs to are very much opposed to such a mean action as to ihjure any tradesman in business because he votes according to his own conscience. Yet strange to say, when the Licensing Bill was before the country, boycotting was advocated by some of these Tories, and I venture to say that if Mr. Harold Smith will only give himself time to think, he will at once have some idea as to whom I refer. Again we find Mr. Harold Smith speaking at Westhoughton. There he tells the people that when the Liberal patty came into office in DOG because they were independent of the Irish vote (he meant to say the Nationalist Party in the House cf Commons) that nothing was heard of Home Rule. Well I wonder if Mr. Harold Smith can remember the Leaders cf the Nationalist Party sending messages to almost every constituency in England, Scotland, and Wales advising all to vote for the Liberal Party ; was it not that they believed the Liberal Party had promised to give them ' Home Rule, and they had implicit confidence that they would keep their promise ? So when the Liberals came into office in 1000 they commenced to work and although Ur. Harold Smith speaks so disrespectfully of the Liberal Government let me remind him that hundreds cf thousands will have to thank God that such a government ever came into office. They at once turned their attention to those who had borne the burden and heat of the day, and instead of many ( f them ending their days in the Workhouse the Liberal Government gave to these people a pension, one which the Tory party sneered at. And we have an equal right in the future to pass such Bills as will be for the good of humanity,thrtugh the veto being taken out of the hands of the Lords and placed in the hands of those who are sent up to the livse of Commons by the will cf the peop'e.—Yours E. W. J. The Padgate Burial Incident.</p>
        <p>Sir, —I was very much surprised in reading the letter and the tone of the same unde.r the above heading in your issue on the 27th January. As one cf the stewards of Padgate Wesleyan Church, I will, with your permission' reply to "Parishioner" to refute his version of this true facts of the case, which is altogether misleading, and give ll ye the real facts as to what actually cccurred. I must confess it is a mo•t difficult ma'ter to reply te one who writes under a nom-de-plume, and has not the courage and manliness to sign his name. If he had only done this, one could have seen him personally to have enlightened his obscure mind on the subject, simply to have avoided any controversy on the matter in deference to the deceased's mother, who during these last few months has lost both husband and only son. But the tone of his letter is only adding insult to injury. There is no other alternative but by replying through the public pres. I say the deceased was a prominent Wesleyan worker, having been our organist for over three years and also taken part in the different servicss throughout the week. My duties brought me constantly in contact with him, and let me say that his kindly dispcsiticn in all his work at the various meetings won for himself the respect and est,ena of every member of our csn- gregation, in fact, in other words—to know him was to love him. S 3 much for not being a prominent Wesleyan worker. Moreover, his mother has been ass Iciated with our Church at Martinscroft for a number of years and was a regular attender there. I don't dispute the facts of him being organist for the Church of England Communicants' Guild during the week. Let it be said to the credit of the lad that this goes to show his broad mindedness in religions matters. I am also aware that the Vicar had administered Sacrament to him two days before his death—all credit to him for doing so. If the Vicar had only shown the same Christian charity to our respected Minister at the interment, there would have been co need for any correspondence whatever, and two denominations would have gone on working amicably together as hitherto. But having insulted our respected pastor he has also insulted the members of his fleck. One wonders whether we are living in the twentieth century age or have gone back again to the middle ages, when religious bigotry was so rampant. " Parishoner " says the Wesleyan minister declined to assist at the service inside, simply because he could not assist at the graveside. Let me give the reason for that refusal. During my visits to the deceased during his illness his mother freely'expressed tome personally the kindness of the members of our Church and also the members of the Church of England people for their kindness in this—her sore trial. On the Friday morning our respected minister (Mr. Morgan) went down to console with the mother on the Ices of her son. She naturally expressed a wish that Mr. Morgan should bury her son, which he consented to r o, and also arranged to have the body brought to our church to hold a short service before proceeding to the Church ground to bury. On the way home from Mrs. Dumbell's he met the Vicar and told him of the arrangements and gave him (verbally) the forty-eight hours notice which is legal in these cases. The Vicar expressed his disappointment as he said he had , administered sacrament to him on the Tuesday previous and was annoyed at not being consulted with regard to the funeral, which was entirely at the discretion of the mother. When Mr. Morgan saw this—in order to avoid any unpleasantness he generously cffered to give up our service at the church before the interment and take the body on to the Vicar's church, and have service there instead, providing that he took part in conjunction with the Vicar at the Church and at the graveside, as requested by the mother, but the Vicar absolutely declined TO have anything more to do with it. I personally went down to see Mrs. Dumbell on the Friday night on a church matter, not knowing any- thing about the arrangements made, when she confided to me how upset she was that this bother should ccme about, and asked my opinion on the matter. My advice to her was to see the Vicar on the Saturday morning and told him Mr. Morgan's compromise was still open if he would agree to it. He consented to do so, We clond our school early in the afternom as •ur young people were so anxious to show their respect and esteem towards one whom they bad learned to love. I may say that the church was crowded, fully three fourths of our people being present. It is at this point where the unpleasantness arcse, after we had so freely given up our service and transferred it to the church for the sake of unity. Before the funeral cortege arrived at the church, Mr. M. rgan saw the Vicar and asked him what part he was taking in the service. The Vicars' reply was —We will both go down to the gates ace meet the cortege there, and you can read the lesson in elle Church (which is open for any laymen to do). Mr. Morgan then asked him what part he should take at the graveside. The Vicar replied—" none. I shall take the full service there. " Mr. Morgan asked if that was his final decision, and • the Vicar said it was. Then Mr. Morgan without entering into any argument bid the Vicar "Good afternoon," and quietly retired, feeling in his own mind that be had been taken a mean advantage of. Now, sir, these are the true facts of the above case and I can vouch for their authenticity. And more- over they can be supplemented by our minister and my co-steward. We are content to leave it in the hands of the pub!ie as to who is the aggrieved party. Yours truly,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>T. B. DUCK NORTH. Green Lawn, Padgate. ....—_.__ MR. A. H. CROSFIELD</head>
        <p>T. B. DUCK NORTH. Green Lawn, Padgate. ....—_.__ MR. A. H. CROSFIELD</p>
        <p>Entertains Liberal Leaders.</p>
        <p>Last evening Mr . A-.--1-17C-rosfiel-d--e-n-tertained prominent members of the Liberal Association at dinner at the Patten Arms Hotel. There was an attendance of about forty and a very pleasant evening was spent. The usual toasts were honoured, the speakers including Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Major Goren, Mr. C. E Parker, Alderman Tinnion, Councillor J. T. Locker, Mr. S. Rigby Armitage. Mr. J. G.Withinshaw, Mr. John Upson and Mr. Mark Howarth.</p>
