      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>LICENSING SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>LICENSING SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CLUBS ON LICENSED PREMISES.</p>
        <p>MAGISTRATES' DISAPPROVAL</p>
        <p>There was cmiparatively little business at the annual licensing sessions for the County Borough of Warrington which were held at the Police Court yesterday. The Mayer (Councillor Dr. Joseph) pre,ided and the other justices present were:— Aldermen J. Smethurst, A. Bennett and I) Tinni,m, Councillors 11. Roberts and C. Jackson Holmes, Messrs. J. NV. Hayward, W. Roberts, A. J. Jolley, S. Wright, W. Furness, G. Piling, Edwin Woods, W. T. Williams, T. Pemberton and T. Locker.</p>
        <p>"UNDESIRABLE PRACTICES."</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CLUBS ON LICENSED PREMISES.</p>
        <p>The Mayor said it had been brought to the notice of the Bench that tontine or slate clubs were held on certain licensed premises in the botough. Inquiries were made by the Chief Constable at the request of the sub-committee and a list SVi,S submitted showing that clubs composed wholly of women were held in four licensed houses and that in fourteen licensed houses deductions wete made from the suhscriptions cf club members to be spent in drink. The sub-com- mittee ccaisidereel these practices undesitable and passed a resoluti,n tecommet.ding an expression of disapproval theieof and for their prompt discontir.u- ar ce ; which the Bench adopted at a general meeting. The Bench were glad to say that they had received assurances that the clut3s complainel LI and the practices had now ceased. THE CHIEF CONSTABLE'S REPORT. WARRINGTON COMPARED WITH OTHER TOWNS.</p>
        <p>In his fifth annuli report, the Chief Constable stated he numbsr of licence holders in U e Borough is 190. The figures show a reduction of two as composted nith last yea,, two off:isences having been given up in conskltration of an applicatkn belt g granted to remove a licence f om 17, Gaskeli-street to 60, Marshbouse lane. At the adjourred Lies nsirg Sessions last .ear the " 114 his Angel" ;1 house Buttermarket-street and the " Brewer's Arms " beer- house, Winn ick-street, were referred to the Com- pensation Authority, but the compensation h ts not yet b€ en p dd. The population of the Boron, h according to the census of 1911 was 72,178, consequently there 15 one licensed house ti every 370 83 inhabitants. 1 With reference to the disthkenness, there is a decrease of three in the number of persons proceeded against as compared with the previous year. The figures for the past tilts e years are as follows :--- 1911, 477 mal a and 101 females, tntal 581 ; 1910, 470 maks ai d 114 females, Ltal 581 ; 1999, 331 Imales and 101 females, tctal 185. Of the 581 yersniss proceeded against, 501 were : apprehended and 17 summers( d, 59 were discharged land 522 convi. tei. The days of the week o s which drunkenness occurred are as folows :—Sunday, 76 ; Monday, 80 ; Tuesday, 05 ; Wednesday, 44 ; Thurs- day, 29 ; Friday, 40 ; Saturday, 238. I A ccmparative table gives the slumber of persons I proceeded against for atunkenmss in ten northern towns for the 'sear ending December 31st. Middles- borough heads the list with 12.39 per 1,000, Warrington coming next with 804. The lowest averaks is Burnley's, 2.6. St. Helens is 4 79, and Wigan, 3 70. '1 here are 13 clubs iegitered in the borough. No complaint has been made to the police sgairst them during the year. During the year one licensee was proceeded against as follows, viz: - Joseph Iktter, Kirg's Arms Public , House' Puttermarket-street, three convictions on Apsil 25th, 1911. Having regard to these convictions against the tenant of the house at the time, I have caused the present licensee to be served with notice of objection to the renewal of his licence. Nearly 2,000 surprise visits cf inspection were made by the police to licensed premises during the twelve months, and on a few occasions the attention of the licensees had to be drawn to minor irregu- larities, but generally the houses have been well conducted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>LICENCE OBJECTED TO.</head>
        <p>LICENCE OBJECTED TO.</p>
        <p>RE-ARRANGEMENT SCHEME FOR BETTER- MARKET-STREET. Mr. Steel, on behalf of the Licensed Victuallers' Association, applied for the renewal of the ale-house licences standing in the names of the members. • ,• The Chief Constable said he objected to the renewal of the licence of the King's Arms,Buttermarket-street, on the grounds stated in his report. Mr. Browne, who represented the licensee and Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. (iesees.),— stated that he admitted the not:ce of objection' the conviction and the circumstances, but he proposed to address the Court at some length. The Clerk : It has been decided to take the case at the adjourned seesions. Mr. Browne : Is there any advantage in that? Notice has been served by the Chief Constable -on the instructions of the sub committee, I have no doubt. The Chief Constable: That is not so. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne : It is hardly material whether it is or not. The Mayor: The magistrates unanimously decided on that course being taken. I dou't know whether they are prepared to reconsider the question. Mr. Browne: I am only going to repeat what I said twelve months ago—that there was a scheme in hand with regard to the Buttermarket-street houses which, as soon as a certain house could be purchased, would be submitted to the Bench for their concurrence. There is an obstacle in the way of purchasing that house which has not yet been removed. We are hoping every month to get it cut of the way and in that case we should be prepared to .come before the Bench with a full scheme fr the re-arrangement of the houses in that neighbourhood. Ihe Mayor : The adjournment might help you in that case.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne : It is rather to shot. If it were for four months, perl aps it would. The Mayor: The casgistrates adhere to thtir decision. The other licences sse:e rerewed. The Bench also renewed the beerhouse and off- licences.</p>
        <p>"SNOOKER POOL" PROHIBITED. • Mr. Browne applied, on behalf of Mr. Metcalfe, Jubilee Buildings, for the removal cf certain restric- tions in connection with his billiard licence. His object was that "snooker poi " should be allowed. There was no harm in the game and it was played in all the cafes and clubs. He asked for the restriction \gainst "plot or pyramiis " to be withdrawn. The Bench declined to remove the restriction. Mr. Browne : I suggest the cafés ought to be inder the same conditions. NO SINGING AT THE PICTURE PALACES. Mr. T. E. Knowles, on behalf of Mr. George Houghton, manager of Andrews' Pictures Limited, applied for permission for a song to be given during the interval at each house. At present the licence was confined to instrumental music.</p>
        <p>agai 1</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne: I will support the application if your worships will grant it to the others. The applicat:on was refused. OFF-LICENCE FOR THE BREWERY. Mr. J. Longland applied on behalf of Alderman TiT Bolton for a beer "off" licence to he attached to Messrs. Bolton's Brewery, Mersey-street. He stated 1 that at present the alderman held a wholesale dealer's licence under which be was entitled to sell not less than 41 gallons or six dozen reputed quarts. Ile could supply bottled beer in any quantities at the licensed house which adjoined the brewery, but he desired that in future all orders for bottled stuff should be received at the office and dealt with direct at the brewery. He was prepared to give an under- taking that he would not supply less than a dozen bottles at a time. The object of the application was not to obtain grea'er facilities for the sale of drink, but merely: as a matter of business convenience. The Chief Constable cfirred no objection to the application. The magistrates' clerk stated that the other breweries had similar licences.</p>
        <p>The application wat granted. RAILWAY REFRESHMENT ROOMS. . _</p>
        <p>Mr. Pinfold, on bsholf of ths Cheshire- LiC-1.1"; Com- mittee, applied for a reduction of the compensation levy in respect of the refreshment rooms at the Central station. Ile submitted figures showing that while the sale of intoxicants was decreasing that of food and minerals was increasing. The refreshment room would never, by any chance, be referred for compensa- tion and therefore it would seem that, on the I princip'e of elementary justice, the committee was I entitled to a greater concession than was at present I allowed.</p>
        <p>Mr. Tait made a similar application on behalf of the L &amp; N.-W. Railway Company. The Bench fixed the levy at the same amount as before—two-thirds of the maximum. ADJOURNED SESSIONS. The adjourned licensing sessions werefixed March '</p>
        <p>‘CosoutTs' ra ' Transparent Cement for Maas, China, U. 6d. and is., poet free.—Adrrt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>LYMM GRAMMAR SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>LYMM GRAMMAR SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Opening of the Extension.</p>
        <p>Undoubtr dip the must interesting ceremony which has taken place at Lymrn kr a considerable time was at the opening on Saturday afternoon of tbe new buildings recently added to the Grammar School. Much interest was centred in the event which was arranged in coil- junction with the annual prize distribution, and a large number assembled in the new ball. After many interest- ing spef.ches had been delivered, the company were invited to inspect the new buildings, which afford faciliti,s of the most up-to-date and commodious charact, r and were greatly admired by all. The old block of buildings includes the boys' maneal and work room, laboratory and lecture theatre, cloak molls lavatr r:es, office and school library, sssembly hall and gymnasium. A door on the \Vest side of the schoolroom leads into the bb c'e of new buildings. On the ground floor, south side are situated classrooms for Form IL and the Kindergarten, senior mistress' room and assistant mistresses' common room, cloak room, &amp;c. On the north side are classrooms for Form V. and IVa., girls cloakroom, lavatories, &amp;c. The west entrance is used as the girls entracce. Two spacious staircases at each end of the luilding lead to the upper floor, on the north side of which thera is a spacious and beautifully fitted out art room, store room for stationery, cooking pantry aud an adeitienal classroom for Forms IVb. and Hi., and a commodious coo Ling room. All the classrooms are fitted out with the latest conveniences and improvements. All the rooms are DOW completed and in use with the excer tion of the manual work room and the cooling room. 1 he furnishing of the former will be proceed. d sriib at once, and it is hoped that the latter will also be shortly ready. The buildings were designed by Mr. 11. Beswick, the county architr ct, and erected by Messrs. Robert Carlyle and Son, 0:d Trafford. The furnishing, &amp;c., has been chitfly carried out by the North of England Furnishing Co., Darlington. and the Educational Supply Association, London. The manual work or room is being furnish d by Messrs. B adford and Co., Ardwick, and much subsidiary work has been done by Mr. H. Saville, of Lymrn. The arduous labours of organisation, supervision and 'Manc' e have been carried I out by a sub-committee consis'lug ofltev G B Thurston</p>
        <p>St. Andrew's University; Foundation Bursary of £2O per annum and Carnegie Bursary of £l5 per annum, St. Andrew's University; *Doris Brookes, London University Matriculation ; Neville Shingler, 2nd Division Clerkship, Civil Service ; Anita Pearce, Board of Education Bursary; Eric Edwards, Preliminary Examination Manchester Technical School. ...</p>
        <p>Oxford Lt;c-al Examinations.—Senior Certificates : Anita Pearce, Maud Nuttall, Freda Hankinscn. Junior Certificates : Ada Joynson, Walter Jones. Royal Drawing Society--The work sent up by the following was commended :-2nd Class : N Schmidt, C Pinkstone, M Nuttßll, J Broadbent, H Ackerley, Jones, F Wardell, F Hankinsen, E Edwards, A Pearce, 3rd Class : W Jones, R Porter, F Wardell, C Nuttall, F Healey. 4th Class : H Worthington, T Wyatt, A Brown, 13 Bradbury, C More, A Coiling, it Coiling. *Pupils who have recently left the school.</p>
        <p>The. Chairman gave an interesting address, in which he said he was sure they would all appreciate what they as governors felt, that that was a red letter dey for education in Lymm. It was a matter which could not ba expressed in words. It was far too deep for words When they looked forward to the future, what na'ght be the outcome of those new buildings atd a first class secondary school, words could not describe how they (the committee) felt. It was no daily or annual occurrence, but it was something that would bear fruit in the generations to come. That school fulfilled a double purpose. A secondary education school like that in their district was a place for education tor those who were not in the habit of ging to elementary schools. They had a large population in Lymm of those whose children would not go to en elemen- tary school. A tchool like Lyrnin Grammar School provided a fist-class education. The speaker then dealt at some length with the advantages asscciated with thst school. Every member of it, both rich or poor, was received and treated in the same manner. Turning to the histotical part of the school the Chairman spoke of its earliest cloys and continued that for over 200 years, the Lymm Grammar School had recownisei that there should be places in the best school in the districts for those children who were too poor to pay for such education in the ordinary course. Three 1 years ago the Board of Education had been to , the schnol and said that they would recognise the school if they would get proper buildings. Then had come the meeting with the County Council. There was ti en a chance of the school having to be closed and f, r L 3 mm to lose for ever the advantage of having</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Mill GRAMMAR SCHOOL, ON WiIICII ovEn £6,000 HAS RECENTLY BEEN SPLNT.</head>
        <p>Mill GRAMMAR SCHOOL, ON WiIICII ovEn £6,000 HAS RECENTLY BEEN SPLNT.</p>
        <p>(chairman), Messra T J Ridgway (honorary clerk), S Gould, W G Groves, the Headmaster and Miss 'Tabbed' (senior mistresc). At the opening ceremony on Saturday the Rev. G. B. Thurston (chairman of the Governors) presided and was supported on the platform by Proftsior Gonner, of Liverpool, Mr. R. P. Ward (director of education for Cheshire), Revs. E. P. Wetherhead, and Grant Bird (chairman of the Higher Education Committee for Cheshire) the Hon. J. E. Cr, as, Messrs. Cec 1 11. S. Wilson (headmaster), G. L. Welford, W. G. Groves, A. Watkin, Mrs. Ridgway and Mrs. Call mder. The Chairman in his opening remarks offered everyone present,in the name of the governors a hearty welcome. He said they were performing a double function that afternoon. There was the prize distribution which should have taken p'ace in September, but the buildings were only halt finished and the headmaster thought it better to postpone that and combine it with the opening of the new buildings. Professor Gonner had con- sented to perform the opening ceremony as represen- tative of the County Council. (Loud applause). The Headmaster, in presenting his anuual report said the gathering was the largest rind most representative they had had there for some time. They deeply regretted the absence of Mr. Ridgway. There was no need for him (the speaker) to enlarge on what Mr. Ridgway had done for the school. On the other hand they were glad to welcome Mrs. Ridgway, who would take her husband's place. (Applause). Turning to his review of the year, the headmaster said that the year that had passed might be properly described as " business carried on as usual during elte.ations." Thanks to the loyal co-opera- tion of everyone the year had been a most pleasant one. The record had been one of quiet and steady wet k with highly satisfactory results. He cmgratulated those who had won prizes turd also commented upon the success which had attended the various efforts of scholars at that school in their walks of life. The most conspicuous ' feature of the year had been the improvement in the drawings in connection with the Echool. Everyone of I the drawings sent in to the exhibition from the school had received special commendation and in the local examinati:n they were very successful. They had in the early part of the year undergone an inspection at the hands of Mr. Deakin (Cambridge University Inspector) and his verdict on the school genet-, Ily was of a highly satisfactory nature. The membership of the school stood at one hundred and fifteen which was considerably in advance of anything they had previously attained. That was very satisfac- tory in itself. He felt confident that when the excel- lent advantages and improvements were better known that number would be icc-eased. They were situated in an excellent county. There were few counties in the country which could boast of such a County Education Committee as they had in Cheshire. (Hear, hear and applause.) They were desirous of having every educa- tional facility for the benefit of the pupils. (Applause). Professor Gonner who was received with much enthusiasm, before distributing the prizes said that speaking as a member of the Education Committee he was sure they were pleased with the very encouraging report of the headmaster. (Hear, bear.) It was full of promise for the future. Ile would congratulate the boys and eirls of the school, both those who had won prizes and those who had not been qaite so successful. He was sure they had all made progress. (Applause.) The Professor then distributed the prizes and certifi- cates to the scholars as follows :</p>
        <p>Form Prizes.—V, Anita Pearce; IVa, Ada Joynson ; IVrb, Ruth Willan ; 111, Jeannie Mcßride; 11, C More, It Coiling, Florence Whittle. Elocution Prizes.—W Jones, A Brown. History.—Carrie Picket9ne. English.—Lily Borrows. General Knowledge.—H Atkinson. Geography and German Prizes.—N Schmidt. French.—Ada Joynson. Arithmetic and Mathematics Prize.—Anita Pearce. Junior Arithmetic.—J Broadbent. Chemistry.—Maud Nuttall. Needlework.—Maud Nuttall. Needlework.—Freda Starkey, Mabel Champs, Mabel Priest ner. Holiday Work.—Ruth Willan, Beatrice Bradshaw, S Fallone.</p>
        <p>Monual Work.—l Jones-Parry, A Edwards. Senior Local Prizes.—Anita Pearce, Maud Nuttall, Freda Hankinson.</p>
        <p>Scripture and Drawing Prizes'.—Freda Hankinson. Headmaster's Prizes.—R Jones, Maud Nuttall. The list of the distinctions for 1910-11 is as follows : —*Oscar de .long, 2nd M.S. Examination, Manchester University; *Thomas Parr, Matriculation Examination,</p>
        <p>'hoto C. E. Ardern,</p>
        <p>assured to them a good secondary school in their midst. After the negotiations, however, a sound and reason- able understanding was arrived at with the result that they wore that day in possession of such fine and beautiful buildings. No one ocmld foretell w het was in store in the future. But it rested with the people of Lymrn, until they saw what the future was going to be to make the school a success.(Elear, hear.) When they reviewed the past they could not help but look forward with confidence to the future. (Aprause). Professor Conner then formally unlocked the door ' leading to the new buildings and afterwards delivered an interesting and educational address in which he said an enlargement of that character constituted an epoch in the life of the school. It marked off the fttore from the past. Those proceedings marked another step in the fulfilment of the great county scheme for secondary education. The local authority for education deter- mined to erect or adopt a certain number of schools in the county for the higher education of boys and girls. They were met by the plea that every place ought to have its school, and that every place had an equal demand. Very rightly it was skid that that would mean the development of a system of necessarily small schools, and they chose certain centres where the first grade could reasonably be expected to flourish. In order that the other districts might not be unduly treated, a resolution was passed and carried out that children coming from outside areas were entitled to ask for their railway fares. They had also carried out a system of scholarships for free admissions to the schools in the country. They had developed the system so that, on the one band, scholarships were given ta children already in the school, ani also to children who had been in the school some time ago could compete for more valuable scholarships carrying with them a maintenance grant. The fruit of that system was seen that day,and if it had not been for it they would not have been able to have spent such a considerable stun in the case of Lymm. There had been a time when the question of the continu- ance of that school had to be seriously considered. There were three schools in the county, he believed, about which the committee had thought seriously. The Lvaim Grammar School was one but now it had been enlarged and it was much better. 1 Continuing, the professor said that if there was an avet ue which should be free and open to all, it was the avenue of education. (Hear, hear.) Nothing would be a greater calamity, not even the comparing of the rich and poor than the dividing of the people into the two classes, the ignorant and the educated. Turning from the actual question of schemes and plans to the wider aspect of education, the Professor said that that function marked a very important epoch in the history of the township. A new centre for education had been erected and a new avenue for knowledge had been opened. Education stood fo, liberty, and lii erty was the power of the people of the country. He wanted all classes to pass from the ranks of ignorance into the ranks of knowledge and education. Education meant emancipation from the obstacles to progress, and advance into a new world of wider thought and a more liberal conception. It meaut also the opportunity to avail oneself or training for different kinds of practical opportunitiot, and different kinds of ways of earning cne's living. (Hear, hear.) Education was not the path of pre greas, but the path of success in the narrower as well as in the wider sense and an occasion like the present marked the emancipation from ignorance which fettered the past , and marked the progress of a freer and more liberal life which would manifest itself in the future. He did not think there was any fear in the democratic future of the country provided education and the means of knowledge were afforded to the democracy. (Applause). In conclusion, he said he did not think the township of Lymm would have anything so importent for many years to come as that day's ceremony. The future of the district like the future of other districts would be happier, brighter and more contented than it would otherwise have been. (Loud applause). Mrs. Ridgway proposed and Colonel Legh secom'ed a hearty vote of thanks to the Professor, which was sup- ported by the Rev. Grant Bird, and carried unanimously. The Chairman was also thanked, on the proposition of the Rev. G. L. Welford, seconded by Mr. A. Watkin. VIE DEDICATORY HYMN. The dedicatory prayer was offered by the Rev E P Wethered, and a dedicatory hymn was sung by the scholars and audience to the town " Lymm Grammar School," composed by Mr Oliver Cr Smith, the words being by Mr Cecil H Wilson, the headmaster of the school.</p>
        <p>The company then inspected the new buildings, and expressed their appreciation of the efforts of the com- mittee.</p>
