      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-02-10_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>ANCIENT ORDER OF FORESTERS COURT 430.</head>
        <p>ANCIENT ORDER OF FORESTERS COURT 430.</p>
        <p>A tea and social in connection with the above was held at the Blue Lion Inn, on Saturday last, about 60 members being present. After a substantial tea, which was provided by the host, Mr Lake, Bro Peter Peacock presided, and amongst others there were present :—District Chief Ranger Bro G W 11 Baines, D.S.C.S. Bro Roden, P.D.C.R's Bros J Chrimes, II James, Walter Smith, Trustees Bros Gregory, Jones and Smith, P.C.R.'s Bros F Taylor, II Roden and lErnest Clayton. Bro Peter Peacock in replying to the toast of the Ancient Order of Foresters, touched upon the Insur- ance Bill, in connection with which the High Court was to decide during the following week as to whether the Arcient Order of Foresters would become an approved society or not. He specially emphasised the importance of initiating as many members as possible, particularly as the bulk of the workers in the country will prefer te join some recognised society. He pointed out that the Ancient Order of Foresters had 1,300.000 members with funds amounting to nearly ten millions. After presentations were made to Bros. Halliwell, James, Pidgeon and Fowlers, Bro. John Chrimes, Secretary of Court 430 replying on behalf of the Court save a review of the past 50 years working, commenc- ing with 70 members and funds £BO, as against 380 members to-day, with funds amounting to £1,600.</p>
        <p>Bth.</p>
