        <p>Deafness A GREAT SUCCESS OF THE NEW INVENTION 'THE AKOU-VIBRA•/ ELECTRICAL SOUND WAVES THE AROU-VIBRA IS APPLIED DAILY AT THE ROOMS BY EXPERI- ENCED DEMONSTRATORS, OR AN INSTRUMENT IS ALLOWED OUT ON HIRE FOR HOME TREATMENT. IT IS QUITE SIMPLE TO USE, AND A THOROUGH LESSON HOW TO USE II Is GIVEN FREE. IT CURES DEAFNESS. THE AKOU-VIBRA IS UNIQUE. IT IS THE ONLY INSTRUMENT OF ITS KIND, AND THE EXTRAORDINARY PROOFS OF SUCCESSFUL CASES TESTIFY TO ITS CURATIVE POWERS : Every one of the following letters are from clients who have undergone the AKOU-VIBRA treatment. Every letter can le seen by request, besides hundreds of others. - -- TO THE DEAF. Tuebrook, Liverpool. Dear Sir,—l am very happy to say my one ear is cured, and I am more than thankful and grateful.— Yours truly, (Mrs.) 6—. TO THE DEAF. Canning-street, Liverpool. Dear Sir,—l have pleasure in testifying that ten days of the Akou-Vibra treatment has much improved my hearing. With my best ear (the left) I can hear six inches further than when I began it. My friends notice the improvement and it is a great comfort to me not to have to ask people con- stantly to repeat, and to hear what is going en at committee meetings, Ste., much better.—Yours faithfully, (Miss) W. J. L. TO THE DEAF. Heswall, Cheshire. Dear Sir,—The Akou-Vihra has assuredly done my wife a large amount of good. She now hears ordinary conversation, as well as the minister. Previous to seeing you she could enjoy neither of these benetits.—Yours faithfully, J. G. -- TO THE DEAF. Aintree. Dear Sir,—l have great pleasure in writing to tell you I am getting on splendidly after six visits. I can hear a great deal better. My deafness has been for six years in the right ear and one and a half years in the left. I am more than delighted to think I can hear so much better.—Wishing you every success, yours truly, J. W. D. -- TO THE DEAF. Rock Ferry. Dear Sir,—l could not yesterday express my gratitude to you for my better helring and your kindness to me during my visits. I shall recom- mend you to others.—Yours gratefully, HONOR SMITH. CALL FOR FREE TEST and examination. You may have a machine for 10 days' home trial and hire. HOURS : 10 to 12.30, 2.30 and 6.0. Saturday to 2 unless by appointment. 369 THE ARCADE, LORD STREET, LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE MINERS' OFFICIALS AND "CIVIL WAR." Speaking at a miners' meeting at St. Helens on Monday night Mr. H. Twist said he was satisfied they were going to have a national strike. They were face to face with the gravest crisis in the whole history of their movement. If they bad been present at the miners' conference last Saturday they would have been flabber- gasted at the multitude of provisions the employers sought to impose. He did not look forward with pleasure to a national strike, and he taw, civil war ahead unless the employers gave way somewhere. They talked about civil war in Belfast, but they would have it unless something was done quickly.</p>
